 Bergman algebrasThe graded universal algebra constructions // Restore the saved color scheme preference, or // enact the browser preference if "automatic", // without expecting DOM load to have completed. // // Also restore any saved readingmode and ToC display preferences. function initializeReadingPreferences() { let saved_theme = localStorage.getItem("ar5iv_theme") || "automatic"; if (saved_theme === "automatic") { if (window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches) { saved_theme = "dark"; } } if (saved_theme == "dark") { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "dark"); } else { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-theme", "light"); } const tocDisplay = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_toc_display'); if (tocDisplay) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-toc-display", tocDisplay); } const readingMode = localStorage.getItem('arxiv_html_paper_reading_mode'); if (readingMode) { document.documentElement.setAttribute("data-reading-mode", readingMode); } } // Run as soon as JS starts, to minimize repainting initializeReadingPreferences(); /* Reading mode hides the chrome and the empty .arxiv-html-header marker: the pinned JS reads the marker's display to know which way to toggle. The marker draws no box. */ .arxiv-html-header { display: contents; } html[data-reading-mode="enabled"] :is(.arxiv-html-site-chrome, .arxiv-html-header, .ds-site-footer) { display: none; } /* The paper controls' icons are blocks, so they sit centred (the pinned JS shows the theme icon by making its span a block). */ .arxiv-html-site-chrome .ds-site-header-tools svg { display: block; width: 18px; height: 18px; flex-shrink: 0; fill: currentColor; } /* ar5iv dims every image in dark mode and puts it on a white wash, for paper figures; arXiv's own render as they are (the logo on the bar, the funder logos on their white chips). Important: ar5iv's dark rules are unlayered, as specific as :root:not([data-theme]) img:not(.ltx_no_dark_filter). */ :is(.ds-site-header, .ds-site-footer) img { filter: none !important; } .ds-site-header img { background-color: transparent !important; } /* Wide (the theme's 1280px): the theme lays the body out as a grid whose full-width top rows were the band's and its sticky reader bar's. The band takes the first and scrolls away; the header, the paper's bar (no site navigation, the paper's controls), takes the second and stays in view, the one sticky bar, with the anchor clearance that requires (DESIGN-POLICIES). The controls show their names. */ @media (min-width: 1280px) { html { scroll-padding-top: 80px; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome { display: contents; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > #ds-announcement { grid-area: announce; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome > .ds-site-header { grid-area: header; position: sticky; top: 0; z-index: 3; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-icon { display: none; } } /* Narrow: the header scrolls away, and the controls show icons (names kept for screen readers; Why HTML? goes). The theme's contents panel is fixed below where its old sticky bar was, and hidden only once the reader has closed it, so until then it is an invisible box over the header and the paper: it shows only while open, under the chrome, in its place in the page. The paper's footer (build logs, reporting instructions) keeps the text's side inset; the theme gives it none, so it met the screen edges. */ @media (max-width: 1279px) { .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-wide { display: none; } .arxiv-html-site-chrome .tool-label { position: absolute; width: 1px; height: 1px; overflow: hidden; clip: rect(0 0 0 0); white-space: nowrap; } html:not([data-toc-display="block"]) .ltx_page_navbar { display: none; } .ltx_page_navbar { position: absolute; top: auto; } .arxiv-html-footer { padding-inline: 1rem; } } Skip to main content Report GitHub Issue × Title: Content selection saved. Describe the issue below: Description: Submit without GitHub Submit in GitHub Why HTML? Report Issue Back to Abstract Download PDF Contents Reading mode Colour theme Abstract. 1 Introduction 2 Γ \Gamma -monoids and graded algebras 2.1 Γ \Gamma -monoids 2.2 Graded algebras 2.3 Matrix form of graded homomorphisms 2.4 Non-stable Graded K K -theory 2.5 Graded hereditary rings 2.6 Smash products of graded algebras 2.6.1 Smash products 2.6.2 Smash products of quotients and extensions 2.6.3 An isomorphism of categories 3 Leavitt path algebras 3.1 Leavitt path algebras of hypergraphs 4 Bergman algebras 5 Graded Bergman universal construction 5.1 Graded universal morphisms 5.1.1 5.2 Universal morphisms for strongly graded rings 5.3 Matrix forms for three types of Bergman algebras 5.4 Universal morphisms and representable functors 5.5 Path algebras as universal algebras 6 Smash products of graded Bergman algebras 6.1 Smash products of graded Bergman algebras I 6.2 Smash products of graded Bergman algebras II 7 The graded 𝒱 \mathcal{V} -monoid of a graded Bergman algebra 7.1 Basic definitions and results 7.2 The graded 𝒱 \mathcal{V} -monoid of a graded Bergman algebra I 7.3 The graded 𝒱 \mathcal{V} -monoid of a graded Bergman algebra II 8 Realisation of Γ \Gamma -monoids as non-stable K K -theory of graded rings 9 Application to graph algebras References License: CC BY 4.0 arXiv:2403.01703v1 [math.RA] 04 Mar 2024 Bergman algebras The graded universal algebra constructions R. Hazrat Address: Roozbeh Hazrat: Centre for Research in Mathematics and Data Science Western Sydney University, Australia Email address: r.hazrat@westernsydney.edu.au , H. Li Address: Huanhuan Li: Center for Pure Mathematics School of Mathematical Sciences Anhui University, Hefei, China Email address: lhh@ahu.edu.cn and R. Preusser Address: Raimund Preusser: School of Mathematics and Statistics Nanjing University of Information Science and Technology, Nanjing, China Email address: raimund.preusser@gmx.de Date: August 24, 2026 Abstract. A half a century ago, George Bergman introduced stunning machinery which would realise any commutative conical monoid as the non-stable K K -theory of a ring. The ring constructed is “minimal” or “universal”. Given the success of graded K K -theory in classification of algebras and its connections to dynamics and operator algebras, the realisation of Γ \Gamma -monoids (monoids with an action of an abelian group Γ \Gamma on them) as non-stable graded K K -theory of graded rings becomes vital. In this paper, we revisit Bergman’s work and develop the graded version of this universal construction. For an abelian group Γ \Gamma , a Γ \Gamma -graded ring R R , and non-zero graded finitely generated projective (left) R R -modules P P and Q Q , we construct a universal Γ \Gamma -graded ring extension S S such that S ⊗ R P ≅ S ⊗ R Q S\otimes_{R}P\cong S\otimes_{R}Q as graded S S -modules. This makes it possible to bring the graded techniques, such as smash products and Zhang twists into Bergman’s machinery. Given a commutative conical Γ \Gamma -monoid M M , we construct a Γ \Gamma -graded ring S S such that 𝒱 gr ​ ( S ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(S) is Γ \Gamma -isomorphic to M M . In fact we show that any finitely generated Γ \Gamma -monoid can be realised as the non-stable graded K K -theory of a hyper Leavitt path algebra. Here 𝒱 gr ​ ( S ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(S) is the monoid of isomorphism classes of graded finitely generated projective S S -modules and the action of Γ \Gamma on 𝒱 gr ​ ( S ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(S) is by shift of degrees. Thus the group completion of M M can be realised as the graded Grothendieck group K 0 gr ​ ( S ) K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(S) . We use this machinery to provide a short proof to the fullness of the graded Grothendieck functor K 0 gr K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0} for the class of Leavitt path algebras (i.e., Graded Classification Conjecture II). Key words and phrases: Bergman algebra, Leavitt path algebra, graded ring, Γ \Gamma -monoid, graded Grothendieck group In memory of Nikolai Vavilov who conveyed the joy of mathematics to us in Chinese, German and Persian Contents 1 Introduction 2 Γ \Gamma -monoids and graded algebras 3 Leavitt path algebras 4 Bergman algebras 5 Graded Bergman universal construction 6 Smash products of graded Bergman algebras 7 The graded 𝒱 \mathcal{V} -monoid of a graded Bergman algebra 8 Realisation of Γ \Gamma -monoids as non-stable K K -theory of graded rings 9 Application to graph algebras References 1. Introduction In 1974 in the seminal paper [ 7 ] George Bergman introduced machinery whereby by adding sufficient generators and relations to an algebra, one can lift any pair of non-zero finitely generated projective modules to become isomorphic. To be precise, let R R be an algebra over a field 𝗄 \mathsf{k} , and let ( P i , Q i ) i ∈ I (P_{i},Q_{i})_{i\in I} be pairs of non-zero finitely generated projective left R R -modules. Bergman’s machinery adjoins certain generators and relations to R R , so that the resulted extension algebra S S obtained provides universal isomorphisms of modules, S ⊗ R P i ≅ S ⊗ R Q i S\otimes_{R}P_{i}\cong S\otimes_{R}Q_{i} , where i ∈ I i\in I . Furthermore, the non-stable K K -theory of the algebra S S can be obtained from that of R R by identifying the pairs ( P i , Q i ) i ∈ I (P_{i},Q_{i})_{i\in I} in the structure. Starting from a field 𝗄 \mathsf{k} and choosing the pair of 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -modules ( 𝗄 n , 𝗄 m ) (\mathsf{k}^{n},\mathsf{k}^{m}) , n , m ∈ ℕ + n,m\in\mathbb{N}^{+} , Bergman observed that his machinery retrieves the celebrated Leavitt algebra R = L 𝗄 ​ ( n , m ) R=L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,m) , where R n ≅ 𝗄 n ⊗ 𝗄 R ≅ 𝗄 m ⊗ 𝗄 R ≅ R m R^{n}\cong\mathsf{k}^{n}\otimes_{\mathsf{k}}R\cong\mathsf{k}^{m}\otimes_{\mathsf{k}}R\cong R^{m} as R R -modules. Many combinatorial algebras constructed in the last 50 years, such as Leavitt path algebras and their generalisations, can be obtained from Bergman’s machinery. Two natural questions immediately arise: (1) The structural properties : To describe the structure of the extension algebra S S obtained from R R relative to the pairs ( P i , Q i ) i ∈ I (P_{i},Q_{i})_{i\in I} . (2) The classification : Given pairs ( P i , Q i ) i ∈ I (P_{i},Q_{i})_{i\in I} of R R -modules and ( P i ′ , Q i ′ ) i ∈ I (P^{\prime}_{i},Q^{\prime}_{i})_{i\in I} of R ′ R^{\prime} -modules, for 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebras R R and R ′ R^{\prime} , respectively, to describe how the extension 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebras S S and S ′ S^{\prime} obtained from the Bergman machinery, respectively, compare. We highlighted Leavitt path algebras as substantial activity is currently taking place on both questions (1) and (2). In fact, there are several conjectures concering the classification problems and what could be the right invariant for Leavitt path algebras [ 1 ] . Leavitt path algebras associated to graphs have rich ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded structures (arising by assigning degree 1 1 to edges) which play a vital role in their studies. Their non-stable graded K K -theory is related to invariants of symbolic dynamics and the equivariant K K -theory of operator algebras and the current open conjecture is that the graded K K -theory could be a complete invariant for these algebras (see [ 13 , 15 ] and § 9 ). This is one of the main motivations of this project: to study the Bergman machinery in the graded setting. Let 𝗄 \mathsf{k} be a commutative ring and let R R be a unital 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra. Further let N N be an R R -module and P P a finitely generated projective R R -module. Let R ​ -rings 𝗄 R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}} be the category of 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebras T T equipped with a 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -homomorphism R → T R\rightarrow T . Bergman’s work started by showing that the following functor is representable: ℱ N , P : R ​ -rings 𝗄 ⟶ Sets T ⟼ Hom ⁡ ( T ⊗ R N , T ⊗ R P ) . \begin{split}\mathcal{F}_{N,P}:R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}&\longrightarrow{}\operatorname{Sets}\\ T&\longmapsto\operatorname{Hom}(T\otimes_{R}N,T\otimes_{R}P).\end{split} (1.1) That is, there exists a universal algebra S S (or the initial object R → S R\rightarrow S in an appropriate category), denoted by R ⟨ f : N ¯ → P ¯ ⟩ R\langle f:\overline{N}\rightarrow\overline{P}\rangle , which provides the S S -homomorphism S ⊗ R N → S ⊗ R P S\otimes_{R}N\rightarrow S\otimes_{R}P . Bergman further showed that there exists a universal algebra S S , denoted by R ​ ⟨ f ¯ = 0 ⟩ R\langle\overline{f}=0\rangle , such that the extension of a given R R -module homomorphim f : N → P f:N\rightarrow P vanishes. Combining these two constructions, allows for forming very interesting universal rings. For example, given a homomorphism f : P → Q f:P\rightarrow Q , one can construct a universal ring R ​ ⟨ f ¯ − 1 ⟩ R\langle\overline{f}^{-1}\rangle , where the extension of f f becomes invertible. The two important universal constructions needed to realise any conical monoid are the following: Let P P and Q Q be non-zero finitely generated projective left R R -modules. Constructing the universal homomorphisms h : P ¯ → Q ¯ h:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q} and h ′ : Q ¯ ¯ → P ¯ ¯ h^{\prime}:\overline{\overline{Q}}\rightarrow\overline{\overline{P}} in subsequent extensions and setting h ′ ​ h ¯ − 1 h^{\prime}\overline{h}-1 and h ¯ ​ h ′ − 1 \overline{h}h^{\prime}-1 to zero, we obtain the universal ring S := R ⟨ h , h − 1 : P ¯ ≅ Q ¯ ⟩ , S:=R\big\langle h,h^{-1}:\overline{P}\cong\overline{Q}\big\rangle, (1.2) where S ⊗ R P ≅ S ⊗ R Q S\otimes_{R}P\cong S\otimes_{R}Q as S S -modules. On the other hand, for P P a non-zero finitely generated projective left R R -module, by constructing the universal homomorphism e : P → P e:P\rightarrow P and setting the homomorphism e 2 − e e^{2}-e to zero we form a universal ring T := R ⟨ e : P ¯ → P ¯ ; e 2 = e ⟩ , T:=R\big\langle e:\overline{P}\to\overline{P};~e^{2}=e\big\rangle, (1.3) such that the extension of e e in T T is an idempotent endomorphism. Bergman then proved [ 7 , Theorems 5.1, 5.2] that 𝒱 ⁡ ( S ) ≅ 𝒱 ⁡ ( R ) / ⟨ [ P ] = [ Q ] ⟩ \mathcal{V}(S)\cong\mathcal{V}(R)/\langle[P]=[Q]\rangle and 𝒱 ⁡ ( T ) ≅ ⟨ 𝒱 ⁡ ( R ) , [ P 1 ] , [ P 2 ] ⟩ / ⟨ [ P ] = [ P 1 ] + [ P 2 ] ⟩ \mathcal{V}(T)\cong\langle\mathcal{V}(R),[P_{1}],[P_{2}]\rangle/\langle[P]=[P_{1}]+[P_{2}]\rangle , for the universal rings S S and T T of ( 1.2 ) and ( 1.3 ), respectively. Here 𝒱 ⁡ ( S ) \mathcal{V}(S) is the monoid of isomorphism classes of finitely generated projective left S S -modules. These constructions allowed Bergman to build up a machinery to realise any (finitely generated) conical commutative monoid as the non-stable K K -theory of a ring. Graded algebras frequently appear when there is a group acting on an algebraic structure. A graded ring can be a model of an algebraic structure that captures and reflects the time evolution and the dynamics. A ring R R is graded by a group Γ \Gamma when, roughly, R R can be partitioned by Γ \Gamma in a way that is compatible with the structure of R R . (See § 2.2 for more details.) Consequently, three categories play a prominent role in this setting: the category of left R R -modules R R - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 \operatorname{{\bf Mod}} , the category of graded left R R -modules R R - 𝐆𝐫 \operatorname{{\bf Gr}} , and the category of left R 0 R_{0} -modules R 0 R_{0} - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 \operatorname{{\bf Mod}} , where R 0 R_{0} is the subring of R R consisting of the elements in the partition corresponding to the identity element 0 0 of Γ \Gamma . A substantial portion of the theory of graded rings concerns the relationships between these categories. While the applications of this theory are numerous, one prominent example is the fundamental theorem of K K -theory, proved by Quillen [ 22 ] , using the category of graded modules in a crucial way. Our starting point is to consider a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra R R over a Γ \Gamma -graded commutative ring 𝗄 \mathsf{k} , a Γ \Gamma -graded (left) R R -module N N and a Γ \Gamma -graded finitely generated projective (left) R R -module P P , where Γ \Gamma is an abelian group, and consider the corresponding functors in the graded setting: ℱ N , P gr : R ​ -gr-rings 𝗄 ⟶ Sets T ⟼ Hom T ​ - ​ 𝐆𝐫 ⁡ ( T ⊗ R N , T ⊗ R P ) \begin{split}\mathcal{F}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{N,P}:R\text{-gr-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}&\longrightarrow{}\operatorname{Sets}\\ T&\longmapsto\operatorname{Hom}_{T\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}}(T\otimes_{R}N,T\otimes_{R}P)\end{split} (1.4) ℱ N 0 , P 0 : R 0 ​ -rings 𝗄 0 ⟶ Sets T ⟼ Hom ⁡ ( T ⊗ R 0 N 0 , T ⊗ R 0 P 0 ) \begin{split}\mathcal{F}_{N_{0},P_{0}}:R_{0}\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}_{0}}&\longrightarrow{}\operatorname{Sets}\\ T&\longmapsto\operatorname{Hom}(T\otimes_{R_{0}}N_{0},T\otimes_{R_{0}}P_{0})\end{split} (1.5) Here R ​ -gr-rings 𝗄 R\text{-gr-rings}_{\mathsf{k}} is the category of Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebras T T equipped with a graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -homomorphism R → T R\rightarrow T . In this paper we show that ℱ N , P gr \mathcal{F}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{N,P} is representable, i.e., there is a Γ \Gamma -graded algebra S S such that Hom ⁡ ( S , − ) ≅ ℱ N , P gr \operatorname{Hom}(S,-)\cong\mathcal{F}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{N,P} . Furthermore, if R R is strongly graded, then S ≅ R ∗ R 0 S 0 S\cong R\ast_{R_{0}}S_{0} , where S 0 S_{0} is the representation for ℱ N 0 , P 0 \mathcal{F}_{N_{0},P_{0}} and ∗ \ast is the coproduct. Forgetting the grading on S S gives us the representation for ℱ N , P \mathcal{F}_{N,P} of ( 1.1 ). Having these graded universal constructions, we can realise any conical commutative Γ \Gamma -monoid as the nonstable graded K K -theory of a suitable graded ring. Namely, for such a Γ \Gamma -monoid M M , there is a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra R R such that M ≅ 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) M\cong\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R) , as Γ \Gamma -monoids. Here 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R) is the monoid of isomorphism classes of graded finitely generated projective R R -modules and the action of Γ \Gamma on 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R) is by shift of degrees (Theorem 8.1 ). In order to carry out our Γ \Gamma -monoid realisations, we had two options; either to develop the entire theory of monoids for co-products rings ( [ 8 ] ) in the graded setting, or to start with a graded ring, and use the fact that the category of graded modules is equivalent to the category of modules over its corresponding smash product (i.e., to pass from the graded setting to the non-graded case and use the available techniques). We opted for the second option; we pass from the graded structure via smash product to the category of modules and use the established results in the non-graded setting. The price we had to pay for such a shortcut, was to drop some generality, such as working with graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebras, where 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a field concentrated in degree zero, rather than with 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebras, where 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a graded field. Our realisations of Γ \Gamma -monoids allow us to provide a short proof to one of the Graded Classification Conjectures (See Conjecture 9.1 (1)): Let A A be a ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra where 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is concentrated in degree zero and 𝒱 gr ​ ( A ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A) is cancellative. Then any order preserving ℤ ⁡ [ x , x − 1 ] \mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}] -module homomorphism ϕ : K 0 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ) → K 0 gr ​ ( A ) \phi:K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A) with ϕ ⁡ ( [ L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ] ) = [ A ] \phi([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)])=[A] is induced by a unital ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -homomorphism ψ : L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) → A \psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow A , i.e., K 0 gr ​ ( ψ ) = ϕ K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi . Here L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(E) is the Leavitt path algebra associated to the graph E E . Replacing the algebra A A with a Leavitt path algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( F ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(F) provides a positive answer to the fullness conjecture. In general given two finitely generated projective modules, it is not straightforward to describe Bergman’s (localisation) algebras explicitly. Given finitely generated R R -modules P P and Q Q , and a map f : P → Q f:P\rightarrow Q , there are few instances where the localisation rings R ⟨ f : P ¯ → Q ¯ ⟩ R\langle f:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q}\rangle , or R ​ ⟨ f ¯ − 1 ⟩ R\langle\overline{f}^{-1}\rangle are explicitly described. We refer to the paper of Sheiham [ 26 ] where the localisation of triangular matrix rings has been described. In this paper, by representing the finitely generated projective modules by the corresponding idempotent matrices, we first describe the Bergman’s constructions in terms of generators and relations. Thus given idempotent matrices e ∈ 𝕄 m ​ ( R ) e\in\mathbb{M}_{m}(R) and f ∈ 𝕄 n ​ ( R ) f\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R) , we define the Bergman algebra B R ​ ( e , f ) B_{R}(e,f) via generators and relations such that e e and f f become equivalent idempotents in B R ​ ( e , f ) B_{R}(e,f) in a universal way. The construction can be extended to families of pairs of idempotents ( e , f ) := { ( e i , f i ) } i ∈ I (e,f):=\{(e_{i},f_{i})\}_{i\in I} . In fact this construction will be carried out for graded rings and homogeneous idempotents in Section 4 . The algebra B R ​ ( e , f ) B_{R}(e,f) obtained this way, coincides with the universal localisation ring R ⟨ f , f − 1 : P ¯ i ≅ Q ¯ i ∣ i ∈ I ⟩ R\langle f,f^{-1}:\overline{P}_{i}\cong\overline{Q}_{i}\mid i\in I\rangle , for pairs of finitely generated projective modules ( P i , Q i ) , i ∈ I (P_{i},Q_{i}),i\in I which represent ( e i , f i ) , i ∈ I (e_{i},f_{i}),{i\in I} . Returning to the graded setting, we show that when R R is a Γ \Gamma -graded commutative 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -semisimple ring which is concentrated in degree zero, then the universal localisation ring R ⟨ f , f − 1 : P ¯ i ≅ Q ¯ i ∣ i ∈ I ⟩ R\langle f,f^{-1}:\overline{P}_{i}\cong\overline{Q}_{i}\mid i\in I\rangle , for any family of pairs of finitely generated projective modules ( P i , Q i ) , i ∈ I (P_{i},Q_{i}),i\in I , is graded isomorphic to a Leavitt path algebra of a hypergraph and conversely any such hyper Leavitt path algebra can be realised as a graded Bergman’s construction over a Γ \Gamma -graded commutative 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -semisimple ring (Theorem 4.10 ). Built on this, we show that any finitely generated conical Γ \Gamma -monoid can be realised as the non-stable graded K K -theory of a hyper Leavitt path algebra (Theorem 8.2 ). As mentioned, starting with a field 𝗄 \mathsf{k} , and projective modules 𝗄 \mathsf{k} and 𝗄 n \mathsf{k}^{n} , Bergman’s machinery would give the Leavitt algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( 1 , n ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,n) . This ring can be graded by assigning 1 1 and − 1 -1 to the suitable generators. However, we can start with a field 𝗄 \mathsf{k} concentrated in degree zero, and consider the ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded projective modules 𝗄 \mathsf{k} and 𝗄 ​ ( − 1 ) n \mathsf{k}(-1)^{n} , where 𝗄 ⁡ ( − 1 ) \mathsf{k}(-1) is the projective module 𝗄 \mathsf{k} shifted by − 1 -1 . Applying our graded Bergman construction, we obtain the algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( 1 , n ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,n) which is naturally ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded from the outset. This allows us to build the graded invariants such as non-stable graded K K -theory from the machinery we develop. The paper is organised as follows: Section 2 provides a background needed for the concepts of Γ \Gamma -monoids and graded algebras, as well as the notion of smash products. In Section 3 , we recall the notion of Leavitt path algebras of hypergraphs, which generalises several extensions of Leavitt path algebras. This is the generalisation which covers all Bergman algebras constructed from commutative semisimple rings as a base ring. In Section 4 , we introduce Bergman algebras, via generators and relations, both the non-graded version and the graded version. Section 5 which is the core part of this paper, carries out Bergman’s localisation construction [ 7 ] in the setting of graded rings. Section 6 investigates how the graded Bergman localisation behaves under the smash product. This is needed in Section 7 to compute the non-stable graded K K -theory of graded Bergman algebras. Section 8 develops many consequences of the graded Bergman machinery: we realise any conical Γ \Gamma -monoid as the non-stable graded K K -theory of an appropriate graded algebra. In fact we show that any finitely generated Γ \Gamma -monoid can be obtained as the non-stable graded K K -theory of a hyper Leavitt path algebra. Using our result, we provide a short positive answer to one of the Graded Classification Conjectures (Conjecture 9.1 (1)) for Leavitt path algebras in Section 9 . In this paper, all rings are unital and all modules are considered as left modules unless otherwise stated. ℕ \mathbb{N} denotes the natural numbers with 0 0 and ℕ + = ℕ \ { 0 } \mathbb{N}^{+}=\mathbb{N}\backslash\{0\} . 2. Γ \Gamma -monoids and graded algebras 2.1. Γ \Gamma -monoids Recall that a monoid is a semigroup with an identity element. Throughout this paper monoids are commutative, written additively, with the identity element denoted by 0 0 . A monoid homomorphism ϕ : M → N \phi:M\rightarrow N is a map between monoids M M and N N which respects the structures and satisfies ϕ ⁡ ( 0 ) = 0 \phi(0)=0 . Every monoid M M is equipped with a natural preordering: n ≤ m n\leq m if n + p = m n+p=m for some p ∈ M p\in M . We write n ∝ m ​ if ​ n ≤ k ​ m , for some ​ k ∈ ℕ + , n\propto m\text{ if }n\leq km,\text{ for some }k\in\mathbb{N}^{+}, and n ≍ m ​ if ​ n ∝ m ​ and ​ m ∝ n . n\asymp m\text{ if }n\propto m\text{ and }m\propto n. Thus n ≍ m n\asymp m if there is a k ∈ ℕ + k\in\mathbb{N}^{+} such that m ≤ k ​ n m\leq kn and n ≤ k ​ m n\leq km . We say i ∈ M i\in M is an order unit if m ∝ i m\propto i for any m ∈ M m\in M . We say M M is conical if m + n = 0 m+n=0 implies that m = n = 0 m=n=0 , where m , n ∈ M m,n\in M . We say M M is cancellative if m 1 + n = m 2 + n m_{1}+n=m_{2}+n implies m 1 = m 2 m_{1}=m_{2} , for all m 1 , m 2 , n ∈ M m_{1},m_{2},n\in M . Given a group Γ \Gamma , a Γ \Gamma -monoid consists of a monoid M M equipped with an action of Γ \Gamma on M M (by monoid automorphisms). We denote the action of α ∈ Γ \alpha\in\Gamma on m ∈ M m\in M by m α {}^{\alpha}m . Throughout this paper Γ \Gamma will be an abelian group and M M a commutative monoid. A monoid homomorphism ϕ : M → N \phi\colon M\rightarrow N between two Γ \Gamma -monoids is called Γ \Gamma - monoid homomorphism if ϕ \phi respects the actions of Γ \Gamma , i.e., ϕ ⁡ ( m α ) = ϕ α ​ ( m ) \phi({}^{\alpha}m)={}^{\alpha}\phi(m) for all m ∈ M m\in M . The natural preordering is respected by the action of Γ \Gamma . An order unit of a Γ \Gamma -monoid M M is an element i ∈ M i\in M such that for any m ∈ M m\in M , there are γ 1 , … , γ t ∈ Γ \gamma_{1},\dots,\gamma_{t}\in\Gamma such that m ≤ ∑ k = 1 t i γ k . m\leq\sum_{k=1}^{t}{}^{\gamma_{k}}i. (2.1) We denote the Γ \Gamma -monoid M M along with the order unit i i by ( M , i ) (M,i) and call it a pointed Γ \Gamma -monoid . A morphism f : ( M , i ) → ( N , j ) f:(M,i)\rightarrow(N,j) in the category of pointed Γ \Gamma -monoids, is a Γ \Gamma -monoid morphism f f such that f ⁡ ( i ) = j f(i)=j . The order unit i i is called a strong order unit if for any m ∈ M m\in M , we can choose all γ k = 0 \gamma_{k}=0 in ( 2.1 ). We say i i is a invariant order unit if i γ = i {}^{\gamma}i=i for any γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma . A congruence on a Γ \Gamma -monoid M M is an equivalence relation ∼ \sim on M M such that m ∼ m ′ m\sim m^{\prime} and n ∼ n ′ n\sim n^{\prime} implies m + n ∼ m ′ + n ′ m+n\sim m^{\prime}+n^{\prime} , and m ∼ m ′ m\sim m^{\prime} implies m γ ∼ m ′ γ {}^{\gamma}m\sim{}^{\gamma}m^{\prime} , for any γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma and m , m ′ , n , n ′ ∈ M m,m^{\prime},n,n^{\prime}\in M . If ∼ \sim is a congruence on M M , then M / ∼ M/\sim is a Γ \Gamma -monoid in the obvious way. We call M / ∼ M/\sim a quotient of M M . If M M is a Γ \Gamma -monoid and ( m i , n i ) (m_{i},n_{i}) , where i ∈ I i\in I , a family of pairs of elements of M M , then the congruence on the Γ \Gamma -monoid M M generated by the relations m i = n i m_{i}=n_{i} will be the congruence generated on M M as a monoid by the relations m i γ = n i γ {}^{\gamma}m_{i}={}^{\gamma}n_{i} , where i ∈ I i\in I and γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma . The action of the abelian group Γ \Gamma respects the relations ∝ \propto and ≍ \asymp and one can see that ≍ \asymp is an equivalence relation. Lemma 2.1 . Let M M be a Γ \Gamma -monoid and i ∈ M i\in M a Γ \Gamma -order unit. Then the following are equivalent. (1) The element i i is a strong order unit; (2) For any γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma we have i ≍ i γ i\asymp{}^{\gamma}i ; (3) The equivalence class [ i ] [i] under ≍ \asymp , which is clearly a sub-semigroup of M M , is in fact a Γ \Gamma -subsemigroup of M M . (4) If M M has a strong order-unit, then all such elements form an equivalence class under ≍ \asymp . Proof. The proofs are straightforward. ∎ Clearly the image of a homomorphism f : M → N f:M\to N , denoted by im ⁡ f \operatorname{im}f , is a Γ \Gamma -submonoid of N N . The kernel ker ⁡ f \ker f of f f is the congruence ∼ \sim on M M defined by m ∼ m ′ m\sim m^{\prime} if f ⁡ ( m ) = f ⁡ ( m ′ ) f(m)=f(m^{\prime}) , so that M / ker ⁡ f M/\ker f is a Γ \Gamma -monoid and the canonical homomorphism M / ker ⁡ f → N M/\ker f\rightarrow N is injective (see Lemma 2.3 ). Let X X be a set. The free Γ \Gamma -monoid on X X , denoted by F ​ ⟨ X ⟩ F\langle X\rangle , is constructed as the free monoid on the set X × Γ X\times\Gamma , with the action of δ ∈ Γ \delta\in\Gamma on ( x , γ ) (x,\gamma) defined by ( x , γ + δ ) (x,\gamma+\delta) . To be precise, F ⟨ X ⟩ = { ∑ x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ n x , γ ( x , γ ) ∣ n x , γ ≥ 0 , for any x ∈ X and γ ∈ Γ ; almost all n x , γ are zero } F\langle X\rangle=\Big\{\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n_{x,\gamma}(x,\gamma)\mid n_{x,\gamma}\geq 0\text{, for any }x\in X\text{ and }\gamma\in\Gamma;\text{ almost all }n_{x,\gamma}\text{ are zero}\Big\} which becomes a Γ \Gamma -monoid with addition ∑ x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ n x , γ ​ ( x , γ ) + ∑ x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ n x , γ ′ ​ ( x , γ ) = ∑ x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ ( n x , γ + n x , γ ′ ) ​ ( x , γ ) \sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n_{x,\gamma}(x,\gamma)+\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n^{\prime}_{x,\gamma}(x,\gamma)=\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}(n_{x,\gamma}+n^{\prime}_{x,\gamma})(x,\gamma) and Γ \Gamma -action ( ∑ x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ n x , γ ( x , γ ) ) δ = ∑ x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ n x , γ x ( γ + δ ) . {}^{\delta}\Big(\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n_{x,\gamma}(x,\gamma)\Big)=\sum_{x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma}n_{x,\gamma}x(\gamma+\delta). One checks easily that F ​ ⟨ X ⟩ F\langle X\rangle has the following universal property. Lemma 2.2 . Let X X be a set, M M a Γ \Gamma -monoid and f : X → M f:X\to M a map. Let g : X → F ​ ⟨ X ⟩ g:X\to F\langle X\rangle be the map defined by x → x ⁡ ( 0 ) x\to x(0) . Then there is a unique homomorphism h : F ​ ⟨ X ⟩ → M h:F\langle X\rangle\to M of Γ \Gamma -monoids such that f = h ​ g f=hg . Lemma 2.3 (Homomorphism theorem) . Let f : M → N f:M\to N be a homomorphism of Γ \Gamma -monoids. Then M / ker ⁡ f ≅ im ⁡ f M/\ker f\cong\operatorname{im}f . Proof. Define a map f ¯ : M / ker ⁡ f → im ⁡ f \bar{f}:M/\ker f\to\operatorname{im}f by f ¯ ​ ( [ m ] ) = f ​ ( m ) \bar{f}([m])=f(m) for any m ∈ M m\in M . Clearly f ¯ \bar{f} is well-defined and bijective. One checks easily that f f is a homomorphism of Γ \Gamma -monoids. ∎ Proposition 2.4 . Any Γ \Gamma -monoid is isomorphic to a quotient of a free Γ \Gamma -monoid. Proof. Let M M be a Γ \Gamma -monoid. It follows from Lemma 2.2 that there is a surjective homomorphism γ : F ​ ⟨ X ⟩ → M \gamma:F\langle X\rangle\to M of Γ \Gamma -monoids. Thus F ⁡ ⟨ X ⟩ / ker ⁡ γ ≅ im ⁡ γ = M F\langle X\rangle/\ker\gamma\cong\operatorname{im}\gamma=M by Lemma 2.3 . ∎ Let X X be a set and R R a set of relations m i = n i m_{i}=n_{i} , where m i , n i ∈ F ⁡ ⟨ X ⟩ m_{i},n_{i}\in F\langle X\rangle , for any i ∈ I i\in I . We write M = ⟨ X ∣ R ⟩ M=\langle X\mid R\rangle if M ≅ F ⟨ X ⟩ / ∼ M\cong F\langle X\rangle/\sim , where ∼ \sim is the congruence on F ​ ⟨ X ⟩ F\langle X\rangle generated by R R . In this case we say that M M is a Γ \Gamma -monoid presented by the generating set X X and the relations R R . 2.2. Graded algebras In this section we collect basic facts that we need about graded rings and graded algebras. We refer the reader to [ 17 , 21 ] for the theory of graded rings. Let Γ \Gamma be an abelian group with identity denoted by 0 0 . A ring R R (possibly without unit) is called a Γ \Gamma -graded ring if R = ⨁ γ ∈ Γ R γ R=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}R_{\gamma} such that each R γ R_{\gamma} is an additive subgroup of R R and R γ ​ R δ ⊆ R γ + δ R_{\gamma}R_{\delta}\subseteq R_{\gamma+\delta} for all γ , δ ∈ Γ \gamma,\delta\in\Gamma . The group R γ R_{\gamma} is called the γ \gamma - homogeneous component of R . R. When it is clear from context that a ring R R is graded by the group Γ , \Gamma, we simply say that R R is a graded ring . We denote the set of all homogeneous elements of the graded ring R R , by R h R^{h} . A unital Γ \Gamma -graded ring R = ⨁ γ ∈ Γ R γ R=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}R_{\gamma} is strongly graded if R α ​ R β = R α + β R_{\alpha}R_{\beta}=R_{\alpha+\beta} for any α , β ∈ Γ \alpha,\beta\in\Gamma and it is crossed-product if each component R α R_{\alpha} , α ∈ Γ \alpha\in\Gamma contains an invertible element. Furthermore, we say R R is a graded field if it is commutative and all non-zero homogeneous elements are invertible. The notion of strongly graded, in particular, is an important case as it widely generalises the case of group rings, and it has been gradually established that many results on group rings can be extended to strongly graded rings. Let 𝗄 \mathsf{k} be a Γ \Gamma -graded commutative ring and R R a unital Γ \Gamma -graded ring. We say R R is a graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra if it is equipped with a graded ring homomorphism ϕ : 𝗄 → Z ⁡ ( R ) \phi:\mathsf{k}\rightarrow Z(R) , where Z ⁡ ( R ) Z(R) is the centre of R R . Note that since Γ \Gamma is abelian, Z ⁡ ( R ) Z(R) is a graded ring. Let S S be a graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra. Then we say S S is a Γ \Gamma -graded R ​ -ring 𝗄 R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}} if it is equipped with a graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra homomorphism R → S R\rightarrow S . We denote the category of Γ \Gamma -graded R ​ -rings 𝗄 R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}} by R ​ -gr-rings 𝗄 R\text{-gr-rings}_{\mathsf{k}} . Considering Γ \Gamma to be trivial we obtain the category of R ​ -rings 𝗄 R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}} . For a graded A A -module M M , we define the α \alpha - shifted graded A A -module M ⁡ ( α ) M(\alpha) as M ⁡ ( α ) = ⨁ γ ∈ Γ M ​ ( α ) γ , M(\alpha)=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}M(\alpha)_{\gamma}, where M ​ ( α ) γ = M α + γ M(\alpha)_{\gamma}=M_{\alpha+\gamma} . That is, as an ungraded module, M ⁡ ( α ) M(\alpha) is a copy of M M , but the grading is shifted by α \alpha . For a Γ \Gamma -graded ring R R , the category of graded R R -modules is denoted by R R - 𝐆𝐫 \operatorname{{\bf Gr}} and the category of R R -modules by R R - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 \operatorname{{\bf Mod}} . For α ∈ Γ \alpha\in\Gamma , the shift functor 𝒯 α : R ​ - ​ 𝐆𝐫 ⟶ R ​ - ​ 𝐆𝐫 , M ⟼ M ⁡ ( α ) \mathcal{T}_{\alpha}:R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}\longrightarrow R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}},\quad M\longmapsto M(\alpha) is an isomorphism with the property 𝒯 α ​ 𝒯 β = 𝒯 α + β \mathcal{T}_{\alpha}\mathcal{T}_{\beta}=\mathcal{T}_{\alpha+\beta} for α , β ∈ Γ \alpha,\beta\in\Gamma . Let R R be a strongly Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra. For any R 0 R_{0} -module N N and any γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma , we identify the R 0 R_{0} -module R γ ⊗ R 0 N R_{\gamma}\otimes_{R_{0}}N with its image in R ⊗ R 0 N R\otimes_{R_{0}}N . Since R = ⨁ γ ∈ Γ R γ R=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}R_{\gamma} and R γ R_{\gamma} are R 0 R_{0} -bimodules, R ⊗ R 0 N R\otimes_{R_{0}}N is a Γ \Gamma -graded R R -module, with R ⊗ R 0 N = ⨁ γ ∈ Γ R γ ⊗ R 0 N . R\otimes_{R_{0}}N=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}R_{\gamma}\otimes_{R_{0}}N. Recall that we have the restriction functor ( − ) 0 : R ​ - ​ 𝐆𝐫 ⟶ R 0 ​ - ​ 𝐌𝐨𝐝 (-)_{0}:R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}\longrightarrow R_{0}\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Mod}} (2.2) sending M M to M 0 M_{0} and ϕ \phi to ϕ | M 0 \phi_{|_{M_{0}}} with M M a graded R R -module and ϕ \phi a graded module homomorphism from M M , and the induction functor R ⊗ R 0 − : R 0 - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 ⟶ R ​ - ​ 𝐆𝐫 N ⟼ R ⊗ R 0 N ψ ⟼ R ⊗ ψ . \begin{split}R\otimes_{R_{0}}-:R_{0}\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}&\longrightarrow R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}\\ N&\longmapsto R\otimes_{R_{0}}N\\ \psi&\longmapsto R\otimes\psi.\end{split} (2.3) When R R is strongly graded, Dade’s Theorem [ 17 , Theorem 1.5.1] (see also [ 21 , Theorem 3.1.1] ), guarantees that the functors ( − ) 0 (-)_{0} and R ⊗ R 0 − R\otimes_{R_{0}}- form mutually inverse equivalences of categories. Note that in general ( − ) 0 : R ​ - ​ 𝐆𝐫 → R 0 ​ - ​ 𝐌𝐨𝐝 (-)_{0}:R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}\xrightarrow{}R_{0}\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Mod}} does not induce a functor ( − ) 0 : R ​ - ​ 𝐆𝐫 proj → R 0 ​ - ​ 𝐌𝐨𝐝 proj (-)_{0}:R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}_{\operatorname{proj}}\xrightarrow{}R_{0}\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}_{\operatorname{proj}} . But R R is strongly graded if and only if R ​ - ​ 𝐆𝐫 proj R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}_{\operatorname{proj}} is equivalent to R ​ - ​ 𝐌𝐨𝐝 proj R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}_{\operatorname{proj}} , if and only if R ​ - ​ 𝐠𝐫 R\text{-}{\bf gr} is equivalent R 0 ​ - ​ 𝐦𝐨𝐝 R_{0}\text{-}{\bf mod} , both under the functors ( 2.2 ) and ( 2.3 ). Here R ​ - ​ 𝐠𝐫 R\text{-}{\bf gr} denotes the category of graded finitely generated R R -modules and R 0 ​ - ​ 𝐦𝐨𝐝 R_{0}\text{-}{\bf mod} is the category of finitely generated R 0 R_{0} -modules. Let R R , S S and A A be Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebras and let i : A → R i:A\rightarrow R and j : A → S j:A\rightarrow S be 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -graded algebra monomorphisms (so we identify A A as a graded subring of R R and S S ). The coproduct R ∗ A S R*_{A}S can be given a natural Γ \Gamma -grading, so that this coproduct is indeed the pushout for the graded maps i i and j j . 2.3. Matrix form of graded homomorphisms Let Γ \Gamma be an abelian group, 𝗄 \mathsf{k} a Γ \Gamma -graded commutative ring with identity and R R a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra. A graded R R -module is called a graded free module, if it is a free module with homogeneous basis. Clearly a graded free module is a free module. A graded free R R -module can be presented as ⨁ i ∈ I R ⁡ ( γ i ) \bigoplus_{i\in I}R(\gamma_{i}) , where γ i ∈ Γ \gamma_{i}\in\Gamma . A graded R R -module P P is called a graded projective module if P P is a projective R R -module. One can check that P P is graded projective if and only if the functor Hom R ​ - ​ 𝐆𝐫 ⁡ ( P , − ) \operatorname{Hom}_{R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}}(P,-) is an exact functor in the category R R - 𝐆𝐫 \operatorname{{\bf Gr}} if and only if P P is graded isomorphic to a direct summand of a graded free R R -module ( [ 17 , Proposition 1.2.15] ). So a module is graded projective module if and only if it is graded and projective. This is not the case for graded free module. A module could be graded and free, but not graded free (see [ 17 , § 1.2.4] ). In particular, if P P is a graded finitely generated projective R R -module, then there is a graded finitely generated projective R R -module Q Q such that P ​ ⨁ Q ≅ ⨁ i = 1 n gr ⁡ R ⁡ ( α i ) , P\bigoplus Q\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}\bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}R(\alpha_{i}), (2.4) with α i ∈ Γ \alpha_{i}\in\Gamma . We denote ⨁ i = 1 n R ⁡ ( α i ) \bigoplus_{i=1}^{n}R(\alpha_{i}) by R n ​ ( α ¯ ) R^{n}(\overline{\alpha}) with α ¯ = ( α 1 , ⋯ , α n ) \overline{\alpha}=(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{n}) . One observes that Hom R ( R ( δ j ) , R ( δ i ) ) ≅ gr R ( δ i − δ j ) {\operatorname{Hom}}_{R}(R(\delta_{j}),R(\delta_{i}))\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}R(\delta_{i}-\delta_{j}) as graded R R -modules. Hence we have the following isomorphisms: Hom R ⁡ ( ⨁ i ∈ I R ⁡ ( α i ) , ⨁ j ∈ J R ⁡ ( β j ) ) ≅ ∏ i ∈ I Hom R ⁡ ( R ⁡ ( α i ) , ⨁ j ∈ J R ⁡ ( β j ) ) ≅ ∏ i ∈ I ⨁ j ∈ J Hom R ⁡ ( R ⁡ ( α i ) , R ⁡ ( β j ) ) ≅ ∏ i ∈ I ⨁ j ∈ J R ⁡ ( β j − α i ) . \begin{split}\operatorname{Hom}_{R}(\bigoplus_{i\in I}R(\alpha_{i}),\bigoplus_{j\in J}R(\beta_{j}))&\cong\prod_{i\in I}\operatorname{Hom}_{R}(R(\alpha_{i}),\bigoplus_{j\in J}R(\beta_{j}))\\ &\cong\prod_{i\in I}\bigoplus_{j\in J}\operatorname{Hom}_{R}(R(\alpha_{i}),R(\beta_{j}))\\ &\cong\prod_{i\in I}\bigoplus_{j\in J}R(\beta_{j}-\alpha_{i}).\end{split} (2.5) For a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra R R , α ¯ = ( α i ) i ∈ I \overline{\alpha}=(\alpha_{i})_{i\in I} , β ¯ = ( β j ) j ∈ J \overline{\beta}=(\beta_{j})_{j\in J} with α i , β j ∈ Γ \alpha_{i},\beta_{j}\in\Gamma , set 𝕄 I × J ​ ( R ) ​ [ α ¯ ] ​ [ β ¯ ] := ( R β j − α i ) i ∈ I , j ∈ J , \mathbb{M}_{I\times J}(R)[\overline{\alpha}][\overline{\beta}]:={\begin{pmatrix}R_{\beta_{j}-\alpha_{i}}\end{pmatrix}}_{i\in I,j\in J}, (2.6) where for each i ∈ I i\in I , only a finite number of entries in ( R β j − α i ) (R_{\beta_{j}-\alpha_{i}}) are non-zero. Thus 𝕄 I × J ​ ( R ) ​ [ α ¯ ] ​ [ β ¯ ] \mathbb{M}_{I\times J}(R)[\overline{\alpha}][\overline{\beta}] consists of matrices with the i ​ j ij -entry in R β j − α i R_{\beta_{j}-\alpha_{i}} , with a finite number of non-zero elements in each row. One observes that 𝕄 I × J ​ ( R ) ​ [ α ¯ ] ​ [ β ¯ ] \mathbb{M}_{I\times J}(R)[\overline{\alpha}][\overline{\beta}] represents the set Hom R ​ - ​ 𝐆𝐫 ⁡ ( ⨁ i ∈ I R ⁡ ( α i ) , ⨁ j ∈ J R ⁡ ( β j ) ) \operatorname{Hom}_{R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}}(\bigoplus_{i\in I}R(\alpha_{i}),\bigoplus_{j\in J}R(\beta_{j})) as follows: Suppose that u : ⨁ i ∈ I R ⁡ ( α i ) → ⨁ j ∈ J R ⁡ ( β j ) u:\bigoplus_{i\in I}R(\alpha_{i})\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{j\in J}R(\beta_{j}) is a graded R R -module homomorphism. Then for each i ∈ I i\in I , we can write u ⁡ ( 1 i ) = ( u i ​ 