on P P . Clearly, e e is an idempotent and graded homomorphism of degree zero. Thus e ∈ End R ⁡ ( R n ​ ( α ¯ ) ) 0 ≅ 𝕄 n ​ ( R ) ​ ( α ¯ ) 0 e\in\operatorname{End}_{R}(R^{n}(\overline{\alpha}))_{0}\cong\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)(\overline{\alpha})_{0} . For (2), let e ∈ 𝕄 n ​ ( R ) ​ ( α ¯ ) 0 e\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)(\overline{\alpha})_{0} be a homogeneous idempotent matrix of degree zero, where α ¯ = ( α 1 , ⋯ , α n ) \overline{\alpha}=(\alpha_{1},\cdots,\alpha_{n}) with α i ∈ Γ \alpha_{i}\in\Gamma . Then 1 − e ∈ 𝕄 n ​ ( R ) ​ ( α ¯ ) 0 1-e\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)(\overline{\alpha})_{0} and R n ​ ( α ¯ ) = R n ​ e ​ ( α ¯ ) ⊕ R n ​ ( 1 − e ) ​ ( α ¯ ) . R^{n}(\overline{\alpha})=R^{n}e(\overline{\alpha})\oplus R^{n}(1-e)(\overline{\alpha}). This shows that R n ​ e ​ ( α ¯ ) R^{n}e(\overline{\alpha}) is a graded finitely generated projective R R -module. ∎ In the first part of the paper we assume 𝗄 \mathsf{k} to be a commutative graded ring. In the second part, for the realisation of conical Γ \Gamma -monoids, we assume 𝗄 \mathsf{k} to be a (graded) field concentrated in degree zero. 2.4. Non-stable Graded K K -theory For a Γ \Gamma -graded ring R R with identity and a graded finitely generated projective (left) R R -module P P , let [ P ] [P] denote the class of graded R R -modules graded isomorphic to P P . Then the set 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) = { [ P ] ∣ P ​ is a graded finitely generated projective R-module } \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)=\big\{\,[P]\mid P\text{ is a graded finitely generated projective R-module}\,\big\} (2.11) with direct sum as addition constitutes a Γ \Gamma -monoid structure defined as follows: [ P ] + [ Q ] = [ P ⊕ Q ] [P]+[Q]=[P\oplus Q] and for γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma and [ P ] ∈ 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) [P]\in\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R) , [ P ] γ = [ P ( γ ) ] {}^{\gamma}[P]=[P(\gamma)] . The group completion of 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R) is called the graded Grothendieck group and is denoted by K 0 gr ​ ( R ) K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(R) , which as the above discussion shows is a ℤ ⁡ [ Γ ] \mathbb{Z}[\Gamma] -module. Following the notion of order unit for a Γ \Gamma -monoid in § 2.1 , an element [ P ] ∈ 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) [P]\in\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R) is an order unit for 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R) if for any [ Q ] ∈ 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) [Q]\in\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R) we have [ Q ] ≤ ∑ 1 ≤ i ≤ n [ P ⁡ ( γ i ) ] [Q]\leq\sum_{1\leq i\leq n}[P(\gamma_{i})] for some n ∈ ℕ n\in\mathbb{N} and γ i ∈ Γ \gamma_{i}\in\Gamma . Since for any graded finitely generated projective module Q Q , there is a graded module P P such that Q ⊕ P ≅ R ⁡ ( γ 1 ) ⊕ R ⁡ ( γ 2 ) ⊕ ⋯ ⊕ R ⁡ ( γ t ) Q\oplus P\cong R(\gamma_{1})\oplus R(\gamma_{2})\oplus\cdots\oplus R(\gamma_{t}) , it follows that that [ R ] [R] is an order unit (see [ 17 , §3] ). The following easy proposition shows that the order unit [ R ] [R] in 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R) can determine when R R is a strongly graded or a crossed product ring (see also [ 21 , Theorems 2.10.1 and 2.10.2] ). This will be used in Theorem 8.1 . Proposition 2.6 . Let R R be a Γ \Gamma -graded ring with identity and 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R) the monoid of graded finitely generated projective modules. Then (1) The ring R R is strongly graded if and only if [ R ] [R] is an strong order unit. (2) The ring R R is crossed-product if and only if [ R ] [R] is an invariant order unit. Proof. (1) The ring R R is strongly graded if and only if, for any α ∈ Γ \alpha\in\Gamma , 1 = ∑ i = 1 n α r i ​ s i 1=\sum_{i=1}^{n_{\alpha}}r_{i}s_{i} , where r i ∈ R α r_{i}\in R_{\alpha} and s i ∈ R − α s_{i}\in R_{-\alpha} . This is equivalent to having matrices 𝐫 = ( r 1 , … , r n α ) ∈ 𝕄 1 × n α ​ ( R ) ​ [ α ¯ ] ​ [ 0 ] {\mathbf{r}}=(r_{1},\dots,r_{n_{\alpha}})\in\mathbb{M}_{1\times n_{\alpha}}(R)[\overline{\alpha}][0] and 𝐬 = ( s 1 , … , s n α ) t ∈ 𝕄 n α × 1 ​ ( R ) ​ [ 0 ] ​ [ α ¯ ] {\mathbf{s}}=(s_{1},\dots,s_{n_{\alpha}})^{t}\in\mathbb{M}_{n_{\alpha}\times 1}(R)[0][\overline{\alpha}] with 𝐫𝐬 = 1 {\mathbf{r}}{\mathbf{s}}=1 . In turn this is equivalent to having an epimorphism ⨁ n α R → R ⁡ ( α ) → 0 \bigoplus_{n_{\alpha}}R\rightarrow R(\alpha)\rightarrow 0 , which is equivalent to [ R ⁡ ( α ) ] ≤ n α ​ [ R ] [R(\alpha)]\leq n_{\alpha}[R] in 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R) . This is now equivalent to [ R ] [R] being a strong order unit. (2) This follows from the observation that there is a graded R R -module isomorphism R ≅ R ⁡ ( α ) R\cong R(\alpha) if and only if there is an invertible element in R α R_{\alpha} . ∎ 2.5. Graded hereditary rings Recall that a unital ring R R is called a left hereditary ring if any left ideal of R R is a projective R R -module [ 28 , Chapter 4] . Analogously, we say a Γ \Gamma -graded ring R R is called a left graded hereditary ring if any graded left ideal of R R is a graded projective module. The following proposition is the graded version of well-known equivalent properties of hereditary rings whose proof follows mutatis mutandis, with an attention given to the grading. Proposition 2.7 . Let R R be a Γ \Gamma -graded ring. The following statements are equivalent. (1) R R is left graded hereditary; (2) Any graded submodule of a graded projective left R R -module is graded projective; (3) Any graded submodule of a graded projective left R R -module is projective; (4) The graded left global dimension is less than two; (5) Ext R ​ - ​ 𝐆𝐫 2 ​ ( − , − ) {\rm Ext}_{R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}}^{2}(-,-) vanishes. Proposition 2.7 (3) implies that a Γ \Gamma -graded ring which is hereditary is indeed graded hereditary. However, the following example shows that a graded hereditary ring is not necessarily hereditary. This is in contrast with the fact that graded regular rings and regular rings which are graded (i.e., their (graded) global dimension is finite) coincide. Consider a strongly Γ \Gamma -graded ring R R . By Dade’s theorem (see Section 2.2 ), the categories R R - 𝐆𝐫 \operatorname{{\bf Gr}} and R 0 R_{0} - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 \operatorname{{\bf Mod}} are equivalent. An application of Proposition 2.7 shows that if R 0 R_{0} is hereditary, then R R is a graded hereditary ring. The ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded ring R = ℤ ⁡ [ x , x − 1 ] R=\mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}] with deg ⁡ ( x ) = 1 \deg(x)=1 and deg ⁡ ( x − 1 ) = − 1 \deg(x^{-1})=-1 is a strongly graded ring with the zero-component the ring ℤ \mathbb{Z} which is hereditary (PID rings are hereditary). Thus R R is a graded hereditary ring. However, R R is not hereditary: for example, the (non-graded) ideal ⟨ 2 , x − 1 ⟩ \langle 2,x-1\rangle is not a projective module. Note that the graded ring 𝗄 ⁡ [ x , x − 1 ] \mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}] , where 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a field, is indeed a PID ring and thus a hereditary ring. We will see the graded rings coming out of the graded Bergman constructions are indeed hereditary and thus they are graded hereditary as well. 2.6. Smash products of graded algebras 2.6.1. Smash products Let R R be a Γ \Gamma -graded ring. The smash product ring R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma is defined as the set of all formal sums ∑ γ ∈ Γ r ( γ ) ​ p γ \sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma} , where r ( γ ) ∈ R r^{(\gamma)}\in R for any γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma , the p γ p_{\gamma} ’s are symbols, and all but finitely many coefficients r ( γ ) r^{(\gamma)} are zero. Addition is defined component-wise and multiplication is defined by linear extension of the rule ( r ​ p α ) ​ ( s ​ p β ) = r ​ s α − β ​ p β (rp_{\alpha})(sp_{\beta})=rs_{\alpha-\beta}p_{\beta} , where r , s ∈ R r,s\in R and α , β ∈ Γ \alpha,\beta\in\Gamma . Instead of 1 ​ p γ 1p_{\gamma} we may write p γ p_{\gamma} . If R R is a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra where 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a commutative graded ring concentrated in degree 0 0 , then R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma is a (not-necessarily unital) 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra with scalar multiplication λ ​ ∑ γ ∈ Γ r ( γ ) ​ p γ = ∑ γ ∈ Γ λ ​ r ( γ ) ​ p γ \lambda\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}=\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}\lambda r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma} , for any λ ∈ 𝗄 \lambda\in\mathsf{k} and ∑ γ ∈ Γ r ( γ ) ​ p γ ∈ R ​ # ​ Γ \sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}\in R\#\Gamma . Lemma 2.8 . Let R R be a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra where 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a commutative graded ring concentrated in degree 0 0 . Then the 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma has the presentation R ​ # ​ Γ = ⟨ r ​ p γ ​ ( r ∈ R h , γ ∈ Γ ) ∣ \displaystyle R\#\Gamma=\Big\langle rp_{\gamma}~(r\in R^{h},\gamma\in\Gamma)\mid{} r ​ p γ + s ​ p γ = ( r + s ) ​ p γ ( r , s ∈ R δ , γ , δ ∈ Γ ) , \displaystyle rp_{\gamma}+sp_{\gamma}=(r+s)p_{\gamma}\quad(r,s\in R_{\delta};~\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma), r p γ s p δ = r s γ − δ p δ ( r , s ∈ R h ; γ , δ ∈ Γ ) ⟩ . \displaystyle~rp_{\gamma}sp_{\delta}=rs_{\gamma-\delta}p_{\delta}\quad(r,s\in R^{h};~\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma)\Big\rangle. Proof. Denote by F F the free 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra generated by the set { r ​ p γ ​ ( r ∈ R h , γ ∈ Γ ) } \{rp_{\gamma}~(r\in R^{h},\gamma\in\Gamma)\} , and by I I the two-sided ideal of F F generated by the set { r p γ + s p γ − ( r + s ) p γ ∣ r , s ∈ R δ ; γ , δ ∈ Γ } ⋃ { r p γ s p δ − r s γ − δ p δ ∣ r , s ∈ R h ; γ , δ ∈ Γ } . \big\{rp_{\gamma}+sp_{\gamma}-(r+s)p_{\gamma}\mid r,s\in R_{\delta};~\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma\big\}\bigcup\big\{rp_{\gamma}sp_{\delta}-rs_{\gamma-\delta}p_{\delta}\mid r,s\in R^{h};~\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma\big\}. Clearly the 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra homomorphism θ : F → R ​ # ​ Γ \theta:F\to R\#\Gamma defined by θ ⁡ ( r ​ p γ ) = r ​ p γ ​ ( r ∈ R h , γ ∈ Γ ) \theta(rp_{\gamma})=rp_{\gamma}~(r\in R^{h},\gamma\in\Gamma) induces a 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra homomorphism θ ¯ : F / I → R ​ # ​ Γ \bar{\theta}:F/I\to R\#\Gamma (since I ⊆ ker ⁡ ( θ ) I\subseteq\ker(\theta) ). Since the elements r ​ p γ ​ ( r ∈ R h , γ ∈ Γ ) rp_{\gamma}~(r\in R^{h},\gamma\in\Gamma) generate the 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma , the homomorphism θ ¯ \bar{\theta} is surjective. It remains to show that θ ¯ \bar{\theta} is injective. Suppose that θ ¯ ​ ( x ) = 0 \bar{\theta}(x)=0 , where x ∈ F / I x\in F/I is non-zero. Clearly we can write x = r 1 ​ p γ 1 + ⋯ + r n ​ p γ n x=r_{1}p_{\gamma_{1}}+\dots+r_{n}p_{\gamma_{n}} , where r 1 , … , r n ∈ R h ∖ { 0 } r_{1},\dots,r_{n}\in R^{h}\setminus\{0\} and γ 1 , … , γ n ∈ Γ \gamma_{1},\dots,\gamma_{n}\in\Gamma . For γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma set J ⁡ ( γ ) := { 1 ≤ i ≤ n ∣ γ i = γ } J(\gamma):=\{1\leq i\leq n\mid\gamma_{i}=\gamma\} . We may assume that deg ⁡ ( r i ) ≠ deg ⁡ ( r j ) , for any ​ γ ∈ Γ ​ and ​ i ≠ j ∈ J ⁡ ( γ ) . \deg(r_{i})\neq\deg(r_{j}),\text{ for any }\gamma\in\Gamma\text{ and }i\neq j\in J(\gamma). (2.12) It follows from 0 = θ ¯ ​ ( x ) = r 1 ​ p γ 1 + ⋯ + r n ​ p γ n = ∑ γ ∈ Γ ( ∑ i ∈ J ⁡ ( γ ) r i ) ​ p γ 0=\bar{\theta}(x)=r_{1}p_{\gamma_{1}}+\dots+r_{n}p_{\gamma_{n}}=\sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma}\Big(\sum_{i\in J(\gamma)}r_{i}\Big)p_{\gamma} that ∑ i ∈ J ⁡ ( γ ) r i = 0 \sum_{i\in J(\gamma)}r_{i}=0 , for any γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma . In view of ( 2.12 ) this implies r i = 0 r_{i}=0 , for any 1 ≤ i ≤ n 1\leq i\leq n , a contradiction. Thus we have shown that θ ¯ \bar{\theta} is injective. ∎ 2.6.2. Smash products of quotients and extensions We continue with the assumption that R R is a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra where 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a commutative graded ring concentrated in degree 0 0 . If I I is a graded ideal of R R , then we denote by I ​ # ​ Γ I\#\Gamma the ideal of R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma consisting of all elements ∑ γ ∈ Γ r ( γ ) ​ p γ \sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma} such that r ( γ ) ∈ I r^{(\gamma)}\in I , for any γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma . Note that the quotient ring R / I R/I is Γ \Gamma -graded ring. Proposition 2.9 . Let R R be a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra where 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a commutative graded ring concentrated in degree 0 0 , and I I a graded ideal of R R . Then ( R / I ) ​ # ​ Γ ≅ R ​ # ​ Γ / I ​ # ​ Γ . (R/I)\#\Gamma\cong R\#\Gamma/I\#\Gamma. Proof. We define the map f : ( R / I ) ​ # ​ Γ \displaystyle f:(R/I)\#\Gamma ⟶ R ​ # ​ Γ / I ​ # ​ Γ \displaystyle\longrightarrow R\#\Gamma/I\#\Gamma ∑ γ ∈ Γ ( r ( γ ) + I ) ​ p γ \displaystyle\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}(r^{(\gamma)}+I)p_{\gamma} ⟼ ∑ γ ∈ Γ r ( γ ) ​ p γ + I ​ # ​ Γ . \displaystyle\longmapsto\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}+I\#\Gamma. Suppose that ∑ γ ∈ Γ ( r ( γ ) + I ) ​ p γ = ∑ γ ∈ Γ ( s ( γ ) + I ) ​ p γ \sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}(r^{(\gamma)}+I)p_{\gamma}=\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}(s^{(\gamma)}+I)p_{\gamma} in ( R / I ) ​ # ​ Γ (R/I)\#\Gamma . Then ∑ γ ∈ Γ ( r ( γ ) − s ( γ ) + I ) ​ p γ = 0 \sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}(r^{(\gamma)}-s^{(\gamma)}+I)p_{\gamma}=0 and hence r ( γ ) − s ( γ ) ∈ I r^{(\gamma)}-s^{(\gamma)}\in I , for any γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma . It follows that ∑ γ ∈ Γ ( r ( γ ) − s ( γ ) ) ​ p γ ∈ I ​ # ​ Γ \sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}(r^{(\gamma)}-s^{(\gamma)})p_{\gamma}\in I\#\Gamma and hence ∑ γ ∈ Γ r ( γ ) ​ p γ + I ​ # ​ Γ = ∑ γ ∈ Γ s ( γ ) ​ p γ + I ​ # ​ Γ \sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}+I\#\Gamma=\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}s^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}+I\#\Gamma . Thus f f is well-defined. Clearly f f is a surjective 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra homomorphism. It remains to show f f is injective. Suppose that f ⁡ ( ∑ γ ∈ Γ ( r ( γ ) + I ) ​ p γ ) = 0 f(\sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}(r^{(\gamma)}+I)p_{\gamma})=0 , i.e., ∑ γ ∈ Γ r ( γ ) ​ p γ + I ​ # ​ Γ = 0 \sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}+I\#\Gamma=0 . Then ∑ γ ∈ Γ r ( γ ) ​ p γ ∈ I ​ # ​ Γ \sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}r^{(\gamma)}p_{\gamma}\in I\#\Gamma and hence r ( γ ) ∈ I r^{(\gamma)}\in I for any γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma . It follows ∑ γ ∈ Γ ( r ( γ ) + I ) ​ p γ = 0 \sum\limits_{\gamma\in\Gamma}(r^{(\gamma)}+I)p_{\gamma}=0 and thus f f is injective. ∎ If X X is a set, we denote by R ​ ⟨ X ⟩ R\langle X\rangle the 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra obtained from R R by adjoining the set X X (i.e., R ​ ⟨ X ⟩ R\langle X\rangle is the free product of R R and the free 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra k ​ ⟨ X ⟩ k\langle X\rangle generated by X X ). If in addition we are given a map deg : X → Γ \deg:X\to\Gamma , then R ​ ⟨ X ⟩ R\langle X\rangle becomes a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra with the induced grading. We denote by X ​ # ​ Γ X\#\Gamma the set { x p γ ∣ x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ } \{xp_{\gamma}\mid x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma\} . Proposition 2.10 . Let R R be a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra where 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a commutative graded ring concentrated in degree 0 0 , and X X a set and deg : X → Γ \deg:X\to\Gamma a map. Consider R ​ ⟨ X ⟩ R\langle X\rangle as a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra with the induced grading. Then R ⁡ ⟨ X ⟩ ​ # ​ Γ ≅ R ​ # ​ Γ ​ ⟨ X ​ # ​ Γ ⟩ / J , R\langle X\rangle\#\Gamma\cong R\#\Gamma\langle X\#\Gamma\rangle/J, where J J is the ideal of R ​ # ​ Γ ​ ⟨ X ​ # ​ Γ ⟩ R\#\Gamma\langle X\#\Gamma\rangle generated by the set { ( x p γ ) ( 1 p γ ) − x p γ , ( 1 p γ + deg ⁡ ( x ) ) ( x p γ ) − x p γ ∣ x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ } . \big\{(xp_{\gamma})(1p_{\gamma})-xp_{\gamma},~(1p_{\gamma+\deg(x)})(xp_{\gamma})-xp_{\gamma}\mid x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma\big\}. Proof. It follows from Lemma 2.8 that R ​ # ​ Γ ​ ⟨ X ​ # ​ Γ ⟩ / J R\#\Gamma\langle X\#\Gamma\rangle/J has the presentation R # Γ ⟨ X # Γ ⟩ / J = ⟨ r p γ , x p γ ( r ∈ R h , x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ ) ∣ r ​ p γ + s ​ p γ = ( r + s ) ​ p γ ( r , s ∈ R δ , γ , δ ∈ Γ ) , r ​ p γ ​ s ​ p δ = r ​ s γ − δ ​ p δ ( r , s ∈ R h , γ , δ ∈ Γ ) , ( x ​ p γ ) ​ ( 1 ​ p γ ) = x ​ p γ ( x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ ) , ( 1 p γ + deg ⁡ ( x ) ) ( x p γ ) = x p γ ( x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ ) ⟩ . \begin{split}R\#\Gamma\langle X\#\Gamma\rangle/J=\Big\langle rp_{\gamma},xp_{\gamma}~(r\in R^{h},x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma)\mid{}&rp_{\gamma}+sp_{\gamma}=(r+s)p_{\gamma}\quad(r,s\in R_{\delta};~\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma),\\ &~rp_{\gamma}sp_{\delta}=rs_{\gamma-\delta}p_{\delta}\quad(r,s\in R^{h};~\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma),\\ &~(xp_{\gamma})(1p_{\gamma})=xp_{\gamma}\quad(x\in X,~\gamma\in\Gamma),\\ &~(1p_{\gamma+\deg(x)})(xp_{\gamma})=xp_{\gamma}\quad(x\in X,~\gamma\in\Gamma)\Big\rangle.\end{split} (2.13) Since the relations in presentation ( 2.13 ) above are satisfied in R ⁡ ⟨ X ⟩ ​ # ​ Γ R\langle X\rangle\#\Gamma , there is a k k -algebra homomorphism f : R ​ # ​ Γ ​ ⟨ X ​ # ​ Γ ⟩ / J → R ⁡ ⟨ X ⟩ ​ # ​ Γ , f:R\#\Gamma\langle X\#\Gamma\rangle/J\rightarrow R\langle X\rangle\#\Gamma, such that f ⁡ ( r ​ p γ ) = r ​ p γ f(rp_{\gamma})=rp_{\gamma} and f ⁡ ( x ​ p γ ) = x ​ p γ f(xp_{\gamma})=xp_{\gamma} , for any r ∈ R h , x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ r\in R^{h},x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma . Clearly the elements r ​ p γ , x ​ p γ rp_{\gamma},xp_{\gamma} , where r ∈ R h , x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ r\in R^{h},x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma generate the k k -algebra R ⁡ ( X ) ​ # ​ Γ R(X)\#\Gamma and hence f f is surjective. It remains to show that f f is injective. Let w w be a non-empty and finite word over the alphabet { r ​ p γ , x ​ p γ ​ ( r ∈ R h , x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ ) } \{rp_{\gamma},xp_{\gamma}~(r\in R^{h},x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma)\} . Then w = y 1 ​ p γ 1 ​ … ​ y n ​ p γ n w=y_{1}p_{\gamma_{1}}\dots y_{n}p_{\gamma_{n}} for some y 1 , … , y n ∈ R ∪ X y_{1},\dots,y_{n}\in R\cup X and γ 1 , … , γ n ∈ Γ \gamma_{1},\dots,\gamma_{n}\in\Gamma . We call w w a path (ending in γ n \gamma_{n} ) if γ i − 1 − γ i = deg ⁡ ( y i ) ​ ( i = 2 , … , n ) \gamma_{i-1}-\gamma_{i}=\deg(y_{i})~(i=2,\dots,n) . We denote the set of of all paths by W W , and for any γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma the set of all paths ending in γ \gamma by W γ W_{\gamma} . Define a map ζ : W → R ​ ⟨ X ⟩ \zeta:W\to R\langle X\rangle by ζ ⁡ ( y 1 ​ p γ 1 ​ … ​ y n ​ p γ n ) = y 1 ​ … ​ y n \zeta(y_{1}p_{\gamma_{1}}\dots y_{n}p_{\gamma_{n}})=y_{1}\dots y_{n} . Now suppose that f ⁡ ( a ) = 0 f(a)=0 for some a ∈ R ​ # ​ Γ ​ ⟨ X ​ # ​ Γ ⟩ / J a\in R\#\Gamma\langle X\#\Gamma\rangle/J . It follows from the presentation ( 2.13 ) that a = ∑ w ∈ W λ w ​ w a=\sum_{w\in W}\lambda_{w}w where λ w ∈ k ⁡ ( w ∈ W ) \lambda_{w}\in k~(w\in W) . Hence 0 = f ⁡ ( a ) = f ⁡ ( ∑ w ∈ W λ w ​ w ) = f ⁡ ( ∑ γ ∈ Γ ∑ w ∈ W γ λ w ​ w ) = ∑ γ ∈ Γ ∑ w ∈ W γ λ w ​ ζ ​ ( w ) ​ p γ . 0=f(a)=f\Big(\sum_{w\in W}\lambda_{w}w\Big)=f\Big(\sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma}\sum_{w\in W_{\gamma}}\lambda_{w}w\Big)=\sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma}\sum_{w\in W_{\gamma}}\lambda_{w}\zeta(w)p_{\gamma}. It follows that ∑ w ∈ W γ λ w ​ ζ ​ ( w ) = 0 \sum_{w\in W_{\gamma}}\lambda_{w}\zeta(w)=0 in R ⁡ ( X ) R(X) for any γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma . One checks easily that this implies that ∑ w ∈ W γ λ w ​ w = 0 \sum_{w\in W_{\gamma}}\lambda_{w}w=0 in R ​ # ​ Γ ​ ⟨ X ​ # ​ Γ ⟩ / J R\#\Gamma\langle X\#\Gamma\rangle/J , for any γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma . Hence a = 0 a=0 and thus f f is injective. ∎ Corollary 2.11 . Let R R be a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra where 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a commutative graded ring concentrated in degree 0 0 , X X a set and deg : X → Γ \deg:X\to\Gamma a map. Consider R ​ ⟨ X ⟩ R\langle X\rangle as a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra with the induced grading. Let I I be a graded ideal of R ​ ⟨ X ⟩ R\langle X\rangle . Then ( R ⁡ ⟨ X ⟩ / I ) ​ # ​ Γ ≅ R ​ # ​ Γ ​ ⟨ X ​ # ​ Γ ⟩ / J , (R\langle X\rangle/I)\#\Gamma\cong R\#\Gamma\langle X\#\Gamma\rangle/J, where J J is the ideal of R ​ # ​ Γ ​ ⟨ X ​ # ​ Γ ⟩ R\#\Gamma\langle X\#\Gamma\rangle generated by the image of I ​ # ​ Γ I\#\Gamma and the set { ( x p γ ) ( 1 p γ ) − x p γ , ( 1 p γ + deg ⁡ ( x ) ) ( x p γ ) − x p γ ∣ x ∈ X , γ ∈ Γ } . \big\{(xp_{\gamma})(1p_{\gamma})-xp_{\gamma},~(1p_{\gamma+\deg(x)})(xp_{\gamma})-xp_{\gamma}\mid x\in X,\gamma\in\Gamma\big\}. (2.14) Proof. It follows from Proposition 2.9 that ( R ⁡ ⟨ X ⟩ / I ) ​ # ​ Γ ≅ R ⁡ ⟨ X ⟩ ​ # ​ Γ / I ​ # ​ Γ (R\langle X\rangle/I)\#\Gamma\cong R\langle X\rangle\#\Gamma/I\#\Gamma . By Proposition 2.10 , there is an isomorphism g : R ⁡ ⟨ X ⟩ ​ # ​ Γ → R ​ # ​ Γ ​ ⟨ X ​ # ​ Γ ⟩ / J ′ g:R\langle X\rangle\#\Gamma\to R\#\Gamma\langle X\#\Gamma\rangle/J^{\prime} , where J ′ J^{\prime} is the ideal of R ​ # ​ Γ ​ ⟨ X ​ # ​ Γ ⟩ R\#\Gamma\langle X\#\Gamma\rangle generated by ( 2.14 ). Hence ( R ⁡ ⟨ X ⟩ / I ) ​ # ​ Γ ≅ R ⁡ ⟨ X ⟩ ​ # ​ Γ / I ​ # ​ Γ ≅ ( R ​ # ​ Γ ​ ⟨ X ​ # ​ Γ ⟩ / J ′ ) / g ⁡ ( I ​ # ​ Γ ) . (R\langle X\rangle/I)\#\Gamma\cong R\langle X\rangle\#\Gamma/I\#\Gamma\cong(R\#\Gamma\langle X\#\Gamma\rangle/J^{\prime})/g(I\#\Gamma). The assertion of the corollary follows. ∎ 2.6.3. An isomorphism of categories Recall that R R - 𝐆𝐫 \operatorname{{\bf Gr}} denotes the category of Γ \Gamma -graded R R -modules and R R - 𝐆𝐫 proj \operatorname{{\bf Gr}}_{\operatorname{proj}} the full subcategory of R R - 𝐆𝐫 \operatorname{{\bf Gr}} whose objects are the projective objects of R R - 𝐆𝐫 \operatorname{{\bf Gr}} that are finitely generated as a R R -module. Moreover, R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 \operatorname{{\bf Mod}} denotes the category of unital R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma -modules and R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 proj \operatorname{{\bf Mod}}_{\operatorname{proj}} the full subcategory of R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 \operatorname{{\bf Mod}} whose objects are the projective objects of R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 \operatorname{{\bf Mod}} that are finitely generated as a R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma -module. In [ 3 ] it was shown that there is an isomorphism ψ : R \psi:R - 𝐆𝐫 → R ​ # ​ Γ \operatorname{{\bf Gr}}\to R\#\Gamma - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 \operatorname{{\bf Mod}} , and in [ 24 ] it was shown that ψ \psi restricts to an isomorphism R R - 𝐆𝐫 proj → R ​ # ​ Γ \operatorname{{\bf Gr}}_{\operatorname{proj}}\to R\#\Gamma - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 proj \operatorname{{\bf Mod}}_{\operatorname{proj}} . The isomorphism ψ \psi maps an object M M of R R - 𝐆𝐫 \operatorname{{\bf Gr}} to the object M ​ # ​ Γ M\#\Gamma of R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 \operatorname{{\bf Mod}} which is defined as follows. As abelian group M ​ # ​ Γ = M M\#\Gamma=M . The R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma -action on M ​ # ​ Γ M\#\Gamma is defined by ( r ​ p α ) ​ m = r ​ m α (rp_{\alpha})m=rm_{\alpha} for any r ∈ R r\in R , α ∈ Γ \alpha\in\Gamma and m ∈ M = M ​ # ​ Γ m\in M=M\#\Gamma . On morphisms, ψ \psi is just the identity map. 3. Leavitt path algebras Let 𝗄 \mathsf{k} be a field. Consider the symbols X = ( x i ​ j ) X=(x_{ij}) and X ∗ = ( x j ​ i ∗ ) X^{*}=(x^{*}_{ji}) , where 1 ≤ i ≤ n 1\leq i\leq n and 1 ≤ j ≤ n + k 1\leq j\leq n+k : X = ( x 11 x 12 … x 1 , n + k x 21 x 22 … x 2 , n + k ⋱ x n , 1 x n , 2 … x n , n + k ) , X ∗ = ( x 11 ∗ x 12 ∗ … x 1 , n ∗ x 21 ∗ x 22 ∗ … x 2 , n ∗ ⋱ x n + k , 1 ∗ x n + k , 2 ∗ … x n + k , n ∗ ) . X=\left(\begin{matrix}x_{11\phantom{n+{},}}&x_{12\phantom{n+{},}}&\dots&x_{1,n+k}\\ x_{21\phantom{n+{},}}&x_{22\phantom{n+{},}}&\dots&x_{2,n+k}\\ \vdots&\vdots&\ddots&\vdots\\ x_{n,1\phantom{n+{},}}&x_{n,2\phantom{n+{},}}&\dots&x_{n,n+k}\end{matrix}\right),\qquad X^{*}=\left(\begin{matrix}x^{*}_{11\phantom{n+{},}}&x^{*}_{12\phantom{n+{},}}&\dots&x^{*}_{1,n\phantom{n+{},}}\\ x^{*}_{21\phantom{n+{},}}&x^{*}_{22\phantom{n+{},}}&\dots&x^{*}_{2,n\phantom{n+{},}}\\ \vdots&\vdots&\ddots&\vdots\\ x^{*}_{n+k,1}&x^{*}_{n+k,2}&\dots&x^{*}_{n+k,n}\end{matrix}\right). (3.1) The Leavitt algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k) is defined as L 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) = 𝗄 ​ ⟨ X , X ∗ ⟩ ⟨ X ​ X ∗ = I n , X ∗ ​ X = I n + k ⟩ . L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)=\frac{\mathsf{k}\langle X,X^{*}\rangle}{\langle XX^{*}=I_{n},\,X^{*}X=I_{n+k}\rangle}. (3.2) Here 𝗄 ​ ⟨ X , X ∗ ⟩ \mathsf{k}\langle X,X^{*}\rangle is the free 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra generated by the symbols x i ​ j x_{ij} and x j ​ i ∗ x_{ji}^{*} ’s. Furthermore, the relations X ​ X ∗ = I n XX^{*}=I_{n} and X ∗ ​ X = I n + k X^{*}X=I_{n+k} , where I n ∈ 𝕄 n ​ ( 𝗄 ) I_{n}\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(\mathsf{k}) and I n + k ∈ 𝕄 n + k ​ ( 𝗄 ) I_{n+k}\in\mathbb{M}_{n+k}(\mathsf{k}) are the identity matrices, stand for the collection of relations after multiplying the matrices and comparing the two sides of the equations. These rings were first studied by Leavitt [ 19 ] and [ 20 , p.130, footnote 6] . Leavitt showed the type of the ring L 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k) is ( n , n + k ) (n,n+k) . Furthermore, L 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k) , for n > 1 n>1 and k ≥ 1 k\geq 1 , is a domain, whereas L 𝗄 ​ ( 1 , k + 1 ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1) , is a simple ring. If in ( 3.2 ) we only consider the relation X ∗ ​ X = I n + k X^{*}X=I_{n+k} , then we call the resulting algebra Cohn algebra C 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) = 𝗄 ​ ⟨ X , X ∗ ⟩ ⟨ X ∗ ​ X = I n + k ⟩ , C_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)=\frac{\mathsf{k}\langle X,X^{*}\rangle}{\langle X^{*}X=I_{n+k}\rangle}, (3.3) which was considered by Cohn [ 11 ] , who showed that C 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) C_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k) , for n > 1 n>1 and k ≥ 1 k\geq 1 , is a domain, whereas C 𝗄 ​ ( 1 , k + 1 ) C_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1) has only one non-trivial proper ideal generated by relation X ​ X ∗ = I n XX^{*}=I_{n} . This result also implies the simplicity of L 𝗄 ​ ( 1 , k + 1 ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1) . The simple rings L 𝗄 ​ ( 1 , k + 1 ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1) received significant attentions as they are the discrete versions of Cuntz algebras. Modeled on this, Leavitt path algebras are introduced [ 1 ] , which attach to a directed graph a certain algebra. In the case that the graph has one vertex and k + 1 k+1 loops, it recovers Leavitt algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( 1 , k + 1 ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,k+1) . In order to recover the Leavitt algebras L 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k) , for n > 1 n>1 , a plethora of generalisations of Leavitt path algebras were introduced, such as Leavitt path algebra of separated graphs, weighted graphs and ultra graphs. Here, following [ 24 ] , we introduce the Leavitt path algebras of hypergraphs. We show that Bergman algebras (see Section 4 ) with coefficients in a commutative semisimple ring are precisely Leavitt path algebras of hypergraphs (Theorem 4.10 ). 3.1. Leavitt path algebras of hypergraphs For sets I I and X X , a function x : I → X x:I\rightarrow X , i ↦ x i = x ⁡ ( i ) i\mapsto x_{i}=x(i) is called a family of elements in X X indexed by I I . A family x x of elements in X X indexed by I I is usually denoted as ( x i ) i ∈ I (x_{i})_{i\in I} . We call the family ( x i ) i ∈ I (x_{i})_{i\in I} non-empty if I ≠ ∅ I\neq\emptyset . A (directed) hypergraph is a quadruple H = ( H 0 , H 1 , s , r ) H=(H^{0},H^{1},s,r) , where H 0 H^{0} and H 1 H^{1} are sets and s s and r r are maps associating to each h ∈ H 1 h\in H^{1} a non-empty family s ⁡ ( h ) = ( s ​ ( h ) i ) i ∈ I h s(h)=(s(h)_{i})_{i\in I_{h}} , respectively, r ⁡ ( h ) = ( r ​ ( h ) j ) j ∈ J h r(h)=(r(h)_{j})_{j\in J_{h}} of elements in H 0 H^{0} . The elements of H 0 H^{0} are called vertices and the elements of H 1 H^{1} hyperedges . In this paper all hypergraphs are assumed to be 𝑟𝑒𝑔𝑢𝑙𝑎𝑟 {\it regular} , i.e. for any hyperedge h h the sets I h I_{h} and J h J_{h} are finite. Definition 3.1 . Let H H be a hypergraph. The not necessarily unital 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( H ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(H) presented by the generating set { v , h i ​ j , h i ​ j ∗ ∣ v ∈ H 0 , h ∈ H 1 , i ∈ I h , j ∈ J h } \{v,h_{ij},h_{ij}^{*}\mid v\in H^{0},h\in H^{1},i\in I_{h},j\in J_{h}\} and the relations (i) u ​ v = δ u ​ v ​ u ( u , v ∈ H 0 ) uv=\delta_{uv}u\quad(u,v\in H^{0}) , (ii) s ( h ) i h i ​ j = h i ​ j = h i ​ j r ( h ) j , r ( h ) j h i ​ j ∗ = h i ​ j ∗ = h i ​ j ∗ s ( h ) i ( h ∈ H 1 , i ∈ I h , j ∈ J h ) s(h)_{i}h_{ij}=h_{ij}=h_{ij}r(h)_{j},~r(h)_{j}h_{ij}^{*}=h_{ij}^{*}=h_{ij}^{*}s(h)_{i}\quad(h\in H^{1},i\in I_{h},j\in J_{h}) , (iii) ∑ j ∈ J h h i ​ j ​ h i ′ ​ j ∗ = δ i ​ i ′ ​ s ​ ( h ) i ( h ∈ H 1 , i , i ′ ∈ I h ) \sum\limits_{j\in J_{h}}h_{ij}h_{i^{\prime}j}^{*}=\delta_{ii^{\prime}}s(h)_{i}\quad(h\in H^{1},~i,i^{\prime}\in I_{h}) and (iv) ∑ i ∈ I h h i ​ j ∗ ​ h i ​ j ′ = δ j ​ j ′ ​ r ​ ( h ) j ( h ∈ H 1 , j , j ′ ∈ J h ) \sum\limits_{i\in I_{h}}h_{ij}^{*}h_{ij^{\prime}}=\delta_{jj^{\prime}}r(h)_{j}\quad(h\in H^{1},~j,j^{\prime}\in J_{h}) is called the Leavitt path algebra of the hypergraph H H . We will sometimes call a Leavitt path algebra of a hypergraph a hyper Leavitt path algebra . It is not difficult to check that if H H is a hypergraph, then L 𝗄 ​ ( H ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(H) has a set of local units, namely the set of all finite sums of distinct elements of H 0 H^{0} . Furthermore, L K ​ ( H ) L_{K}(H) is a unital ring if and only if H 0 H^{0} is finite ( [ 24 , Proposition 8] ). Remark 3.2 . Let H H be a hypergraph. We can describe the relations (ii)-(iv) in Definition 3.1 using matrices as follows. For any hyperedge h ∈ H 1 h\in H^{1} , let e h e_{h} be the I h × I h I_{h}\times I_{h} -matrix whose entry at position ( i , i ′ ) (i,i^{\prime}) is δ i ​ i ′ ​ s ​ ( h ) i \delta_{ii^{\prime}}s(h)_{i} , and f h f_{h} the J h × J h J_{h}\times J_{h} -matrix whose entry at position ( j , j ′ ) (j,j^{\prime}) is δ j ​ j ′ ​ r ​ ( h ) j \delta_{jj^{\prime}}r(h)_{j} . Moreover, we denote for any hyperedge h ∈ H 1 h\in H^{1} the I h × J h I_{h}\times J_{h} -matrix whose entry at position ( i , j ) (i,j) is h i ​ j h_{ij} also by h h , and the J h × I h J_{h}\times I_{h} -matrix whose entry at position ( j , i ) (j,i) is h i ​ j ∗ h^{*}_{ij} by h ∗ h^{*} . Then L 𝗄 ​ ( H ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(H) is the 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra presented by the generating set { v , h i ​ j , h i ​ j ∗ ∣ v ∈ H 0 , h ∈ H 1 , i ∈ I h , j ∈ J h } \{v,h_{ij},h_{ij}^{*}\mid v\in H^{0},h\in H^{1},i\in I_{h},j\in J_{h}\} and relations (I) the elements of H 0 H^{0} are pairwise orthogonal idempotents, (II) h = e h h f h , h ∗ = f h h ∗ e h ( h ∈ H 1 ) h=e_{h}hf_{h},\quad h^{*}=f_{h}h^{*}e_{h}\quad(h\in H^{1}) , (III) h ​ h ∗ = e h ( h ∈ H 1 ) hh^{*}=e_{h}\quad(h\in H^{1}) and (IV) h ∗ ​ h = f h ( h ∈ H 1 ) h^{*}h=f_{h}\quad(h\in H^{1}) . Remark 3.3 . Let H H be a hypergraph and Γ \Gamma an abelian group. For any h ∈ H 1 h\in H^{1} choose an i h ∈ I h i_{h}\in I_{h} and a j h ∈ J h j_{h}\in J_{h} . A map w w which associates to any generator h i ​ j ​ ( h ∈ H 1 , i ∈ I h , j ∈ J h ) h_{ij}~(h\in H^{1},i\in I_{h},j\in J_{h}) an element from Γ \Gamma , such that w ⁡ ( h i ​ j ) = w ⁡ ( h i ​ j h ) − w ⁡ ( h i h ​ j h ) + w ⁡ ( h i h ​ j ) w(h_{ij})=w(h_{ij_{h}})-w(h_{i_{h}j_{h}})+w(h_{i_{h}j}) for any h ∈ H 1 h\in H^{1} , i ∈ I h i\in I_{h} and j ∈ J h j\in J_{h} is called a Γ \Gamma -weight map for H H . By [ 24 , Lemma 70] , a Γ \Gamma -weight map w w for H H induces a Γ \Gamma -grading on L 𝗄 ​ ( H ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(H) such that deg ⁡ ( v ) = 0 \deg(v)=0 , deg ⁡ ( h i ​ j ) = w ⁡ ( h i ​ j ) \deg(h_{ij})=w(h_{ij}) and deg ⁡ ( h i ​ j ∗ ) = − w ⁡ ( h i ​ j ) \deg(h_{ij}^{*})=-w(h_{ij}) for any v ∈ H 0 v\in H^{0} , h ∈ H 1 h\in H^{1} , i ∈ I h i\in I_{h} and j ∈ J h j\in J_{h} . Example 3.4 (Leavitt path algebras) . Suppose that E E is a directed graph , i.e. a quadruple E = ( E 0 , E 1 , r , s ) E=(E^{0},E^{1},r,s) , where E 0 E^{0} and E 1 E^{1} are sets and r , s r,s are maps from E 1 E^{1} to E 0 E^{0} . The elements of E 0 E^{0} are called vertices and the elements of E 1 E^{1} edges . We may think of an edge e ∈ E 1 e\in E^{1} as an arrow from s ⁡ ( e ) s(e) to r ⁡ ( e ) r(e) . Assume that E E is row-finite , i.e. for each vertex v ∈ E 0 v\in E^{0} there are at most finitely many edges in s − 1 ​ ( v ) s^{-1}(v) . Denote by E reg E^{\operatorname{reg}} the set of regular vertices of the graph E E , i.e. the set of all vertices v v for which s − 1 ​ ( v ) s^{-1}(v) is not the empty set. The 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(E) presented by the generating set { v , e , e ∗ ∣ v ∈ E 0 , e ∈ E 1 } \{v,e,e^{*}\mid v\in E^{0},e\in E^{1}\} and the relations (i) u ​ v = δ u ​ v ​ u ( u , v ∈ E 0 ) uv=\delta_{uv}u\quad(u,v\in E^{0}) , (ii) s ( e ) e = e = e r ( e ) , r ( e ) e ∗ = e ∗ = e ∗ s ( e ) ( e ∈ E 1 ) s(e)e=e=er(e),~r(e)e^{*}=e^{*}=e^{*}s(e)\quad(e\in E^{1}) , (iii) ∑ e ∈ s − 1 ​ ( v ) e ​ e ∗ = v ( v ∈ E reg ) \sum_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}ee^{*}=v\quad(v\in E^{\operatorname{reg}}) and (iv) e ∗ ​ f = δ e ​ f ​ r ​ ( e ) ( e , f ∈ E 1 ) e^{*}f=\delta_{ef}r(e)\quad(e,f\in E^{1}) is called the Leavitt path algebra of E E (cf. for example [ 1 ] ). For each v ∈ E reg v\in E^{\operatorname{reg}} write s − 1 ​ ( v ) = { e v , j ∣ j ∈ J v } s^{-1}(v)=\{e^{v,j}\mid j\in J_{v}\} . Define a hypergraph H = ( H 0 , H 1 , s ′ , r ′ ) H=(H^{0},H^{1},s^{\prime},r^{\prime}) by H 0 = E 0 H^{0}=E^{0} , H 1 = { h v ∣ v ∈ E reg } H^{1}=\{h^{v}\mid v\in E^{\operatorname{reg}}\} , s ′ ​ ( h v ) = ( v ) i ∈ { 1 } s^{\prime}(h^{v})=(v)_{i\in\{1\}} and r ′ ​ ( h v ) = ( r ⁡ ( e v , j ) ) j ∈ J v r^{\prime}(h^{v})=(r(e^{v,j}))_{j\in J_{v}} . There is a 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra isomorphism ϕ : L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) → L 𝗄 ​ ( H ) \phi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(H) such that ϕ ⁡ ( u ) = u \phi(u)=u , ϕ ⁡ ( e v , j ) = h 1 ​ j v \phi(e^{v,j})=h^{v}_{1j} and ϕ ⁡ ( ( e v , j ) ∗ ) = ( h 1 ​ j v ) ∗ \phi((e^{v,j})^{*})=(h^{v}_{1j})^{*} for any u ∈ E 0 u\in E^{0} , v ∈ E reg v\in E^{\operatorname{reg}} and j ∈ J v j\in J_{v} . Example 3.5 (Vertex-weighted Leavitt path algebras) . Suppose that ( E , w ) (E,w) is a vertex-weighted graph, i.e. E E is a directed graph and w w is a map associating to each regular vertex a positive integer. We again assume that E E is row-finite. The 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( E , w ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(E,w) presented by the generating set { v , e i , e i ∗ ∣ v ∈ E 0 , e ∈ E 1 , 1 ≤ i ≤ w ( s ( e ) ) } \{v,e_{i},e_{i}^{*}\mid v\in E^{0},e\in E^{1},1\leq i\leq w(s(e))\} and the relations (i) u ​ v = δ u ​ v ​ u ( u , v ∈ E 0 ) uv=\delta_{uv}u\quad(u,v\in E^{0}) , (ii) s ( e ) e i = e i = e i r ( e ) , r ( e ) e i ∗ = e i ∗ = e i ∗ s ( e ) ( e ∈ E 1 , 1 ≤ i ≤ w ( s ( e ) ) ) s(e)e_{i}=e_{i}=e_{i}r(e),~r(e)e_{i}^{*}=e_{i}^{*}=e_{i}^{*}s(e)\quad(e\in E^{1},1\leq i\leq w(s(e))) , (iii) ∑ e ∈ s − 1 ​ ( v ) e i ​ e j ∗ = δ i ​ j ​ v ( v ∈ E reg , 1 ≤ i , j ≤ w ⁡ ( v ) ) \sum_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}e_{i}e_{j}^{*}=\delta_{ij}v\quad(v\in E^{\operatorname{reg}};~1\leq i,j\leq w(v)) and (iv) ∑ 1 ≤ i ≤ w ⁡ ( v ) e i ∗ ​ f i = δ e ​ f ​ r ​ ( e ) ( v ∈ E reg , e , f ∈ s − 1 ​ ( v ) ) \sum_{1\leq i\leq w(v)}e_{i}^{*}f_{i}=\delta_{ef}r(e)\quad(v\in E^{\operatorname{reg}};~e,f\in s^{-1}(v)) is called the Leavitt path algebra of ( E , w ) (E,w) . For each v ∈ E reg v\in E^{\operatorname{reg}} write s − 1 ​ ( v ) = { e v , j ∣ j ∈ J v } s^{-1}(v)=\{e^{v,j}\mid j\in J_{v}\} . Define a hypergraph H = ( H 0 , H 1 , s ′ , r ′ ) H=(H^{0},H^{1},s^{\prime},r^{\prime}) by H 0 = E 0 H^{0}=E^{0} , H 1 = { h v ∣ v ∈ E reg 0 } H^{1}=\{h^{v}\mid v\in E^{0}_{\operatorname{reg}}\} , s ′ ​ ( h v ) = ( v ) i ∈ { 1 , … , w ⁡ ( v ) } s^{\prime}(h^{v})=(v)_{i\in\{1,\dots,w(v)\}} and r ′ ​ ( h v ) = ( r ⁡ ( e v , j ) ) j ∈ J v r^{\prime}(h^{v})=(r(e^{v,j}))_{j\in J_{v}} . There is a 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra isomorphism ψ : L 𝗄 ​ ( E , w ) → L 𝗄 ​ ( H ) \psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E,w)\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(H) such that ψ ⁡ ( u ) = u \psi(u)=u , ψ ⁡ ( e i v , j ) = h i ​ j v \psi(e^{v,j}_{i})=h^{v}_{ij} and ψ ⁡ ( ( e i v , j ) ∗ ) = ( h i ​ j v ) ∗ \psi((e^{v,j}_{i})^{*})=(h^{v}_{ij})^{*} for any u ∈ E 0 u\in E^{0} , v ∈ E reg v\in E^{\operatorname{reg}} , i ∈ { 1 , … , w ⁡ ( v ) } i\in\{1,\dots,w(v)\} and j ∈ J v j\in J_{v} . 4. Bergman algebras Let R R be a ring and e ∈ 𝕄 m ​ ( R ) e\in\mathbb{M}_{m}(R) and f ∈ 𝕄 n ​ ( R ) f\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R) be idempotent matrices. Recall that e e and f f are called equivalent , denoted e ∼ f e\sim f , if there are matrices h ∈ 𝕄 m × n ​ ( R ) h\in\mathbb{M}_{m\times n}(R) and h ∗ ∈ M n × m ​ ( R ) h^{*}\in M_{n\times m}(R) such that e = h ​ h ∗ e=hh^{*} and f = h ∗ ​ h f=h^{*}h . Definition 4.1 . Let R R be a 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra, where 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a commutative ring. Let e ∈ 𝕄 m ​ ( R ) e\in\mathbb{M}_{m}(R) and f ∈ 𝕄 n ​ ( R ) f\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R) be idempotent matrices. Let h = ( h i ​ j ) h=(h_{ij}) and h ∗ = ( h j ​ i ∗ ) h^{*}=(h^{*}_{ji}) , where 1 ≤ i ≤ m 1\leq i\leq m and 1 ≤ j ≤ n 1\leq j\leq n , be a collection of symbols. Then we define a chain of Bergman algebras B R 1 ​ ( e , f ) ⟶ B R 2 ​ ( e , f ) ⟶ B R 3 ​ ( e , f ) ⟶ B R ​ ( e , f ) , B^{1}_{R}(e,f)\longrightarrow B^{2}_{R}(e,f)\longrightarrow B^{3}_{R}(e,f)\longrightarrow B_{R}(e,f), as follows: B R 1 ​ ( e , f ) \displaystyle B^{1}_{R}(e,f) : = R ⁡ ⟨ h ⟩ / ⟨ e ​ h ​ f = h ⟩ \displaystyle:=R\big\langle h\big\rangle\big/\big\langle ehf=h\big\rangle B R 2 ​ ( e , f ) \displaystyle B^{2}_{R}(e,f) : = R ⁡ ⟨ h , h ∗ ⟩ / ⟨ e ​ h ​ f = h , f ​ h ∗ ​ e = h ∗ ⟩ \displaystyle:=R\big\langle h,h^{*}\big\rangle\big/\big\langle ehf=h,fh^{*}e=h^{*}\big\rangle B R 3 ​ ( e , f ) \displaystyle B^{3}_{R}(e,f) : = R ⁡ ⟨ h , h ∗ ⟩ / ⟨ e ​ h ​ f = h , f ​ h ∗ ​ e = h ∗ , h ​ h ∗ = e ⟩ \displaystyle:=R\big\langle h,h^{*}\big\rangle\big/\big\langle ehf=h,fh^{*}e=h^{*},hh^{*}=e\big\rangle and B R ​ ( e , f ) = B R 4 ​ ( e , f ) \displaystyle B_{R}(e,f)=B^{4}_{R}(e,f) : = R ⁡ ⟨ h , h ∗ ⟩ / ⟨ e ​ h ​ f = h , f ​ h ∗ ​ e = h ∗ , h ​ h ∗ = e , h ∗ ​ h = f ⟩ . \displaystyle:=R\big\langle h,h^{*}\big\rangle\big/\big\langle ehf=h,fh^{*}e=h^{*},hh^{*}=e,h^{*}h=f\big\rangle. (4.1) We further define, B R ​ ( e ) = R ⁡ ⟨ h ⟩ / ⟨ e ​ h ​ e = h , h 2 = h ⟩ . B_{R}(e)=R\big\langle h\big\rangle\big/\big\langle ehe=h,h^{2}=h\big\rangle. Here R ​ ⟨ h , h ∗ ⟩ R\langle h,h^{*}\rangle is the free ring generated by symbols h i ​ j h_{ij} and h j ​ i ∗ h^{*}_{ji} ’s with coefficients from the 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra R R . We assume the symbols commute with 𝗄 \mathsf{k} . In fact R ​ ⟨ h , h ∗ ⟩ R\langle h,h^{*}\rangle is the coproduct R ∗ 𝗄 𝗄 ⟨ h i ​ j , h j ​ i ∗ ∣ 1 ≤ i ≤ m , 1 ≤ j ≤ n ⟩ R*_{\mathsf{k}}\mathsf{k}\langle h_{ij},h^{*}_{ji}\mid 1\leq i\leq m,1\leq j\leq n\rangle . Furthermore, the relations such as e ​ h ​ f = h ehf=h stand for the collection of relations after multiplying the matrices and comparing the two sides of the equation. Clearly there is a canonical 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra homomorphim R → B R i ​ ( e , f ) R\rightarrow B_{R}^{i}(e,f) , where the images of e e and f f in B R ​ ( e , f ) B_{R}(e,f) become equivalent. In this note, we particularly concentrate on the Bergman algebra B R ​ ( e , f ) B_{R}(e,f) defined in ( 4.1 ), as many of combinatorial algebras can be realised as such Bergman algebras. Example 4.2 . We can recover classical Cohn and Leavitt algebras ( 3.3 ) and ( 3.2 ), respectively, as follows. For a field 𝗄 \mathsf{k} , and idempotent matrices 1 ∈ 𝗄 1\in\mathsf{k} and I n ∈ 𝕄 n ​ ( 𝗄 ) I_{n}\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(\mathsf{k}) , we have B 𝗄 1 ​ ( 1 , I n ) \displaystyle B^{1}_{\mathsf{k}}(1,I_{n}) ≅ 𝗄 ⁡ ⟨ x 1 , x 2 , … , x n ⟩ \displaystyle\cong\mathsf{k}\langle x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{n}\rangle B 𝗄 2 ​ ( 1 , I n ) \displaystyle B^{2}_{\mathsf{k}}(1,I_{n}) ≅ 𝗄 ⁡ ⟨ x 1 , x 2 , … , x n , x 1 ∗ , x 2 ∗ , … , x n ∗ ⟩ \displaystyle\cong\mathsf{k}\langle x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{n},x_{1}^{*},x_{2}^{*},\dots,x_{n}^{*}\rangle B 𝗄 3 ​ ( I n , 1 ) \displaystyle B^{3}_{\mathsf{k}}(I_{n},1) ≅ C 𝗄 ​ ( 1 , n ) \displaystyle\cong C_{\mathsf{k}}(1,n) B 𝗄 ​ ( 1 , I n ) = B 𝗄 4 ​ ( 1 , I n ) \displaystyle B_{\mathsf{k}}(1,I_{n})=B^{4}_{\mathsf{k}}(1,I_{n}) ≅ L 𝗄 ​ ( 1 , n ) , \displaystyle\cong L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,n), furthermore B ⁡ ( I n , I n + k ) = B 4 ​ ( I n , I n + k ) \displaystyle B(I_{n},I_{n+k})=B^{4}(I_{n},I_{n+k}) ≅ L 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) \displaystyle\cong L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k) B 3 ​ ( I n + k , I n ) \displaystyle B^{3}(I_{n+k},I_{n}) ≅ C 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) \displaystyle\cong C_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k) Remark 4.3 . In this note we use categories of left modules over rings. One can also use categories of right modules. In that case, one would have B 𝗄 3 ​ ( 1 , I n ) ≅ C 𝗄 ​ ( 1 , n ) B^{3}_{\mathsf{k}}(1,I_{n})\cong C_{\mathsf{k}}(1,n) returning to right R R -modules. Example 4.4 . This example demonstrates how rich the structure of seemingly easy to construct Bergman algebras of ( 4.1 ) could be. Consider the Bergman algebra B 𝕄 2 ​ ( 𝗄 ) ​ ( 1 , 1 ) B_{\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})}(1,1) . Here 1 1 is the identity matrix of the 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra 𝕄 2 ​ ( 𝗄 ) \mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k}) . One can immediately see that B 𝕄 2 ​ ( 𝗄 ) ​ ( 1 , 1 ) ≅ 𝕄 2 ​ ( 𝗄 ) ∗ 𝗄 𝗄 ⁡ [ x , x − 1 ] . B_{\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})}(1,1)\cong\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})*_{\mathsf{k}}\mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}]. Furthermore, the discussion in Section 4 of [ 7 ] shows that the Leavitt algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( 2 , 2 ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(2,2) coincides with a corner of this Bergman algebra, specifically, L 𝗄 ​ ( 2 , 2 ) ≅ e 11 ​ B 𝕄 2 ​ ( 𝗄 ) ​ ( 1 , 1 ) ​ e 11 . L_{\mathsf{k}}(2,2)\cong e_{11}B_{\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k})}(1,1)e_{11}. Lemma 4.5 . Let R R be a 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra, where 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a commutative ring. Let e ∈ 𝕄 m ​ ( R ) e\in\mathbb{M}_{m}(R) and f ∈ 𝕄 n ​ ( R ) f\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R) be idempotent matrices. If idempotent matrices e ′ ∈ 𝕄 k ​ ( R ) e^{\prime}\in\mathbb{M}_{k}(R) and f ′ ∈ 𝕄 l ​ ( R ) f^{\prime}\in\mathbb{M}_{l}(R) are equivalent to e e and f f , respectively, then B R i ​ ( e , f ) B_{R}^{i}(e,f) is 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra isomorphic to B R i ​ ( e ′ , f ′ ) B_{R}^{i}(e^{\prime},f^{\prime}) , where 1 ≤ i ≤ 4 1\leq i\leq 4 . Proof. We show that if e ∼ e ′ e\sim e^{\prime} then B R i ​ ( e , f ) ≅ B R i ​ ( e ′ , f ) B_{R}^{i}(e,f)\cong B_{R}^{i}(e^{\prime},f) . The general result follows by symmetry. Since e ∼ e ′ e\sim e^{\prime} , there are matrices x ∈ 𝕄 m × k ​ ( R ) x\in\mathbb{M}_{m\times k}(R) , y ∈ 𝕄 k × m ​ ( R ) y\in\mathbb{M}_{k\times m}(R) such that e = x ​ y e=xy , e ′ = y ​ x e^{\prime}=yx . Define the map ϕ : B R i ​ ( e , f ) → B R i ​ ( e ′ , f ) \phi:B_{R}^{i}(e,f)\rightarrow B_{R}^{i}(e^{\prime},f) by sending h ↦ x ​ h ′ h\mapsto xh^{\prime} and h ∗ h^{*} ↦ h ∗ ′ ​ y \mapsto{h^{*}}^{\prime}y (if h ∗ h^{*} is present in the definition of B R i ​ ( e , f ) B_{R}^{i}(e,f) ). The converse map ψ : B R i ​ ( e ′ , f ) → B R i ​ ( e , f ) \psi:B_{R}^{i}(e^{\prime},f)\rightarrow B_{R}^{i}(e,f) is defined by sending h ′ ↦ y ​ h h^{\prime}\mapsto yh and h ∗ ′ ↦ h ∗ ​ x {h^{*}}^{\prime}\mapsto h^{*}x . We leave it to the reader to check these assignments respect the defining relations and give a 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra isomorphism between the two algebras. ∎ Example 4.6 . We note that if the idempotents e ∈ 𝕄 m ​ ( R ) e\in\mathbb{M}_{m}(R) and f ∈ 𝕄 n ​ ( R ) f\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R) are equivalent, i.e., e = h ​ h ∗ e=hh^{*} and f = h ∗ ​ h f=h^{*}h , then by considering h 1 = e ​ h ​ f h_{1}=ehf and h 1 ∗ = f ​ h ​ e h_{1}^{*}=fhe we have e = h 1 ​ h 1 ∗ e=h_{1}h_{1}^{*} and f = h 1 ∗ ​ h 1 f=h_{1}^{*}h_{1} , with, e ​ h 1 ​ f = h 1 eh_{1}f=h_{1} and f ​ h 1 ∗ ​ e = h 1 ∗ fh_{1}^{*}e=h_{1}^{*} . However the algebras B ′ ​ ( e , f ) := R ⁡ ⟨ h , h ∗ ⟩ / ⟨ h ​ h ∗ = e , h ∗ ​ h = f ⟩ . B^{\prime}(e,f):=R\big\langle h,h^{*}\big\rangle\big/\big\langle hh^{*}=e,h^{*}h=f\big\rangle. and B R ​ ( e , f ) = R ⁡ ⟨ h , h ∗ ⟩ / ⟨ e ​ h ​ f = h , f ​ h ∗ ​ e = h ∗ , h ​ h ∗ = e , h ∗ ​ h = f ⟩ . B_{R}(e,f)=R\big\langle h,h^{*}\big\rangle\big/\big\langle ehf=h,fh^{*}e=h^{*},hh^{*}=e,h^{*}h=f\big\rangle. are not necessarily isomorphic, as the following example shows. Suppose that 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a field (or more generally an integral domain). Let e = f ∈ 𝕄 2 ​ ( 𝗄 ) e=f\in\mathbb{M}_{2}(\mathsf{k}) be the idempotent matrix which has a one at position ( 1 , 1 ) (1,1) and zeros elsewhere (note that the projective 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -module P P defined by e e is just 𝗄 \mathsf{k} , the free 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -module of rank 1). Let R R be the free 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra 𝗄 ​ ⟨ h , h ∗ ⟩ \mathsf{k}\langle h,h^{*}\rangle subject to the relations { h ​ h ∗ = e , h ∗ ​ h = e , e ​ h ​ e = h , e ​ h ∗ ​ e = h ∗ } \{hh^{*}=e,h^{*}h=e,ehe=h,eh^{*}e=h^{*}\} . It follows from the relations e ​ h ​ e = h ehe=h , e ​ h ∗ ​ e = h ∗ eh^{*}e=h^{*} that all entries of h h and h ∗ h^{*} that are not at position ( 1 , 1 ) (1,1) are zero. So R R is just the Laurent polynomial ring 𝗄 ⁡ [ x , x − 1 ] \mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}] . Let S S be the free 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra 𝗄 ​ ⟨ h , h ∗ ⟩ \mathsf{k}\langle h,h^{*}\rangle subject to the relations { h ​ h ∗ = e , h ∗ ​ h = e } \{hh^{*}=e,h^{*}h=e\} . Since e = h ​ h ∗ = h ​ h ∗ ​ h ​ h ∗ = h ​ e ​ h ∗ = h ​ e ​ e ​ h ∗ e=hh^{*}=hh^{*}hh^{*}=heh^{*}=heeh^{*} implies e = e ​ h ​ e ​ e ​ h ∗ ​ e , e=eheeh^{*}e, it follows that h 11 ​ h 11 ∗ = 1 h_{11}h^{*}_{11}=1 . But we also have h 11 ​ h 11 ∗ + h 12 ​ h 21 ∗ = ( h ​ h ∗ ) 11 = e 11 = 1 h_{11}h^{*}_{11}+h_{12}h^{*}_{21}=(hh^{*})_{11}=e_{11}=1 and hence h 12 ​ h 21 ∗ = 0 h_{12}h^{*}_{21}=0 . If both h 12 h_{12} and h 21 ∗ h^{*}_{21} are non-zero in S S , then the ring S S has zero divisors, while R = 𝗄 ⁡ [ x , x − 1 ] R=\mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}] has not. Suppose now that one of the entries h 12 h_{12} or h 21 ∗ h^{*}_{21} is zero in S S . Clearly h 21 ∗ ​ h 12 + h 22 ∗ ​ h 22 = ( h ∗ ​ h ) 22 = e 22 = 0 h^{*}_{21}h_{12}+h^{*}_{22}h_{22}=(h^{*}h)_{22}=e_{22}=0 and hence h 22 ∗ ​ h 22 = 0 h^{*}_{22}h_{22}=0 since h 12 = 0 h_{12}=0 or h 21 ∗ = 0 h^{*}_{21}=0 . We show that h 22 ∗ h^{*}_{22} and h 22 h_{22} are non-zero in S S , which implies that S S has zero divisors. Let R ′ R^{\prime} be the ring obtained from R = 𝗄 ⁡ [ x , x − 1 ] R=\mathsf{k}[x,x^{-1}] by adjoining two elements y y and z z subject to y ​ z = 0 yz=0 and z ​ y = 0 zy=0 . Then y y and z z are non-zero in R ′ R^{\prime} . Since diag ⁡ ( x , y ) ​ diag ⁡ ( x − 1 , z ) = diag ⁡ ( 1 , 0 ) = diag ⁡ ( x − 1 , z ) ​ diag ⁡ ( x , y ) , \operatorname{diag}(x,y)\operatorname{diag}(x^{-1},z)=\operatorname{diag}(1,0)=\operatorname{diag}(x^{-1},z)\operatorname{diag}(x,y), there is a ring homomorphism S → R ′ S\rightarrow R^{\prime} mapping any r ∈ R r\in R to itself, and h 11 ↦ x , h 11 ∗ ↦ x − 1 , h 22 ↦ y , h 22 ∗ ↦ z , h_{11}\mapsto x,h^{*}_{11}\mapsto x^{-1},h_{22}\mapsto y,h^{*}_{22}\mapsto z, and the other entries of h h and h ∗ h^{*} to zero. Since the image of h 22 h_{22} under this homomorphism is non-zero, h 22 h_{22} must be non-zero as well. Similarly it follows that h 22 ∗ h^{*}_{22} is non-zero. Thus S S has zero divisors while R R has not. The rings introduced in Definition 4.1 , come directly from Bergman’s machinery of universal constructions, by working with idempotent matrices instead of projective modules. Let P P and Q Q be non-zero finitely generated projective R R -modules. In [ 7 ] , Bergman constructed a ring S 1 = R ⟨ h : P ¯ → Q ¯ ⟩ S_{1}=R\big\langle h:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q}\big\rangle such that there is a “universal” S 1 S_{1} -module homomorphism h : S 1 ⊗ R P → S 1 ⊗ R Q h:S_{1}\otimes_{R}P\rightarrow S_{1}\otimes_{R}Q . He further constructed universal algebras S 2 = R ⟨ h , h ∗ : P ¯ → Q ¯ , Q ¯ → P ¯ ⟩ S_{2}=R\big\langle h,h^{*}:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q},\overline{Q}\rightarrow\overline{P}\big\rangle and S 3 = R ⟨ h , h ∗ : P ¯ → Q ¯ , Q ¯ → P ¯ , h ∗ h = 1 ⟩ S_{3}=R\big\langle h,h^{*}:\overline{P}\rightarrow\overline{Q},\overline{Q}\rightarrow\overline{P},h^{*}h=1\big\rangle and finally S = S 4 = R ⟨ h , h ∗ : P ¯ ≅ Q ¯ ⟩ . S=S_{4}=R\big\langle h,h^{*}:\overline{P}\cong\overline{Q}\big\rangle. Thus, over S S we have S ⊗ R P ≅ S ⊗ R Q S\otimes_{R}P\cong S\otimes_{R}Q . Representing P P and Q Q as idempotent matrices e e and f f , respectively, in § 5.3 we will establish that the rings S i S_{i} , above coincide with the Bergman algebras B R i ​ ( e , f ) B^{i}_{R}(e,f) , 1 ≤ i ≤ 4 1\leq i\leq 4 , in Definition 4.1 , which explains our terminology. In fact in § 5.3 we will carry out the graded version of these constructions. By setting the grade group trivial we obtain the above statements. We note that the paper [ 7 ] works with right modules whereas we work here with left modules. We can extend the definition of Bergman algebras to families of pairs of idempotents. Let ( e , f ) := { ( e i , f i ) } i ∈ I (e,f):=\{(e_{i},f_{i})\}_{i\in I} , be a collection of pairs of idempotents. Then we define B R ( e , f ) := R ⟨ h i , h i ∗ ∣ i ∈ I ⟩ / ⟨ e i h i f i = h i , f i h i ∗ e i = h i ∗ , h i h i ∗ = e i , h i ∗ h i = f i ∣ i ∈ I ⟩ . B_{R}(e,f):=R\big\langle h_{i},h_{i}^{*}\mid i\in I\big\rangle\big/\big\langle e_{i}h_{i}f_{i}=h_{i},f_{i}h_{i}^{*}e_{i}=h_{i}^{*},h_{i}h_{i}^{*}=e_{i},h_{i}^{*}h_{i}=f_{i}\mid i\in I\big\rangle. (4.2) Similarly we define B R i ​ ( e , f ) B^{i}_{R}(e,f) , for 1 ≤ i ≤ 3 1\leq i\leq 3 as well as B R ​ ( e ) B_{R}(e) . Example 4.7 . Let ( e , f ) (e,f) be the collection { ( 1 , 1 ) , ( 1 , 1 ) } \{(1,1),(1,1)\} . Then B 𝗄 ​ ( e , f ) = 𝗄 ⁡ ⟨ x , x − 1 , y , y − 1 ⟩ , B_{\mathsf{k}}(e,f)=\mathsf{k}\langle x,x^{-1},y,y^{-1}\rangle, the (non-commutative) Laurent polynomial ring with two variables. Next we define the graded version of the Bergman algebras of Definition 4.1 . Definition 4.8 . Let R R be a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra, where 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a Γ \Gamma -graded commutative ring. Let e ∈ 𝕄 m ​ ( R ) ​ ( β ¯ ) 0 e\in\mathbb{M}_{m}(R)(\overline{\beta})_{0} and f ∈ 𝕄 n ​ ( R ) ​ ( γ ¯ ) 0 f\in\mathbb{M}_{n}(R)(\overline{\gamma})_{0} be idempotent matrices, where β ¯ = ( β 1 , … , β m ) \overline{\beta}=(\beta_{1},\dots,\beta_{m}) and γ ¯ = ( γ 1 , … , γ n ) \overline{\gamma}=(\gamma_{1},\dots,\gamma_{n}) . Furthermore, let h = ( h i ​ j ) h=(h_{ij}) and h ∗ = ( h j ​ i ∗ ) h^{*}=(h^{*}_{ji}) , where 1 ≤ i ≤ m 1\leq i\leq m and 1 ≤ j ≤ n 1\leq j\leq n , be a collection of symbols. To the symbols h i ​ j h_{ij} assign the degree γ j − β i \gamma_{j}-\beta_{i} and h j ​ i ∗ h^{*}_{ji} the degree β i − γ j \beta_{i}-\gamma_{j} for 1 ≤ i ≤ m 1\leq i\leq m and 1 ≤ j ≤ n 1\leq j\leq n . Then the Bergman algebra B R i ​ ( e , f ) B_{R}^{i}(e,f) defined in Definition 4.1 form Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebras. For ( e , f ) := { ( e i , f i ) } i ∈ I (e,f):=\{(e_{i},f_{i})\}_{i\in I} , a collection of pairs of homogeneous idempotents, the graded Bergman algebra B R i ​ ( e , f ) B^{i}_{R}(e,f) is defined similarly as in ( 4.2 ) It has already been established in the literature that Leavitt path algebras can be realised as Bergman algebras (using the module theoretical language). This identification was then used to describe the non-stable K K -theory of Leavitt path algebras (Theorem 3.2.5 in [ 1 ] ). Lemma 4.9 . Suppose that R = Π t ∈ T ​ 𝗄 R=\Pi_{t\in T}\mathsf{k} , where 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is field and T T some finite set. For any t ∈ T t\in T , let ϵ t \epsilon_{t} be the element of R R whose t t -component is 1 1 and whose other components are 0 0 . Then any idempotent matrix with entries in R R is equivalent to a matrix of the form diag ⁡ ( ϵ t 1 , … , ϵ t n ) \operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_{t_{1}},\dots,\epsilon_{t_{n}}) , where t 1 , … , t n ∈ T t_{1},\dots,t_{n}\in T . Proof. Recall that there is a 1 − 1 1-1 correspondence between equivalence classes of idempotent matrices over R R and isomorphism classes of finitely generated projective R R -modules. For any t ∈ T t\in T , let P t = R ​ ϵ t P_{t}=R\epsilon_{t} . It is well-known that any finitely generated projective R R -module P P is isomorphic to a direct sum P = P t 1 ⊕ ⋯ ⊕ P t n P=P_{t_{1}}\oplus\dots\oplus P_{t_{n}} , where t 1 , … , t n ∈ T t_{1},\dots,t_{n}\in T . It follows that the corresponding idempotent matrix equals (up to equivalence) ( ϵ t 1 ) ⊕ ⋯ ⊕ ( ϵ t n ) = diag ⁡ ( ϵ t 1 , … , ϵ t n ) (\epsilon_{t_{1}})\oplus\dots\oplus(\epsilon_{t_{n}})=\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_{t_{1}},\dots,\epsilon_{t_{n}}) . ∎ The following theorem shows that if the 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra R R is commutative 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -semisimple, then the Bergman algebras B R ​ ( e , f ) B_{R}(e,f) of ( 4.2 ) coincide with the class of hyper Leavitt path algebras of Section 3.1 . Theorem 4.10 . Let 𝗄 \mathsf{k} be a field, Γ \Gamma an abelian group and R R a commutative semisimple 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra considered as Γ \Gamma -graded algebra concentrated in degree zero. Let ( e , f ) (e,f) be a family of pairs of homogeneous idempotents. Then the Bergman algebra B R ​ ( e , f ) B_{R}(e,f) as defined in ( 4.2 ) is Γ \Gamma -graded isomorphic to a unital hyper Leavitt path algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( H ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(H) whose grading is induced by a weight map. Conversely, any unital hyper Leavitt path algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( H ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(H) whose grading is induced by a weight map is graded isomorphic to a Bergman algebra B R ​ ( e , f ) B_{R}(e,f) , where R R is a commutative semisimple 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra concentrated in degree zero. Proof. First we show that if R R is a Γ \Gamma -graded commutative semisimple 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra concentrated in degree zero, then B R ​ ( e , f ) B_{R}(e,f) is graded isomorphic to a unital hyper Leavitt path algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( H ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(H) whose grading is induced by a weight map. Write R = Π t ∈ T ​ 𝗄 R=\Pi_{t\in T}\mathsf{k} , where T T is some finite set. For any t ∈ T t\in T we let ϵ t \epsilon_{t} be the element of R R whose t t -component is 1 1 and whose other components are 0 0 . Moreover, write ( e , f ) = { ( e u , f u ) } u ∈ U (e,f)=\{(e_{u},f_{u})\}_{u\in U} , where for any u ∈ U u\in U , e u ∈ 𝕄 m u ⁡ ( R ) ​ ( α ¯ u ) 0 e_{u}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{m_{u}}(R)(\overline{\alpha}_{u})_{0} and f u ∈ 𝕄 n u ⁡ ( R ) ​ ( β ¯ u ) 0 f_{u}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n_{u}}(R)(\overline{\beta}_{u})_{0} , α ¯ u ∈ Γ m u \overline{\alpha}_{u}\in\Gamma^{m_{u}} and β ¯ u ∈ Γ n u \overline{\beta}_{u}\in\Gamma^{n_{u}} , are homogeneous idempotent matrices. Since R = Π t ∈ T ​ 𝗄 R=\Pi_{t\in T}\mathsf{k} , by Lemma 4.5 and 4.9 , we may assume that for any u ∈ U u\in U , e u = diag ⁡ ( ϵ t u , 1 , … , ϵ t u , m u ) e_{u}=\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_{t_{u,1}},\dots,\epsilon_{t_{u,m_{u}}}) and f u = diag ⁡ ( ϵ t u , 1 ′ , … , ϵ t u , n u ′ ) f_{u}=\operatorname{diag}(\epsilon_{t^{\prime}_{u,1}},\dots,\epsilon_{t^{\prime}_{u,n_{u}}}) , for some t u , 1 , … , t u , m u , t u , 1 ′ , … , t u , n u ′ ∈ T t_{u,1},\dots,t_{u,m_{u}},t^{\prime}_{u,1},\dots,t^{\prime}_{u,n_{u}}\in T . Define a hypergraph H = ( H 0 , H 1 , s , r ) H=(H^{0},H^{1},s,r) by H 0 = { v t ∣ t ∈ T } H^{0}=\{v_{t}\mid t\in T\} , H 1 = { h u ∣ u ∈ U } H^{1}=\{h_{u}\mid u\in U\} , I h u = { 1 , … , m u } ​ ( h u ∈ H 1 ) I_{h_{u}}=\{1,\dots,m_{u}\}~(h_{u}\in H^{1}) , J h u = { 1 , … , n u } ​ ( h u ∈ H 1 ) J_{h_{u}}=\{1,\dots,n_{u}\}~(h_{u}\in H^{1}) , s 