:M\xrightarrow{}P a graded module homomorphism. Then there exists a Γ \Gamma -graded R ​ -ring 𝗄 R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}} S S such that S ⊗ R f = 0 S\otimes_{R}f=0 , and S S is universal for this property: Given any Γ \Gamma -graded R ​ -ring 𝗄 R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}} T T with T ⊗ R f = 0 T\otimes_{R}f=0 , there exists a unique graded homomorphism of R ​ -rings 𝗄 R\text{-rings}_{\mathsf{k}} , S → T S\xrightarrow{}T . Proof. We write P P as a direct summand of a graded free module ⨁ m ∈ K R ⁡ ( γ m ) \bigoplus_{m\in K}R(\gamma_{m}) . Then for any graded R ​ -ring 𝗄 R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}} T T , the map i ¯ : T ⊗ R P → ⨁ m ∈ K T ⁡ ( γ m ) \overline{i}:T\otimes_{R}P\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{m\in K}T(\gamma_{m}) induced by the inclusion i : P → ⨁ m ∈ K R ⁡ ( γ m ) i:P\xrightarrow{}\bigoplus_{m\in K}R(\gamma_{m}) is again an inclusion. Hence for each homogeneous elements x ∈ M x\in M , we write f ⁡ ( x ) = ∑ m ∈ K f m ​ x f(x)=\sum_{m\in K}f_{mx} where f m ​ x f_{mx} is a homogeneous element in R ⁡ ( γ m ) R(\gamma_{m}) . Observe that for any Γ \Gamma -graded R ​ -ring 𝗄 R\text{-ring}_{\mathsf{k}} T T , T ⊗ R f = 0 T\otimes_{R}f=0 if and only if for any homogeneous elements x ∈ ⋃ α ∈ Γ M α x\in\bigcup_{\alpha\in\Gamma}M_{\alpha} all the f m ​ x f_{mx} is zero in T T . So the desired universal ring S S is the quotient of R R by the two-sided ideal generated by these homogeneous elements f m ​ x f_{mx} . ∎ The ring constructed in Theorem 5.2 will be denoted by R ⟨ f : M ¯ → P ¯ ⟩ R\langle f:\overline{M}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P}\rangle (or R ​ ⟨ M ¯ → P ¯ ⟩ R\langle\overline{M}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P}\rangle ), and that of Theorem 5.4 by R ​ ⟨ f ¯ = 0 ⟩ R\langle\overline{f}=0\rangle . Note that the graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -homomorphism from R R to R ​ ⟨ f ¯ = 0 ⟩ R\langle\overline{f}=0\rangle in Theorem 5.4 is a canonical projection. 5.1.1. Combining Theorems 5.2 and 5.4 we can construct graded rings with rich structures. Let f : P → Q f:P\rightarrow Q be a module hom