\otimes_{R}g)-1_{S_{1}\otimes_{R}P} and that h g ∘ h ∗ − f h_{g}\circ h^{*}-f is the unique graded homomorphism corresponding to ( S 1 ⊗ R g ) ∘ i − 1 S 1 ⊗ R Q (S_{1}\otimes_{R}g)\circ i-1_{S_{1}\otimes_{R}Q} . We omit the details here. ∎ 5.4. Universal morphisms and representable functors We recall some facts on representable functors and universal morphisms. Let 𝒞 \mathcal{C} be a small category and F : 𝒞 → Sets F:\mathcal{C}\xrightarrow{}\operatorname{Sets} a functor, where Sets \operatorname{Sets} is the category of sets with set functions as morphisms. We say that F F is representable if there exists a natural isomorphism Hom 𝒞 ⁡ ( A , − ) → F \operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,-)\xrightarrow{}F for some object A A in 𝒞 \mathcal{C} . For an element A A in 𝒞 \mathcal{C} , we say that an element u ∈ F ⁡ ( A ) u\in F(A) is universal if for any object B B in 𝒞 \mathcal{C} and v ∈ F ⁡ ( B ) v\in F(B) , there is a unique morphism f : A → B f:A\xrightarrow{}B with v = F ​ ( f ) ​ ( u ) v=F(f)(u) . In this case, this property is called the universal property of ( A , u ) (A,u) . Lemma 5.11 . Let A A be an object in 𝒞 \mathcal{C} . The representations α : Hom 𝒞 ⁡ ( A , − ) → F \alpha:\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,-)\xrightarrow{}F are in bijection to universal elements of the form ( A , u ) (A,u) via α → α A ​ ( 1 A ) \alpha\xrightarrow{}\alpha_{A}(1_{A}) . Proof. By Yoneda Lemma, the natural transformations α : Hom 𝒞 ⁡ ( A , − ) → F \alpha:\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,-)\xrightarrow{}F correspond to elements in F ⁡ ( A ) F(A) via α ↦ α A ​ ( 1 A ) \alpha\mapsto\alpha_{A}(1_{A}) . It remains to check that α \alpha is an isomorphism if and only if α A ​ ( 1 A ) \alpha_{A}(1_{A}) in F ⁡ ( A ) F(A) is an universal element. Actually, for any object B B in 𝒞 \mathcal{C} , we have α B : Hom 𝒞 ⁡ ( A , B ) → F ⁡ ( B ) \alpha_{B}:\operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,B)\xrightarrow{}F(B) . By the naturality of α \alpha , for any morphism f : A → B f:A\xrightarrow{}B in 𝒞 \mathcal{C} , we have α B ​ ( f ) = α B ​ ( f ∘ 1 A ) = F ⁡ ( f ) ​ ( α A ​ ( 1 A ) ) \alpha_{B}(f)=\alpha_{B}(f\circ 1_{A})=F(f)(\alpha_{A}(1_{A})) . Thus α \alpha is a natural isomorphism if and only if for any object B B in 𝒞 \mathcal{C} , the map Hom 𝒞 ⁡ ( A , B ) → F ⁡ ( B ) , f ↦ F ⁡ ( f ) ​ ( α A ​ ( 1 A ) ) \operatorname{Hom}_{\mathcal{C}}(A,B)\xrightarrow{}F(B),f\mapsto F(f)(\alpha_{A}(1_{A})) is bijection, which is a restatement of saying that α A ​ ( 1 A ) \alpha_{A}(1_{A}) in F ⁡ ( A ) F(A) is a universal element. ∎ In what follows, we continue with the assumption that Γ \Gamma is an abelian group, 𝗄 \mathsf{k} a Γ \Gamma -graded commutative ring and R R a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra. Suppose that M M is a Γ \Gamma -graded R R -module and P P a graded finitely generated projective R R -module. Theorem 5.2 now says that the functor from Γ \Gamma -graded R ​ - rings 𝗄 R\text{-}\text{rings}_{\mathsf{k}} to Sets \operatorname{Sets} , associating to T T the set Hom T ​ - ​ 𝐆𝐫 ⁡ ( T ⊗ R M , T ⊗ R P ) \operatorname{Hom}_{T\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}}(T\otimes_{R}M,T\otimes_{R}P) , is representable. More precisely, we have the functor ℱ M , P gr : R ​ -gr-rings 𝗄 ⟶ Sets T ⟼ Hom T ​ - ​ 𝐆𝐫 ⁡ ( T ⊗ R M , T ⊗ R P ) \begin{split}\mathcal{F}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{M,P}:R\text{-gr-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}&\longrightarrow{}\operatorname{Sets}\\ T&\longmapsto\operatorname{Hom}_{T\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}}(T\otimes_{R}M,T\otimes_{R}P)\end{split} such that F M , P gr ( f ) = T ′ ⊗ T − F^{\operatorname{gr}}_{M,P}(f)=T^{\prime}\otimes_{T}- for any graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra homomorphism f : T → T ′ f:T\xrightarrow{}T^{\prime} . Then, there is a natural isomorphism α : Hom R ​ -gr-rings 𝗄 ⁡ ( S , − ) → F M , P gr \alpha:\operatorname{Hom}_{R\text{-gr-rings}_{\mathsf{k}}}(S,-)\xrightarrow{}F^{\operatorname{gr}}_{M,P} with S S the graded algebra constructed in Theorem 5.2 . We recall from [ 25 , Chapter 4] the notion of universal localization of algebras. Let R R be an algebra and σ \sigma be a family of morphisms between finitely generated projective R R -modules. A homomorphism θ : R → S \theta:R\xrightarrow{}S of algebras is called σ \sigma -inverting if for each morphism ξ ∈ σ \xi\in\sigma , the morphism S ⊗ R ξ S\otimes_{R}\xi in S S - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 {\mathbf{Mod}} is invertible; θ \theta is called a universal localization with respect to σ \sigma if in addition each σ \sigma -inverting homomorphism θ ′ : R → S ′ \theta^{\prime}:R\xrightarrow{}S^{\prime} factors uniquely through θ \theta . We adopt this to the setting of graded algebras. Let Γ \Gamma be an abelian group, R , R, S S two Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebras and Σ \Sigma a family of morphisms between Γ \Gamma -graded finitely generated projective R R -modules. A homomorphism θ : R → S \theta:R\xrightarrow{}S of Γ \Gamma -graded algebras is graded Σ \Sigma -inverting , if for all ξ ∈ Σ \xi\in\Sigma , the morphism S ⊗ R ξ S\otimes_{R}\xi in S S - 𝐆𝐫 \operatorname{{\bf Gr}} is invertible; θ \theta is called a graded universal localization with repect to Σ \Sigma if in addition every Σ \Sigma -inverting homomorphism θ ′ : R → S ′ \theta^{\prime}:R\xrightarrow{}S^{\prime} of Γ \Gamma -graded algebras factors uniquely through θ \theta . In [ 10 , Proposition 3.1] , it was shown that, the ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded universal localisation exists. Using our graded constructions, here we show that, for any arbitrary abelian group Γ \Gamma , the Γ \Gamma -graded universal localisation exists. Recall from § 5.1.1 that given a graded homomorphism f : P → Q f:P\rightarrow Q between graded finitely generated projective R R -modules P P and Q Q , we can construct a graded ring R ​ ⟨ f ¯ − 1 ⟩ R\langle{\overline{f}}^{-1}\rangle where the homomorphism f ¯ \overline{f} becomes invertible. Lemma 5.12 . Let R R and S S be Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebras and Σ \Sigma a family of morphisms between Γ \Gamma -graded finitely generated projective R R -modules. If θ : R → S \theta:R\xrightarrow{}{}S is a Γ \Gamma -graded universal localization with respect to Σ \Sigma , then S ≅ R ​ ⟨ ξ ¯ − 1 ⟩ ξ ∈ Σ S\cong R\langle\overline{\xi}^{-1}\rangle_{\xi\in\Sigma} . Proof. For each ξ : P ξ → Q ξ \xi:P_{\xi}\xrightarrow{}{}Q_{\xi} in Σ \Sigma , collect the pair of graded finitely generated projective R R -modules ( Q ξ , P ξ ) (Q_{\xi},P_{\xi}) . By a repeated application of Theorem 5.2 for each pair ( Q ξ , P ξ ) (Q_{\xi},P_{\xi}) , the Γ \Gamma -graded ring S Σ = R ⟨ Δ ξ : Q ¯ ξ → P ¯ ξ ∣ ξ ∈ Σ ⟩ , S_{\Sigma}=R\big\langle\Delta_{\xi}:\overline{Q}_{\xi}\rightarrow\overline{P}_{\xi}\mid\xi\in\Sigma\big\rangle, is the graded R ​ - ring 𝗄 R\text{-}\text{ring}_{\mathsf{k}} with a universal family of graded module homomorphisms { Δ ξ : S Σ ⊗ R Q ξ → S Σ ⊗ R P ξ | ( Q ξ , P ξ ) , ξ ∈ Σ } . \big\{\Delta_{\xi}:S_{\Sigma}\otimes_{R}Q_{\xi}\xrightarrow{}{}S_{\Sigma}\otimes_{R}P_{\xi}\,|\,(Q_{\xi},P_{\xi}),\xi\in\Sigma\big\}. As S Σ S_{\Sigma} is a graded R ​ - ring 𝗄 R\text{-}\text{ring}_{\mathsf{k}} , we have the graded algebra homomorphism θ 1 : R → S Σ \theta_{1}:R\xrightarrow{}S_{\Sigma} . Let R Σ R_{\Sigma} be the graded S Σ ​ - ring 𝗄 S_{\Sigma}\text{-}\text{ring}_{\mathsf{k}} such that { R Σ ⊗ θ 2 ( 1 − Δ ξ ∘ S Σ ⊗ θ 1 ξ ) = 0 ; R Σ ⊗ θ 2 ( 1 − S Σ ⊗ θ 1 ξ ∘ Δ ξ ) = 0 . \begin{cases}R_{\Sigma}\otimes_{\theta_{2}}(1-\Delta_{\xi}\circ S_{\Sigma}\otimes_{\theta_{1}}\xi)=0;\\ \ R_{\Sigma}\otimes_{\theta_{2}}(1-S_{\Sigma}\otimes_{\theta_{1}}\xi\circ\Delta_{\xi})=0.\end{cases} (5.10) Here θ 2 : S Σ → R Σ \theta_{2}:S_{\Sigma}\xrightarrow{}{}R_{\Sigma} is the graded algebra homomorphism such that R Σ R_{\Sigma} is a graded S Σ ​ - ring 𝗄 S_{\Sigma}\text{-}\text{ring}_{\mathsf{k}} . From the construction, we have R Σ = R ​ ⟨ ξ ¯ − 1 ⟩ ξ ∈ Σ R_{\Sigma}=R\langle\overline{\xi}^{-1}\rangle_{\xi\in\Sigma} . Since S S is a graded localisation ring, for each ξ : P ξ → Q ξ ∈ Σ \xi:P_{\xi}\xrightarrow{}{}Q_{\xi}\in\Sigma , we have the isomorphism S ⊗ R ξ : S ⊗ R P ξ → S ⊗ R Q ξ S\otimes_{R}\xi:S\otimes_{R}P_{\xi}\xrightarrow{}{}S\otimes_{R}Q_{\xi} . Now by the universal property of Δ ξ \Delta_{\xi} , there exists a unique graded algebra homomorphism λ : S Σ → S \lambda:S_{\Sigma}\xrightarrow{}{}S such that ( S ⊗ R ξ ) − 1 = S ⊗ λ Δ ξ (S\otimes_{R}\xi)^{-1}=S\otimes_{\lambda}\Delta_{\xi} . Hence S S satisfies ( 5.10 ), that is, { 1 − ( S ⊗ λ Δ ξ ) ∘ ( S ⊗ R ξ ) = S ⊗ λ ( 1 − Δ ξ ∘ ( S Σ ⊗ θ 1 ξ ) ) = 0 ; 1 − ( S ⊗ R ξ ) ∘ ( S ⊗ λ Δ ξ ) = S ⊗ λ ( 1 − ( S Σ ⊗ θ 1 ξ ) ∘ Δ ξ ) = 0 . \begin{cases}1-(S\otimes_{\lambda}\Delta_{\xi})\circ(S\otimes_{R}\xi)=S\otimes_{\lambda}(1-\Delta_{\xi}\circ(S_{\Sigma}\otimes_{\theta_{1}}\xi))=0;\\ 1-(S\otimes_{R}\xi)\circ(S\otimes_{\lambda}\Delta_{\xi})=S\otimes_{\lambda}(1-(S_{\Sigma}\otimes_{\theta_{1}}\xi)\circ\Delta_{\xi})=0.\end{cases} Next we prove that S S has the universal property of R Σ R_{\Sigma} . For any graded S Σ ​ - ring 𝗄 S_{\Sigma}\text{-}\text{ring}_{\mathsf{k}} T T with T ⊗ ( 1 − Δ ξ ∘ ( S Σ ⊗ θ 1 ξ ) ) = 0 T\otimes(1-\Delta_{\xi}\circ(S_{\Sigma}\otimes_{\theta_{1}}\xi))=0 and T ⊗ ( 1 − ( S Σ ⊗ θ 1 ξ ) ∘ Δ ξ ) = 0 , T\otimes(1-(S_{\Sigma}\otimes_{\theta_{1}}\xi)\circ\Delta_{\xi})=0, there exists a unique graded algebra homomorphism η : S → T \eta:S\xrightarrow{}{}T in the following diagram R \textstyle{R\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} θ 1 \scriptstyle{\theta_{1}} θ \scriptstyle{\theta} S Σ \textstyle{S_{\Sigma}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} λ \scriptstyle{\lambda} θ 2 T \scriptstyle{\theta_{2}^{T}} T \textstyle{T} S \textstyle{S\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} η \scriptstyle{\eta} such that θ 2 T ∘ θ 1 = η ∘ θ \theta_{2}^{T}\circ\theta_{1}=\eta\circ\theta . The proof is completed. ∎ We are in a position to extend [ 10 , Proposition 3.1 (1)] , which only worked for ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded algebras. The existence of a ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded universal localization follows from the argument in [ 25 , Theorem 4.1] adapted for categories with automorphisms. Here we prove that the existence of any Γ \Gamma -graded universal localization follows from graded Bergman’s constructions where Γ \Gamma is any abelian group. Recall that a Γ \Gamma -graded ring R R (with unit) is called left graded hereditary if any graded submodule of a graded projective left R R -module is (graded) projective, which is equivalent to that the graded global dimension for R R being less than or equal to 1 1 (see § 2.5 ). Proposition 5.13 . Let Γ \Gamma be an abelian group, R R a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra and Σ \Sigma a family of morphisms between Γ \Gamma -graded finitely generated projective R R -modules. We have the following statements. (1) The induced homomorphism R → R ​ ⟨ ξ ¯ − 1 ⟩ ξ ∈ Σ R\xrightarrow{}R\langle{\overline{\xi}}^{-1}\rangle_{\xi\in\Sigma} , r ↦ r ¯ r\mapsto\overline{r} , is a Γ \Gamma -graded universal localization of R R with respect to Σ \Sigma , denoted by θ Σ \theta_{\Sigma} , and is unique up to isomorphism. (2) Moreover, the underlying homomorphism θ Σ : R → R ​ ⟨ ξ ¯ − 1 ⟩ ξ ∈ Σ \theta_{\Sigma}:R\xrightarrow{}R\langle{\overline{\xi}}^{-1}\rangle_{\xi\in\Sigma} of ungraded algebras is a universal localization of R R with respect to σ = U ⁡ ( Σ ) \sigma=U(\Sigma) which is the image of Σ \Sigma under the forgetful functor U U . (3) If R R is graded hereditary, so is R ​ ⟨ ξ ¯ − 1 ⟩ ξ ∈ Σ R\langle{\overline{\xi}}^{-1}\rangle_{\xi\in\Sigma} . Proof. (1) Recall from ( 5.10 ) that S Σ = R ⟨ Δ ξ : Q ξ ¯ → P ξ ¯ | ξ : P ξ → Q ξ ∈ Σ ⟩ S_{\Sigma}=R\langle\Delta_{\xi}:\overline{Q_{\xi}}\xrightarrow{}\overline{P_{\xi}}|\xi:P_{\xi}\xrightarrow{}Q_{\xi}\in\Sigma\rangle and that R Σ R_{\Sigma} satisfies that R Σ ⊗ R ξ : R Σ ⊗ R P ξ → ∼ R Σ ⊗ R Q ξ , R_{\Sigma}\otimes_{R}\xi:R_{\Sigma}\otimes_{R}P_{\xi}\xrightarrow{\sim}R_{\Sigma}\otimes_{R}Q_{\xi}, is an isomorphism for each ξ : P ξ → Q ξ ∈ Σ \xi:P_{\xi}\xrightarrow{}Q_{\xi}\in\Sigma . For any Σ \Sigma -inverting graded homomorphism θ ′ : R → S ′ \theta^{\prime}:R\xrightarrow{}S^{\prime} , there exists a unique graded R R -algebra homomorphism λ : S Σ → S ′ \lambda:S_{\Sigma}\xrightarrow{}{}S^{\prime} such that ( S ′ ⊗ R ξ ) − 1 = S ′ ⊗ λ Δ ξ (S^{\prime}\otimes_{R}\xi)^{-1}=S^{\prime}\otimes_{\lambda}\Delta_{\xi} . Hence we have θ ′ = λ ∘ θ 1 . \theta^{\prime}=\lambda\circ\theta_{1}. (5.11) For each ξ \xi in Σ \Sigma , we have the isomorphism S ′ ⊗ R ξ : S ′ ⊗ R P ξ → S ′ ⊗ R Q ξ S^{\prime}\otimes_{R}\xi:S^{\prime}\otimes_{R}P_{\xi}\xrightarrow{}{}S^{\prime}\otimes_{R}Q_{\xi} , and { 1 − ( S ′ ⊗ λ Δ ξ ) ∘ ( S ′ ⊗ R ξ ) = S ′ ⊗ λ ( 1 − Δ ξ ∘ ( S Σ ⊗ θ 1 ξ ) ) = 0 ; 1 − ( S ′ ⊗ R ξ ) ∘ ( S ′ ⊗ λ Δ ξ ) = S ′ ⊗ λ ( 1 − ( S Σ ⊗ θ 1 ξ ) ∘ Δ ξ ) = 0 . \begin{cases}1-(S^{\prime}\otimes_{\lambda}\Delta_{\xi})\circ(S^{\prime}\otimes_{R}\xi)=S^{\prime}\otimes_{\lambda}(1-\Delta_{\xi}\circ(S_{\Sigma}\otimes_{\theta_{1}}\xi))=0;\\ 1-(S^{\prime}\otimes_{R}\xi)\circ(S^{\prime}\otimes_{\lambda}\Delta_{\xi})=S^{\prime}\otimes_{\lambda}(1-(S_{\Sigma}\otimes_{\theta_{1}}\xi)\circ\Delta_{\xi})=0.\end{cases} There exists a unique graded S Σ S_{\Sigma} -algebra homomorphism η : R Σ → S ′ \eta:R_{\Sigma}\xrightarrow{}S^{\prime} in the following diagram. R \textstyle{R\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} θ 1 \scriptstyle{\theta_{1}} θ ′ \scriptstyle{\theta^{\prime}} S Σ \textstyle{S_{\Sigma}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} λ \scriptstyle{\lambda} θ 2 \scriptstyle{\theta_{2}} R Σ \textstyle{R_{\Sigma}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} η \scriptstyle{\eta} S ′ \textstyle{S^{\prime}} Thus we have η ∘ θ 2 = λ . \eta\circ\theta_{2}=\lambda. (5.12) Therefore we have θ 2 ∘ θ 1 ∘ η = θ ′ \theta_{2}\circ\theta_{1}\circ\eta=\theta^{\prime} . Suppose that there exists η ′ : R Σ → S ′ \eta^{\prime}:R_{\Sigma}\xrightarrow{}S^{\prime} such that θ 2 ∘ θ 1 ∘ η ′ = θ ′ \theta_{2}\circ\theta_{1}\circ\eta^{\prime}=\theta^{\prime} . Combining with ( 5.11 ) we have that θ 2 ∘ θ 1 ∘ η ′ = θ ′ = λ ∘ θ 1 \theta_{2}\circ\theta_{1}\circ\eta^{\prime}=\theta^{\prime}=\lambda\circ\theta_{1} . By the uniqueness of λ \lambda we have λ = η ′ ∘ θ 2 \lambda=\eta^{\prime}\circ\theta_{2} . By comparing with ( 5.12 ) we have η = η ′ \eta=\eta^{\prime} . Then we prove that θ 2 ∘ θ 1 : R → R Σ \theta_{2}\circ\theta_{1}:R\xrightarrow{}R_{\Sigma} is a graded universal localization of R R with respect to Σ \Sigma , which will be denoted by θ Σ \theta_{\Sigma} . The uniqueness of θ Σ \theta_{\Sigma} up to isomorphism follows from the universal property of Bergman algebras. (2) Take Γ \Gamma to be the trivial group. Then we have the second statement. (3) By Bergman’s construction R ​ ⟨ ξ ¯ − 1 ⟩ ξ ∈ Σ R\langle{\overline{\xi}}^{-1}\rangle_{\xi\in\Sigma} is hereditary. As it is a graded ring, it is graded hereditary. ∎ 5.5. Path algebras as universal algebras Bergman’s proof of Theorem 5.3 in [ 7 ] indicates that any lower triangle matrix ring can be realised as the universal algebra of the form R ⟨ f i : P ¯ i → Q ¯ i ⟩ R\langle f_{i}:\overline{P}_{i}\rightarrow\overline{Q}_{i}\rangle , where R R can be chosen as an appropriate commutative 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -semisimple ring. Furthermore, inverting these maps, i.e., R ⟨ f i , f i − 1 : P ¯ i ≅ Q ¯ i ⟩ R\langle f_{i},f_{i}^{-1}:\overline{P}_{i}\cong\overline{Q}_{i}\rangle would then produce the whole matrix ring. As the lower triangle matrix rings are path algebras of certain kind of line graphs, it is natural to ask whether all path algebras of graphs can be obtained as universal algebras. Here we show this is in fact the case, namely for any finite graph E E , the path algebra P 𝗄 ( E ) ≅ R ⟨ f i : P ¯ i → Q ¯ i ⟩ P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\cong R\langle f_{i}:\overline{P}_{i}\rightarrow\overline{Q}_{i}\rangle , where R R is an commutative 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -semisimple ring and ( P i , Q i ) (P_{i},Q_{i}) are finitely generated projective 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -modules determined by the shape of the graph. Although it is usually quite difficult to describe the universal ring obtained by inverting morphisms (see for example [ 26 ] ), here we show that the Leavitt path algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(E) is the universal algebra, which inverts the maps f i : P i → Q i f_{i}:P_{i}\rightarrow Q_{i} . Although the results of this section might be known (or predictable) to the experts, we provide the details to demonstrate what it means to build a graded ring from the inset and not to assign the grading from the outset, as it has been done so far in the theory of combinatorial algebras. Namely, previously we would construct (Leavitt) path algebras and then assign a grading to them. By using our graded Bergman machinery, the algebras obtained are already equipped with a grading. Let E E be a finite graph. We use E reg E^{\operatorname{reg}} to denote the set of vertices in E E at which there are at least one edge starting from it. Recall that the path algebra of the graph E E is defined as P 𝗄 ( E ) = 𝗄 ⟨ E 0 , E 1 ⟩ / ⟨ u v = δ u , v , s ( e ) e = e r ( e ) = e , u , v ∈ E 0 , e ∈ E 1 ⟩ , P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)=\mathsf{k}\big\langle E^{0},E^{1}\big\rangle\big/\big\langle uv=\delta_{u,v},s(e)e=er(e)=e,u,v\in E^{0},e\in E^{1}\big\rangle, (5.13) where 𝗄 ​ ⟨ E 0 , E 1 ⟩ \mathsf{k}\big\langle E^{0},E^{1}\big\rangle is the free (non-unital) algebra with vertices and edges of the graph as the generators. Assigning zero to vertices and 1 1 to edges, since the defining relations in ( 5.13 ) are homogeneous, P 𝗄 ​ ( E ) P_{\mathsf{k}}(E) becomes a ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded ring (with positive support). In Lemma 5.14 we will realise P 𝗄 ​ ( E ) P_{\mathsf{k}}(E) as a ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded universal algebra. Consider the semisimple 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra R = ∏ E 0 𝗄 R=\prod_{E^{0}}\mathsf{k} , the product of | E 0 | |E^{0}| -copies of the field 𝗄 \mathsf{k} as a ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded ring which is concentrated in the zero component. Denote p v ​ ( i ) p_{v}(i) , where v ∈ E 0 v\in E^{0} and i ∈ ℤ i\in\mathbb{Z} , the graded finitely generated projective R R -module with 𝗄 ⁡ ( i ) \mathsf{k}(i) appears in v v -th component of R R and zero elsewhere. Throughout, we write p v p_{v} for p v ​ ( 0 ) p_{v}(0) . Recall that p v ​ ( i ) , i ∈ ℤ p_{v}(i),i\in\mathbb{Z} , is the shift of the projective R R -module p v p_{v} by i i . Note that ⨁ v ∈ E 0 p v ≅ R \bigoplus_{v\in E^{0}}p_{v}\cong R as graded R R -modules. Lemma 5.14 . Let E E be a finite graph and 𝗄 \mathsf{k} a field. Let R = ∏ E 0 𝗄 R=\prod_{E^{0}}\mathsf{k} be the ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded algebra concentrated in degree zero, with graded finitely generated projective modules p v ​ ( i ) p_{v}(i) as above. Then we have a ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra isomorphism P 𝗄 ( E ) ≅ R ⟨ g v : p v ¯ ⟶ ⨁ e ∈ s − 1 ​ ( v ) p r ⁡ ( e ) ​ ( 1 ) ¯ , v ∈ E reg ⟩ . P_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\cong R\,\big\langle g^{v}:\overline{p_{v}}\longrightarrow{}\overline{\bigoplus_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}{p_{r(e)}(1)}},\,{v\in E^{\operatorname{reg}}}\big\rangle. Proof. We observe that for any regular vertex v ∈ E 0 v\in E^{0} , we have the following diagram with exact rows of R R -modules ⨁ w ≠ v p w \textstyle{\bigoplus_{w\neq v}p_{w}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} u \scriptstyle{u} R \textstyle{R\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} ε \scriptstyle{\varepsilon} h v \scriptstyle{h^{v}} p v \textstyle{p_{v}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} 0 \textstyle{0} Q ⁡ ( 1 ) \textstyle{Q(1)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} ⨁ e ∈ s − 1 ​ ( v ) R ⁡ ( 1 ) \textstyle{\bigoplus\limits_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}R(1)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} 1 − f v \scriptstyle{1-f^{v}} f v \scriptstyle{f^{v}} ⨁ e ∈ s − 1 