) ( k , m ∈ M ; γ ∈ A ) ⟩ , \begin{split}T_{A}=\Big\langle&rp_{\beta}~~(r\in R_{\alpha};~\alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A});\quad h^{(\gamma)}_{jm},(h^{\prime})^{(\gamma)}_{mj}~~(\gamma\in A,j\in J,m\in M)\mid{}\\ &rp_{\beta}+sp_{\beta}=(r+s)p_{\beta}\quad(r,s\in R_{\alpha};~\alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A}),\\ &rp_{\beta}sp_{\beta^{\prime}}=rs_{\beta-\beta^{\prime}}p_{\beta^{\prime}}\quad(r\in R_{\alpha};~s\in R_{\alpha^{\prime}};~\alpha+\beta,\beta,\alpha^{\prime}+\beta^{\prime},\beta^{\prime}\in\Gamma_{A}),\\ &h_{jm}^{(\gamma)}(1p_{\gamma-\gamma_{m}})=h_{jm}^{(\gamma)}=(1p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}})h_{jm}^{(\gamma)}\quad(j\in J,m\in M,\gamma\in A),\\ &(h^{\prime})_{mj}^{(\gamma)}(1p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}})=(h^{\prime})_{mj}^{(\gamma)}=(1p_{\gamma-\gamma_{m}})(h^{\prime})_{mj}^{(\gamma)}\quad(j\in J,m\in M,\gamma\in A),\\ &\sum_{i\in J}d^{(\gamma)}_{ji}h_{im}^{(\gamma)}=h_{jm}^{(\gamma)}=\sum_{k\in M}h_{jk}^{(\gamma)}e_{km}^{(\gamma)}\quad(j\in J,m\in M,\gamma\in A),\\ &\sum_{k\in M}e_{mk}^{(\gamma)}(h^{\prime}_{kj})^{(\gamma)}=(h^{\prime}_{mj})^{(\gamma)}=\sum_{i\in J}(h^{\prime})_{mi}^{(\gamma)}d_{ij}^{(\gamma)}\quad(j\in J,m\in M,\gamma\in A),\\ &\sum_{m\in M}h_{im}^{(\gamma)}(h^{\prime})^{(\gamma)}_{mj}=d_{ij}^{(\gamma)}\quad(i,j\in J;~\gamma\in A),\\ &\sum_{j\in J}(h^{\prime})_{kj}^{(\gamma)}h_{jm}^{(\gamma)}=e_{km}^{(\gamma)}\quad(k,m\in M;~\gamma\in A)\Big\rangle,\end{split} (6.3) where Γ A = { γ − γ m , γ − β j ∣ γ ∈ A , j ∈ J , m ∈ M } \Gamma_{A}=\{\gamma-\gamma_{m},\gamma-\beta_{j}\mid\gamma\in A,j\in J,m\in M\} . Clearly T A T_{A} is a unital algebra with identity ∑ γ ∈ Γ A p γ \sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma_{A}}p_{\gamma} . Moreover, S ​ # ​ Γ = lim → A ⁡ T A , S\#\Gamma=\varinjlim_{A}T_{A}, where A A runs through all finite subsets of Γ \Gamma . We will show that the T A T_{A} ’s are isomorphic to Bergman algebras. Let A ⊆ Γ A\subseteq\Gamma a finite subset. We denote by R ​ # ​ Γ A R\#\Gamma_{A} the subalgebra of R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma consisting of all sums of elements r ​ p β rp_{\beta} , where r ∈ R α r\in R_{\alpha} and α + β , β ∈ Γ A \alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A} . Note that R ​ # ​ Γ A R\#\Gamma_{A} is is a unital algebra with identity ∑ γ ∈ Γ A p γ \sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma_{A}}p_{\gamma} . Lemma 6.1 . The 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra R ​ # ​ Γ A R\#\Gamma_{A} has the presentation R # Γ A = ⟨ r p β ( r ∈ R α ; α + β , β ∈ Γ A ) ∣ r ​ p β + s ​ p β = ( r + s ) ​ p β ( r , s ∈ R α , α + β , β ∈ Γ A ) , r p β s p β ′ = r s β − β ′ p β ′ ( r ∈ R α ; s ∈ R α ′ ; α + β , β , α ′ + β ′ , β ′ ∈ Γ A ) ⟩ . \begin{split}R\#\Gamma_{A}=\Big\langle&rp_{\beta}\quad(r\in R_{\alpha};~\alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A})\mid{}\\ &rp_{\beta}+sp_{\beta}=(r+s)p_{\beta}\quad(r,s\in R_{\alpha};~\alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A}),\\ &rp_{\beta}sp_{\beta^{\prime}}=rs_{\beta-\beta^{\prime}}p_{\beta^{\prime}}\quad(r\in R_{\alpha};~s\in R_{\alpha^{\prime}};~\alpha+\beta,\beta,\alpha^{\prime}+\beta^{\prime},\beta^{\prime}\in\Gamma_{A})\Big\rangle.\end{split} (6.4) Proof. Denote by Z Z the 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra presented by ( 6.4 ). It follows from Lemma 2.8 that there is a 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra homomorphism ϕ : Z → R ​ # ​ Γ \phi:Z\to R\#\Gamma such that ϕ ⁡ ( r ​ p γ ) = r ​ p γ \phi(rp_{\gamma})=rp_{\gamma} , where r ∈ R α r\in R_{\alpha} and α + β , β ∈ Γ A \alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A} . Clearly one can write any element z ∈ Z z\in Z in the form z = ∑ β ∈ Γ A ∑ α + β ∈ Γ A r β ( α ) ​ p β z=\sum_{\beta\in\Gamma_{A}}\sum_{\alpha+\beta\in\Gamma_{A}}r_{\beta}^{(\alpha)}p_{\beta} , where each r β ( α ) ∈ R α r^{(\alpha)}_{\beta}\in R_{\alpha} . It follows that ϕ ⁡ ( Z ) = R ​ # ​ Γ A \phi(Z)=R\#\Gamma_{A} . It remains to show that ϕ \phi is injective. Suppose that ϕ ⁡ ( z ) = 0 \phi(z)=0 , for some z ∈ Z z\in Z . Write z = ∑ β ∈ Γ A ∑ α + β ∈ Γ A r β ( α ) ​ p β z=\sum_{\beta\in\Gamma_{A}}\sum_{\alpha+\beta\in\Gamma_{A}}r_{\beta}^{(\alpha)}p_{\beta} , where each r β ( α ) ∈ R α r^{(\alpha)}_{\beta}\in R_{\alpha} . Since ϕ ⁡ ( z ) = 0 \phi(z)=0 , it follows that each r β ( α ) = 0 r^{(\alpha)}_{\beta}=0 . Thus ϕ \phi is injective and therefore Z ≅ ϕ ⁡ ( Z ) = R ​ # ​ Γ A Z\cong\phi(Z)=R\#\Gamma_{A} . ∎ For any γ ∈ A \gamma\in A define the matrices d ( γ ) \displaystyle d^{(\gamma)} : = ( d i ​ j ( γ ) ) i , j ∈ J = ( d i ​ j ​ p γ − β j ) i , j ∈ J ∈ 𝕄 J × J ⁡ ( R ​ # ​ Γ A ) , \displaystyle:=(d_{ij}^{(\gamma)})_{i,j\in J}=(d_{ij}p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}})_{i,j\in J}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{J\times J}(R\#\Gamma_{A}), e ( γ ) \displaystyle e^{(\gamma)} : = ( e k ​ m ( γ ) ) k , m ∈ K = ( e k ​ m ​ p γ − γ m ) k , m ∈ K ∈ 𝕄 M × M ⁡ ( R ​ # ​ Γ A ) . \displaystyle:=(e_{km}^{(\gamma)})_{k,m\in K}=(e_{km}p_{\gamma-\gamma_{m}})_{k,m\in K}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{M\times M}(R\#\Gamma_{A}). A straightforward computation shows that d ( γ ) d^{(\gamma)} and e ( γ ) e^{(\gamma)} are idempotent matrices. Hence they define idempotent endomorphisms of ⨁ j ∈ J R ​ # ​ Γ A \bigoplus_{j\in J}R\#\Gamma_{A} and ⨁ m ∈ M R ​ # ​ Γ A \bigoplus_{m\in M}R\#\Gamma_{A} , respectively. It follows that P A ( γ ) := ( ⨁ j ∈ J R ​ # ​ Γ A ) ​ d ( γ ) P_{A}^{(\gamma)}:=(\bigoplus_{j\in J}R\#\Gamma_{A})d^{(\gamma)} and Q A ( γ ) := ( ⨁ m ∈ M R ​ # ​ Γ A ) ​ e ( γ ) Q_{A}^{(\gamma)}:=(\bigoplus_{m\in M}R\#\Gamma_{A})e^{(\gamma)} are non-zero finitely generated projective R ​ # ​ Γ A R\#\Gamma_{A} -modules. Define the 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra B A := R # Γ A ⟨ g ( γ ) , ( g ( γ ) ) − 1 : P A ( γ ) ¯ ≅ Q A ( γ ) ¯ ( γ ∈ A ) ⟩ . B_{A}:=R\#\Gamma_{A}\Big\langle g^{(\gamma)},(g^{(\gamma)})^{-1}:\overline{P_{A}^{(\gamma)}}\cong\overline{Q_{A}^{(\gamma)}}~(\gamma\in A)\Big\rangle. We will show that T A ≅ B A T_{A}\cong B_{A} . By Lemma 5.8 and ( 6.4 ) we have B A = ⟨ r p β ( r ∈ R α ; α + β , β ∈ Γ A ) ; g ( γ ) j ​ m , ( g ′ ) ( γ ) m ​ j ( γ ∈ A , j ∈ J , m ∈ M ) ∣ r ​ p β + s ​ p β = ( r + s ) ​ p β ( r , s ∈ R α , α + β , β ∈ Γ A ) , r ​ p β ​ s ​ p β ′ = r ​ s β − β ′ ​ p β ′ ( r ∈ R α , s ∈ R α ′ , α + β , β , α ′ + β ′ , β ′ ∈ Γ A ) , ∑ i ∈ J d j ​ i ( γ ) ​ g i ​ m ( γ ) = g j ​ m ( γ ) = ∑ k ∈ M g j ​ k ( γ ) ​ e k ​ m ( γ ) ( j ∈ J , m ∈ M , γ ∈ A ) , ∑ k ∈ M e m ​ k ( γ ) ​ ( g k ​ j ′ ) ( γ ) = ( g m ​ j ′ ) ( γ ) = ∑ i ∈ J ( g ′ ) m ​ i ( γ ) ​ d i ​ j ( γ ) ( j ∈ J , m ∈ M , γ ∈ A ) , ∑ m ∈ M g i ​ m ( γ ) ​ ( g ′ ) m ​ j ( γ ) = d i ​ j ( γ ) ( i , j ∈ J , γ ∈ A ) , ∑ j ∈ J ( g ′ ) k ​ j ( γ ) g j ​ m ( γ ) = e k ​ m ( γ ) ( k , m ∈ M ; γ ∈ A ) ⟩ , \begin{split}B_{A}=\Big\langle&rp_{\beta}~(r\in R_{\alpha};~\alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A});~~~~g^{(\gamma)}_{jm},(g^{\prime})^{(\gamma)}_{mj}~~(\gamma\in A,j\in J,m\in M)\mid{}\\ &rp_{\beta}+sp_{\beta}=(r+s)p_{\beta}\quad(r,s\in R_{\alpha};~\alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A}),\\ &rp_{\beta}sp_{\beta^{\prime}}=rs_{\beta-\beta^{\prime}}p_{\beta^{\prime}}\quad(r\in R_{\alpha};~s\in R_{\alpha^{\prime}};~\alpha+\beta,\beta,\alpha^{\prime}+\beta^{\prime},\beta^{\prime}\in\Gamma_{A}),\\ &\sum_{i\in J}d^{(\gamma)}_{ji}g_{im}^{(\gamma)}=g_{jm}^{(\gamma)}=\sum_{k\in M}g_{jk}^{(\gamma)}e_{km}^{(\gamma)}\quad(j\in J,m\in M,\gamma\in A),\\ &\sum_{k\in M}e_{mk}^{(\gamma)}(g^{\prime}_{kj})^{(\gamma)}=(g^{\prime}_{mj})^{(\gamma)}=\sum_{i\in J}(g^{\prime})_{mi}^{(\gamma)}d_{ij}^{(\gamma)}\quad(j\in J,m\in M,\gamma\in A),\\ &\sum_{m\in M}g_{im}^{(\gamma)}(g^{\prime})^{(\gamma)}_{mj}=d_{ij}^{(\gamma)}\quad(i,j\in J;~\gamma\in A),\\ &\sum_{j\in J}(g^{\prime})_{kj}^{(\gamma)}g_{jm}^{(\gamma)}=e_{km}^{(\gamma)}\quad(k,m\in M;~\gamma\in A)\Big\rangle,\end{split} (6.5) Lemma 6.2 . In B A B_{A} the relations g j ​ m ( γ ) ​ ( 1 ​ p γ − γ m ) = g j ​ m ( γ ) = ( 1 ​ p γ − β j ) ​ g j ​ m ( γ ) ( j ∈ J , m ∈ M , γ ∈ A ) , \displaystyle g_{jm}^{(\gamma)}(1p_{\gamma-\gamma_{m}})=g_{jm}^{(\gamma)}=(1p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}})g_{jm}^{(\gamma)}\quad(j\in J,m\in M,\gamma\in A), (6.6) ( g ′ ) m ​ j ( γ ) ​ ( 1 ​ p γ − β j ) = ( g ′ ) m ​ j ( γ ) = ( 1 ​ p γ − γ m ) ​ ( g ′ ) m ​ j ( γ ) ( j ∈ J , m ∈ M , γ ∈ A ) , \displaystyle(g^{\prime})_{mj}^{(\gamma)}(1p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}})=(g^{\prime})_{mj}^{(\gamma)}=(1p_{\gamma-\gamma_{m}})(g^{\prime})_{mj}^{(\gamma)}\quad(j\in J,m\in M,\gamma\in A), (6.7) hold. Proof. We only prove relation ( 6.6 ) and leave relation ( 6.7 ) to the reader. Clearly g j ​ m ( γ ) ​ ( 1 ​ p γ − γ m ) = \displaystyle g_{jm}^{(\gamma)}(1p_{\gamma-\gamma_{m}})= ( ∑ k ∈ M g j ​ k ( γ ) ​ e k ​ m ( γ ) ) ​ ( 1 ​ p γ − γ m ) \displaystyle\Big(\sum_{k\in M}g_{jk}^{(\gamma)}e_{km}^{(\gamma)}\Big)(1p_{\gamma-\gamma_{m}}) = \displaystyle= ∑ k ∈ M g j ​ k ( γ ) ​ ( e k ​ m ​ p γ − γ m ) ​ ( 1 ​ p γ − γ m ) \displaystyle\sum_{k\in M}g_{jk}^{(\gamma)}(e_{km}p_{\gamma-\gamma_{m}})(1p_{\gamma-\gamma_{m}}) = \displaystyle= ∑ k ∈ M g j ​ k ( γ ) ​ ( e k ​ m ​ p γ − γ m ) \displaystyle\sum_{k\in M}g_{jk}^{(\gamma)}(e_{km}p_{\gamma-\gamma_{m}}) = \displaystyle= ∑ k ∈ M g j ​ k ( γ ) ​ e k ​ m ( γ ) \displaystyle\sum_{k\in M}g_{jk}^{(\gamma)}e_{km}^{(\gamma)} = \displaystyle= g j ​ m ( γ ) . \displaystyle g_{jm}^{(\gamma)}. Similarly ( 1 ​ p γ − β j ) ​ g j ​ m ( γ ) = \displaystyle(1p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}})g_{jm}^{(\gamma)}= ( 1 ​ p γ − β j ) ​ ∑ i ∈ J d j ​ i ( γ ) ​ g i ​ m ( γ ) \displaystyle(1p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}})\sum_{i\in J}d_{ji}^{(\gamma)}g^{(\gamma)}_{im} = \displaystyle= ( 1 ​ p γ − β j ) ​ ∑ i ∈ J ( d j ​ i ​ p γ − β i ) ​ g i ​ m ( γ ) \displaystyle(1p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}})\sum_{i\in J}(d_{ji}p_{\gamma-\beta_{i}})g^{(\gamma)}_{im} = \displaystyle= ∑ i ∈ J ( 1 ​ p γ − β j ) ​ ( d j ​ i ​ p γ − β i ) ​ g i ​ m ( γ ) \displaystyle\sum_{i\in J}(1p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}})(d_{ji}p_{\gamma-\beta_{i}})g^{(\gamma)}_{im} = \displaystyle= ∑ i ∈ J ( ( d j ​ i ) γ − β j − ( γ − β i ) ​ p γ − β i ) ​ g i ​ m ( γ ) \displaystyle\sum_{i\in J}((d_{ji})_{\gamma-\beta_{j}-(\gamma-\beta_{i})}p_{\gamma-\beta_{i}})g^{(\gamma)}_{im} = \displaystyle= ∑ i ∈ J ( d j ​ i ​ p γ − β i ) ​ g i ​ m ( γ ) \displaystyle\sum_{i\in J}(d_{ji}p_{\gamma-\beta_{i}})g^{(\gamma)}_{im} = \displaystyle= ∑ i ∈ J d j ​ i ( γ ) ​ g i ​ m ( γ ) \displaystyle\sum_{i\in J}d_{ji}^{(\gamma)}g^{(\gamma)}_{im} = \displaystyle= g j ​ m ( γ ) \displaystyle g_{jm}^{(\gamma)} since deg ⁡ ( d j ​ i ) = β i − β j \deg(d_{ji})=\beta_{i}-\beta_{j} . ∎ Proposition 6.3 . Let A ⊆ Γ A\subseteq\Gamma be a finite subset. Then T A ≅ B A T_{A}\cong B_{A} . Proof. The proposition follows from ( 6.3 ), ( 6.5 ), ( 6.6 ) and ( 6.7 ). ∎ The theorem below is the main result of this subsection. It follows directly from Proposition 6.3 and the fact that S ​ # ​ Γ = lim → A ⁡ T A S\#\Gamma=\varinjlim_{A}T_{A} , where A A runs through all finite subsets of Γ \Gamma . Theorem 6.4 . The 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra R ⟨ h , h − 1 : P ¯ ≅ Q ¯ ⟩ # Γ R\big\langle h,h^{-1}:\overline{P}\cong\overline{Q}\big\rangle\#\Gamma is the direct limit of the Bergman algebras R # Γ A ⟨ g ( γ ) , ( g ( γ ) ) − 1 : P A ( γ ) ¯ ≅ Q A ( γ ) ¯ ( γ ∈ A ) ⟩ , R\#\Gamma_{A}\Big\langle g^{(\gamma)},(g^{(\gamma)})^{-1}:\overline{P_{A}^{(\gamma)}}\cong\overline{Q_{A}^{(\gamma)}}~(\gamma\in A)\Big\rangle, where A A runs through all finite subsets of Γ \Gamma . We finish this subsection by proving a lemma which will be used in §7. For γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma we define the R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma -modules P ( γ ) := ( ⨁ j ∈ J R ​ # ​ Γ ) ​ d ( γ ) P^{(\gamma)}:=(\bigoplus_{j\in J}R\#\Gamma)d^{(\gamma)} and Q ( γ ) := ( ⨁ m ∈ M R ​ # ​ Γ ) ​ e ( γ ) Q^{(\gamma)}:=(\bigoplus_{m\in M}R\#\Gamma)e^{(\gamma)} . Lemma 6.5 . Let γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma . Then P ( γ ) ≅ P ⁡ ( − γ ) ​ # ​ Γ P^{(\gamma)}\cong P(-\gamma)\#\Gamma and Q ( γ ) ≅ Q ⁡ ( − γ ) ​ # ​ Γ Q^{(\gamma)}\cong Q(-\gamma)\#\Gamma as R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma -modules. Proof. We only prove that P ⁡ ( − γ ) ​ # ​ Γ ≅ P ( γ ) P(-\gamma)\#\Gamma\cong P^{(\gamma)} . The proof that Q ⁡ ( − γ ) ​ # ​ Γ ≅ Q ( γ ) Q(-\gamma)\#\Gamma\cong Q^{(\gamma)} is similar. Recall that P ⁡ ( − γ ) ​ # ​ Γ = P ⁡ ( − γ ) = P = Im ⁡ ( d ) P(-\gamma)\#\Gamma=P(-\gamma)=P=\operatorname{Im}(d) as a set. Hence any element of P ⁡ ( − γ ) ​ # ​ Γ P(-\gamma)\#\Gamma can be written as x ​ d xd where x ∈ ⨁ j ∈ J R ⁡ ( β j ) x\in\bigoplus_{j\in J}R(\beta_{j}) . We define the map θ : P ⁡ ( − γ ) ​ # ​ Γ \displaystyle\theta:P(-\gamma)\#\Gamma ⟶ P ( γ ) \displaystyle\longrightarrow P^{(\gamma)} x ​ d \displaystyle xd ⟼ x ( γ ) ​ d ( γ ) , \displaystyle\longmapsto x^{(\gamma)}d^{(\gamma)}, where x ( γ ) ∈ ⨁ j ∈ J R ​ # ​ Γ x^{(\gamma)}\in\bigoplus_{j\in J}R\#\Gamma is defined by x j ( γ ) = x j ​ p γ − β j ​ ( j ∈ J ) x^{(\gamma)}_{j}=x_{j}p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}}~(j\in J) . Clearly x ​ d = y ​ d \displaystyle xd=yd ⇔ \displaystyle\Leftrightarrow~~ ( x − y ) ​ d = 0 \displaystyle(x-y)d=0 ⇔ \displaystyle\Leftrightarrow~~ ∑ i ∈ J ( x i − y i ) ​ d i ​ j = 0 ​ ∀ j ∈ J ⁡ ( in ​ R ) \displaystyle\sum_{i\in J}(x_{i}-y_{i})d_{ij}=0~\forall j\in J~(\text{in }R) ⇔ \displaystyle\ \Leftrightarrow~~ ( ∑ i ∈ J ( x i − y i ) ​ d i ​ j ) ​ p γ − β j = 0 ​ ∀ j ∈ J ⁡ ( in ​ R ​ # ​ Γ ) \displaystyle(\sum_{i\in J}(x_{i}-y_{i})d_{ij})p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}}=0~\forall j\in J~(\text{in }R\#\Gamma) ⇔ \displaystyle\Leftrightarrow~~ ∑ i ∈ J ( x i − y i ) ​ d i ​ j ​ p γ − β j = 0 ​ ∀ j ∈ J \displaystyle\sum_{i\in J}(x_{i}-y_{i})d_{ij}p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}}=0~\forall j\in J ⇔ \displaystyle\Leftrightarrow~~ ∑ i ∈ J ( x i − y i ) ​ p γ − β i ​ d i ​ j ​ p γ − β j = 0 ​ ∀ j ∈ J \displaystyle\sum_{i\in J}(x_{i}-y_{i})p_{\gamma-\beta_{i}}d_{ij}p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}}=0~\forall j\in J ⇔ \displaystyle\Leftrightarrow~~ ∑ i ∈ J ( x i ( γ ) − y i ( γ ) ) ​ d i ​ j ( γ ) = 0 ​ ∀ j ∈ J \displaystyle\sum_{i\in J}(x^{(\gamma)}_{i}-y^{(\gamma)}_{i})d^{(\gamma)}_{ij}=0~\forall j\in J ⇔ \displaystyle\Leftrightarrow~~ ( x ( γ ) − y ( γ ) ) ​ d ( γ ) = 0 \displaystyle(x^{(\gamma)}-y^{(\gamma)})d^{(\gamma)}=0 ⇔ \displaystyle\Leftrightarrow~~ x ( γ ) ​ d ( γ ) = y ( γ ) ​ d ( γ ) \displaystyle x^{(\gamma)}d^{(\gamma)}=y^{(\gamma)}d^{(\gamma)} for any x , y ∈ ⨁ j ∈ J R ⁡ ( β j ) x,y\in\bigoplus_{j\in J}R(\beta_{j}) . Hence θ \theta is well-defined and injective. Next we show that θ \theta is surjective. Let z ∈ P ( γ ) = Im ⁡ ( d ( γ ) ) z\in P^{(\gamma)}=