\in J}e_{ik}p_{\gamma+\beta_{j}-\beta_{k}}d_{kj}p_{\gamma}\quad(i,j\in J;~\gamma\in\Gamma),\\ &\sum_{k\in J}e_{ik}p_{\gamma+\beta_{j}-\beta_{k}}e_{kj}p_{\gamma}=e_{ij}p_{\gamma}(i,j\in J;~\gamma\in\Gamma)\Big\rangle.\end{split} (6.16) For any γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma and i , j ∈ J i,j\in J define d i ​ j ( γ ) := d i ​ j ​ p γ − β j d_{ij}^{(\gamma)}:=d_{ij}p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}} and e i ​ j ( γ ) := e i ​ j ​ p γ − β j e^{(\gamma)}_{ij}:=e_{ij}p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}} . Using these conventions it follows from ( 6.16 ) that S # Γ = ⟨ r p γ , e i ​ j ( γ ) ( r ∈ R h ; γ ∈ Γ ; i , j ∈ J ) ∣ r ​ p γ + s ​ p γ = ( r + s ) ​ p γ ( r , s ∈ R δ , γ , δ ∈ Γ ) , r ​ p γ ​ s ​ p δ = r ​ s γ − δ ​ p δ ( r , s ∈ R h , γ , δ ∈ Γ ) , e i ​ j ( γ ) ​ ( 1 ​ p γ − β j ) = e i ​ j ( γ ) = ( 1 ​ p γ − β i ) ​ e i ​ j ( γ ) ( i , j ∈ J , γ ∈ Γ ) , ∑ k ∈ J d i ​ k ( γ ) ​ e k ​ j ( γ ) = e i ​ j ( γ ) = ∑ k ∈ J e i ​ k ( γ ) ​ d k ​ j ( γ ) ( i , j ∈ J , γ ∈ Γ ) , ∑ k ∈ J e i ​ k ( γ ) e k ​ j ( γ ) = e i ​ j ( γ ) ( i , j ∈ J ; γ ∈ Γ ) ⟩ . \begin{split}S\#\Gamma=\Big\langle&rp_{\gamma},e_{ij}^{(\gamma)}\quad(r\in R^{h};~\gamma\in\Gamma;~i,j\in J)\mid{}\\ &rp_{\gamma}+sp_{\gamma}=(r+s)p_{\gamma}\quad(r,s\in R_{\delta};~\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma),\\ &rp_{\gamma}sp_{\delta}=rs_{\gamma-\delta}p_{\delta}\quad(r,s\in R^{h};~\gamma,\delta\in\Gamma),\\ &e_{ij}^{(\gamma)}(1p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}})=e_{ij}^{(\gamma)}=(1p_{\gamma-\beta_{i}})e_{ij}^{(\gamma)}\quad(i,j\in J;~\gamma\in\Gamma),\\ &\sum_{k\in J}d_{ik}^{(\gamma)}e_{kj}^{(\gamma)}=e_{ij}^{(\gamma)}=\sum_{k\in J}e_{ik}^{(\gamma)}d_{kj}^{(\gamma)}\quad(i,j\in J;~\gamma\in\Gamma),\\ &\sum_{k\in J}e_{ik}^{(\gamma)}e_{kj}^{(\gamma)}=e_{ij}^{(\gamma)}(i,j\in J;~\gamma\in\Gamma)\Big\rangle.\end{split} (6.17) For any finite subset A ⊆ Γ A\subseteq\Gamma we define the k k -algebra T A T_{A} by T A = ⟨ r p β ( r ∈ R α ; α + β , β ∈ Γ A ) ; e i ​ j ( γ ) ( γ ∈ A ; i , j ∈ J ) ∣ r ​ p β + s ​ p β = ( r + s ) ​ p β ( r , s ∈ R α , α + β , β ∈ Γ A ) , r ​ p β ​ s ​ p β ′ = r ​ s β − β ′ ​ p β ′ ( r ∈ R α , s ∈ R α ′ , α + β , β , α ′ + β ′ , β ′ ∈ Γ A ) , e i ​ j ( γ ) ​ ( 1 ​ p γ − β j ) = e i ​ j ( γ ) = ( 1 ​ p γ − β i ) ​ e i ​ j ( γ ) ( i , j ∈ J , γ ∈ A ) , ∑ k ∈ J d i ​ k ( γ ) ​ e k ​ j ( γ ) = e i ​ j ( γ ) = ∑ k ∈ J e i ​ k ( γ ) ​ d k ​ j ( γ ) ( i , j ∈ J , γ ∈ A ) , ∑ k ∈ J e i ​ k ( γ ) e k ​ j ( γ ) = e i ​ j ( γ ) ( i , j ∈ J ; γ ∈ A ) ⟩ , \begin{split}T_{A}=\Big\langle&rp_{\beta}~(r\in R_{\alpha};~\alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A});~~~~e_{ij}^{(\gamma)}~(\gamma\in A;~i,j\in J)\mid{}\\ &rp_{\beta}+sp_{\beta}=(r+s)p_{\beta}\quad(r,s\in R_{\alpha};~\alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A}),\\ &rp_{\beta}sp_{\beta^{\prime}}=rs_{\beta-\beta^{\prime}}p_{\beta^{\prime}}\quad(r\in R_{\alpha};~s\in R_{\alpha^{\prime}};~\alpha+\beta,\beta,\alpha^{\prime}+\beta^{\prime},\beta^{\prime}\in\Gamma_{A}),\\ &e_{ij}^{(\gamma)}(1p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}})=e_{ij}^{(\gamma)}=(1p_{\gamma-\beta_{i}})e_{ij}^{(\gamma)}\quad(i,j\in J;~\gamma\in A),\\ &\sum_{k\in J}d_{ik}^{(\gamma)}e_{kj}^{(\gamma)}=e_{ij}^{(\gamma)}=\sum_{k\in J}e_{ik}^{(\gamma)}d_{kj}^{(\gamma)}\quad(i,j\in J;~\gamma\in A),\\ &\sum_{k\in J}e_{ik}^{(\gamma)}e_{kj}^{(\gamma)}=e_{ij}^{(\gamma)}\quad(i,j\in J;~\gamma\in A)\Big\rangle,\end{split} (6.18) where Γ A = { γ − β j ∣ γ ∈ A , j ∈ J } \Gamma_{A}=\{\gamma-\beta_{j}\mid\gamma\in A,j\in J\} . Clearly T A T_{A} is a unital algebra with identity ∑ γ ∈ Γ A p γ \sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma_{A}}p_{\gamma} . Moreover, S ​ # ​ Γ = lim → A ⁡ T A S\#\Gamma=\varinjlim_{A}T_{A} , where A A runs through all finite subsets of Γ \Gamma . We will show that the T A T_{A} ’s are isomorphic to Bergman algebras. Let A ⊆ Γ A\subseteq\Gamma a finite subset. We denote by R ​ # ​ Γ A R\#\Gamma_{A} the subalgebra of R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma consisting of all sums of elements r ​ p β rp_{\beta} , where r ∈ R α r\in R_{\alpha} and α + β , β ∈ Γ A \alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A} . Note that R ​ # ​ Γ A R\#\Gamma_{A} is is a unital algebra with identity ∑ γ ∈ Γ A p γ \sum_{\gamma\in\Gamma_{A}}p_{\gamma} . Lemma 6.6 . The algebra R ​ # ​ Γ A R\#\Gamma_{A} has the presentation R # Γ A = ⟨ r p β ( r ∈ R α ; α + β , β ∈ Γ A ) ∣ r ​ p β + s ​ p β = ( r + s ) ​ p β ( r , s ∈ R α , α + β , β ∈ Γ A ) , r p β s p β ′ = r s β − β ′ p β ′ ( r ∈ R α ; s ∈ R α ′ ; α + β , β , α ′ + β ′ , β ′ ∈ Γ A ) ⟩ . \begin{split}R\#\Gamma_{A}=\Big\langle&rp_{\beta}\quad(r\in R_{\alpha};~\alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A})\mid{}\\ &rp_{\beta}+sp_{\beta}=(r+s)p_{\beta}\quad(r,s\in R_{\alpha};~\alpha+\beta,\beta\in\Gamma_{A}),\\ &rp_{\beta}sp_{\beta^{\prime}}=rs_{\beta-\beta^{\prime}}p_{\beta^{\prime}}\quad(r\in R_{\alpha};~s\in R_{\alpha^{\prime}};~\alpha+\beta,\beta,\alpha^{\prime}+\beta^{\prime},\beta^{\prime}\in\Gamma_{A})\Big\rangle.\end{split} (6.19) Proof. See the proof of Lemma 6.1 . ∎ For any γ ∈ A \gamma\in A define the matrices d ( γ ) \displaystyle d^{(\gamma)} : = ( d i ​ j ( γ ) ) i , j ∈ J = ( d i ​ j ​ p γ − β j ) i , j ∈ J ∈ 𝕄 J × J ⁡ ( R ​ # ​ Γ A ) . \displaystyle:=(d_{ij}^{(\gamma)})_{i,j\in J}=(d_{ij}p_{\gamma-\beta_{j}})_{i,j\in J}\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{J\times J}(R\#\Gamma_{A}). A straightforward computation shows that the matrices d ( γ ) d^{(\gamma)} are idempotent. Hence they define idempotent endomorphisms of ⨁ j ∈ J R ​ # ​ Γ A \bigoplus_{j\in J}R\#\Gamma_{A} . It follows that P A ( γ ) := ( ⨁ j ∈ J R ​ # ​ Γ A ) ​ d ( γ ) P_{A}^{(\gamma)}:=(\bigoplus_{j\in J}R\#\Gamma_{A})d^{(\gamma)} are non-zero finitely generated projective R ​ # ​ Γ A R\#\Gamma_{A} -modules. Define the 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra B A := R # Γ A ⟨ f ( γ ) : P A ( γ ) ¯ → P A ( γ ) ¯ ; ( f ( γ ) ) 2 = f ( γ ) ( γ ∈ A ) ⟩ . B_{A}:=R\#\Gamma_{A}\Big\langle f^{(\gamma)}:\overline{P_{A}^{(\gamma)}}\to\overline{P_{A}^{(\gamma)}};~(f^{(\gamma)})^{2}=f^{(\gamma)}~(\gamma\in A)\Big\rangle. We will show that T A ≅ B A T_{A}\cong B_{A} . By Lemma 5.8 and ( 6.19 ) we have B A = ⟨ r p β ( r ∈ R α ; α + β , β ∈ Γ A ) ; f i ​ j ( γ ) ( γ ∈ A ; i , j ∈ J ) ∣ r ​ p β + s ​ p β = ( r