 A A runs through all finite subsets of Γ \Gamma . We finish this subsection with a lemma which will be used in §7. For γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma we define the R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma -module P ( γ ) := ( ⨁ j ∈ J R ​ # ​ Γ ) ​ d ( γ ) P^{(\gamma)}:=(\bigoplus_{j\in J}R\#\Gamma)d^{(\gamma)} . Lemma 6.10 . Let γ ∈ A \gamma\in A . Then P ( γ ) ≅ P ⁡ ( − γ ) ​ # ​ Γ P^{(\gamma)}\cong P(-\gamma)\#\Gamma as R ​ # ​ Γ R\#\Gamma -modules. Proof. See the proof of Lemma 6.5 . ∎ 7. The graded 𝒱 \mathcal{V} -monoid of a graded Bergman algebra 7.1. Basic definitions and results Let R R be a not necessarily unital ring. Recall that a R R -module M M is called unital if R ​ M = M RM=M . We denote by R R - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 \operatorname{{\bf Mod}} the category of unital R R -modules. Furthermore, we denote by R R - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 proj \operatorname{{\bf Mod}}_{\operatorname{proj}} the full subcategory of R R - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 \operatorname{{\bf Mod}} whose objects are the projective objects of R R - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 \operatorname{{\bf Mod}} that are finitely generated as a R R -module. If R R has local units, we define 𝒱 ⁡ ( R ) = { [ P ] ∣ P ∈ R ​ - ​ 𝐌𝐨𝐝 proj } \mathcal{V}(R)=\{[P]\mid P\in R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Mod}}_{\operatorname{proj}}\} where [ P ] [P] denotes the isomorphism class of P P as a R R -module. 𝒱 ⁡ ( R ) \mathcal{V}(R) becomes an abelian monoid by defining [ P ] + [ Q ] = [ P ⊕ Q ] [P]+[Q]=[P\oplus Q] . It is well-known that 𝒱 \mathcal{V} is a functor that commutes with direct limits. There is a different, but equivalent definition of 𝒱 \mathcal{V} using idempotent matrices over R R , cf. [ 3 , Subsection 4A] . Let R R now be a Γ \Gamma -graded ring. Recall that a R R -module M M is called Γ \Gamma -graded if there is a decomposition M = ⨁ γ ∈ Γ M γ M=\bigoplus_{\gamma\in\Gamma}M_{\gamma} such that R α ​ M γ ⊆ M α ​ γ R_{\alpha}M_{\gamma}\subseteq M_{\alpha\gamma} for any α , γ ∈ Γ \alpha,\gamma\in\Gamma . We denote by R R - 𝐆𝐫 \operatorname{{\bf Gr}} the category of Γ \Gamma -graded unital R R -modules with morphisms the R R -module homomorphisms that preserve grading. Moreover, we denote by R R - 𝐆𝐫 proj \operatorname{{\bf Gr}}_{\operatorname{proj}} the full subcategory of R R - 𝐆𝐫 \operatorname{{\bf Gr}} whose objects are the projective objects of R R - 𝐆𝐫 \operatorname{{\bf Gr}} that are finitely generated as a R R -module. If R R has graded local units, we define 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) = { [ P ] ∣ P ∈ R ​ - ​ 𝐆𝐫 proj } \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)=\{[P]\mid P\in R\text{-}\operatorname{{\bf Gr}}_{\operatorname{proj}}\} where [ P ] [P] denotes the isomorphism class of P P as a graded R R -module. 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R) becomes a Γ \Gamma -monoid by defining [ P ] + [ Q ] = [ P ⊕ Q ] [P]+[Q]=[P\oplus Q] and γ . [ P ] = [ P ⁡ ( γ ) ] \gamma.[P]=[P(\gamma)] . Proposition 7.1 ( [ 24 , Proposition 66] ) . Let R R be a Γ \Gamma -graded ring with graded local units. Then R R - 𝐆𝐫 proj ≅ R ​ # ​ Γ \operatorname{{\bf Gr}}_{\operatorname{proj}}\cong R\#\Gamma - 𝐌𝐨𝐝 proj \operatorname{{\bf Mod}}_{\operatorname{proj}} by an isomorphism that commutes with direct sums. It follows that 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) ≅ 𝒱 ⁡ ( R ​ # ​ Γ ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)\cong\mathcal{V}(R\#\Gamma) . Let R R be a unital ring and ϵ ∈ 𝕄 n × n ⁡ ( R ) \epsilon\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n\times n}(R) an idempotent matrix. Then ϵ \epsilon defines an endomorphism of the R R -module R n R^{n} , which we also denote by ϵ \epsilon . Clearly the image M ⁡ ( ϵ ) = Im ⁡ ϵ M(\epsilon)=\operatorname{Im}\epsilon is a finitely generated projective R R -module (since R n = Ker ⁡ ϵ ⊕ Im ⁡ ϵ R^{n}=\operatorname{Ker}\epsilon\oplus\operatorname{Im}\epsilon ). Now let M M be a finitely generated projective R R -module. Then M ⊕ N = R n M\oplus N=R^{n} for some R R -module N N and n ∈ ℕ n\in\mathbb{N} . Define an endomorphism ϵ \epsilon of R n R^{n} by ϵ | M = id M \epsilon|_{M}=\operatorname{id}_{M} and ϵ | N = 0 \epsilon|_{N}=0 . Then clearly M = Im ⁡ ϵ M=\operatorname{Im}\epsilon and ϵ \epsilon is ide