atrix ϵ ∈ 𝕄 n × n ⁡ ( R ) \epsilon\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n\times n}(R) such that deg ⁡ ( ϵ i ​ j ) = γ j − γ i ​ ( 1 ≤ i , j ≤ n ) \deg(\epsilon_{ij})=\gamma_{j}-\gamma_{i}~(1\leq i,j\leq n) where γ 1 , … , γ n ∈ Γ \gamma_{1},\dots,\gamma_{n}\in\Gamma . Lemma 7.2 . Let R R be a Γ \Gamma -graded ring and ϵ ∈ 𝕄 n × n ⁡ ( R ) \epsilon\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n\times n}(R) an idempotent matrix with homogeneous entries such deg ⁡ ( ϵ i ​ j ) = γ j − γ i ​ ( 1 ≤ i , j ≤ n ) \deg(\epsilon_{ij})=\gamma_{j}-\gamma_{i}~(1\leq i,j\leq n) where γ 1 , … , γ n ∈ Γ \gamma_{1},\dots,\gamma_{n}\in\Gamma . Then M ⁡ ( ϵ ) ​ # ​ Γ ≅ M ⁡ ( ϵ ​ # ​ Γ ) M(\epsilon)\#\Gamma\cong M(\epsilon\#\Gamma) , where ϵ ​ # ​ Γ ∈ 𝕄 n × n ⁡ ( R ) \epsilon\#\Gamma\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n\times n}(R) is defined by ( ϵ ​ # ​ Γ ) i ​ j = ϵ i ​ j ​ p − γ j ​ ( 1 ≤ i , j ≤ n ) (\epsilon\#\Gamma)_{ij}=\epsilon_{ij}p_{-\gamma_{j}}~(1\leq i,j\leq n) . Proof. See the proof of Lemma 6.5 . ∎ Lemma 7.3 . Let ϕ : R → S \phi:R\to S be a ring homomorphism and ϵ ∈ 𝕄 n × n ⁡ ( R ) \epsilon\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n\times n}(R) an idempotent matrix. Then S ⊗ R M ⁡ ( ϵ ) ≅ M ⁡ ( ϕ ⁡ ( ϵ ) ) S\otimes_{R}M(\epsilon)\cong M(\phi(\epsilon)) , where ϕ ⁡ ( ϵ ) ∈ 𝕄 n × n ⁡ ( S ) \phi(\epsilon)\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n\times n}(S) is obtained from ϵ \epsilon by applying ϕ \phi to each entry. Proof. Recall that as an abelian monoid, S ⊗ R M ⁡ ( ϵ ) S\otimes_{R}M(\epsilon) has the presentation ⟨ s ⊗ m ⁡ ( s ∈ S , m ∈ M ⁡ ( ϵ ) ) ∣ ( s 1 + s 2 ) ⊗ m = s 1 ⊗ m + s 2 ⊗ m ⁡ ( s 1 , s 2 ∈ S , m ∈ M ⁡ ( ϵ ) ) , s ⊗ ( m 1 + m 2 ) = s ⊗ m 1 + s ⊗ m 2 ​ ( s ∈ S , m 1 , m 2 ∈ M ⁡ ( ϵ ) ) , s . r ⊗ m = s ⊗ r . m ( s ∈ S ; r ∈ R ; m ∈ M ( ϵ ) ) ⟩ . \begin{split}\Big\langle s\otimes m~(s\in S,m\in M(\epsilon))\mid{}&(s_{1}+s_{2})\otimes m=s_{1}\otimes m+s_{2}\otimes m~(s_{1},s_{2}\in S;~m\in M(\epsilon)),\\ &s\otimes(m_{1}+m_{2})=s\otimes m_{1}+s\otimes m_{2}~(s\in S;~m_{1},m_{2}\in M(\epsilon)),\\ &s.r\otimes m=s\otimes r.m~(s\in S;~r\in R;~m\in M(\epsilon))\Big\rangle.\end{split} (7.1) Clearly for any k , l ∈ ℕ k,l\in\mathbb{N} , ϕ \phi induces a map 𝕄 k × l ⁡ ( R ) → 𝕄 k × l ⁡ ( S ) \operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{k\times l}(R)\to\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{k\times l}(S) , which we also denote by ϕ \phi . We define a monoid homomorphism θ \theta from the free abelian monoid ⟨ s ⊗ m ⁡ ( s ∈ S , m ∈ M ⁡ ( ϵ ) ) ⟩ \big\langle s\otimes m~(s\in S,m\in M(\epsilon))\big\rangle to the abelian monoid M ⁡ ( ϕ ⁡ ( ϵ ) ) M(\phi(\epsilon)) by θ ⁡ ( s ⊗ m ) = s ​ ϕ ​ ( m ) \theta(s\otimes m)=s\phi(m) for any s ∈ S s\in S and m ∈ M ⁡ ( ϵ ) m\in M(\epsilon) . Note that if m ∈ M ⁡ ( ϵ ) m\in M(\epsilon) , then m = x ​ ϵ m=x\epsilon for some x ∈ R n x\in R^{n} and hence ϕ ⁡ ( m ) = ϕ ⁡ ( x ) ​ ϕ ​ ( ϵ ) ∈ M ⁡ ( ϕ ⁡ ( ϵ ) ) \phi(m)=\phi(x)\phi(\epsilon)\in M(\phi(\epsilon)) . One checks routinely that θ \theta preserves the defining relations in presentation ( 7.1 ). Hence θ \theta induces a monoid homomorphism η : S ⊗ R M ⁡ ( ϵ ) → M ⁡ ( ϕ ⁡ ( ϵ ) ) \eta:S\otimes_{R}M(\epsilon)\to M(\phi(\epsilon)) . First we show that η \eta surjective. For any 1 ≤ j ≤ n 1\leq j\leq n , let α j \alpha_{j} be the element of R n R^{n} whose j j -th component is 1 1 and whose other components are 0 0 . Define β 1 , … , β n ∈ S n \beta_{1},\dots,\beta_{n}\in S^{n} similarly. Let now m ∈ M ⁡ ( ϕ ⁡ ( ϵ ) ) m\in M(\phi(\epsilon)) . Then there is a y ∈ S n y\in S^{n} such that m = y ​ ϕ ​ ( ϵ ) m=y\phi(\epsilon) . It follows that m = y ​ ϕ ​ ( ϵ ) = ∑ k = 1 n y k ​ β k ​ ϕ ​ ( ϵ ) = ∑ k = 1 n η ⁡ ( y k ⊗ α k ​ ϵ ) m=y\phi(\epsilon)=\sum_{k=1}^{n}y_{k}\beta_{k}\phi(\epsilon)=\sum_{k=1}^{n}\eta(y_{k}\otimes\alpha_{k}\epsilon) . Hence η \eta is surjective. Next we show that η \eta is injective. Suppose that η ⁡ ( s ⊗ m ) = η ⁡ ( s ′ ⊗ m ′ ) \eta(s\otimes m)=\eta(s^{\prime}\otimes m^{\prime}) . Then s ​ ϕ ​ ( m ) = s ′ ​ ϕ ​ ( m ′ ) s\phi(m)=s^{\prime}\phi(m^{\prime}) in S n S^{n} , i.e. s ​ ϕ ​ ( m k ) = s ′ ​ ϕ ​ ( m k ′ ) ​ ( 1 ≤ k ≤ n ) s\phi(m_{k})=s^{\prime}\phi(m^{\prime}_{k})~(1\leq k\leq n) in S S . It follows that s ⊗ m = s ⊗ ∑ k = 1 n m k ​ α k = ∑ k = 1 n s ⊗ m k ​ α k = ∑ k = 1 n s ​ ϕ ​ ( m k ) ⊗ α k = = ∑ k = 1 n s ′ ​ ϕ ​ ( m k ′ ) ⊗ α k = ∑ k = 1 n s ′ ⊗ m k ′ ​ α k = s ′ ⊗ ∑ k = 1 n m k ′ ​ α k ​ s ′ ⊗ m ′ . s\otimes m=s\otimes\sum_{k=1}^{n}m_{k}\alpha_{k}=\sum_{k=1}^{n}s\otimes m_{k}\alpha_{k}=\sum_{k=1}^{n}s\phi(m_{k})\otimes\alpha_{k}=\\ =\sum_{k=1}^{n}s^{\prime}\phi(m^{\prime}_{k})\otimes\alpha_{k}=\sum_{k=1}^{n}s^{\prime}\otimes m^{\prime}_{k}\alpha_{k}=s^{\prime}\otimes\sum_{k=1}^{n}m^{\prime}_{k}\alpha_{k}s^{\prime}\otimes m^{\prime}. Hence η \eta is injective. We leave it to the reader to show that η \eta preserves the action of S S . ∎ Lemma 7.4 . Let ϕ : R → S \phi:R\to S be a homomorphism of Γ \Gamma -graded rings and ϵ ∈ 𝕄 n × n ⁡ ( R ) \epsilon\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n\times n}(R) an idempotent matrix with homogeneous entries such deg ⁡ ( ϵ i ​ j ) = γ j − γ i ​ ( 1 ≤ i , j ≤ n ) \deg(\epsilon_{ij})=\gamma_{j}-\gamma_{i}~(1\leq i,j\leq n) where γ 1 , … , γ n ∈ Γ \gamma_{1},\dots,\gamma_{n}\in\Gamma . Then S ⊗ R M ⁡ ( ϵ ) ≅ M ⁡ ( ϕ ⁡ ( ϵ ) ) S\otimes_{R}M(\epsilon)\cong M(\phi(\epsilon)) as graded S S -modules, where ϕ ⁡ ( ϵ ) ∈ 𝕄 n × n ⁡ ( S ) \phi(\epsilon)\in\operatorname{\mathbb{M}}_{n\times n}(S) is obtained from ϵ \epsilon by applying ϕ \phi to each entry. Proof. The proof of the previous lemma shows that there is an isomorphism η : S ⊗ R M ⁡ ( ϵ ) → M ⁡ ( ϕ ⁡ ( ϵ ) ) \eta:S\otimes_{R}M(\epsilon)\to M(\phi(\epsilon)) of S S -modules. We leave it to the reader to check that η \eta preserves the grading. ∎ 7.2. The graded 𝒱 \mathcal{V} -monoid of a graded Bergman algebra I Let R R be a Γ \Gamma -graded k k -algebra where Γ \Gamma is an abelian group and 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a graded field concentrated in degree zero. Moreover, let P P and Q Q be non-zero graded finitely generated projective R R -modules. Set S := R ⟨ h , h − 1 : P ¯ ≅ Q ¯ ⟩ . S:=R\bi