}}(R)/\big([P(\gamma)]=[Q(\gamma)]~(\gamma\in\Gamma)\big)=\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)/\big([P]=[Q]\big) . ∎ Example 7.7 . Consider the Leavitt algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k) . As already observed by Bergman [ 7 ] , L 𝗄 ( n , n + k ) ≅ 𝗄 ⟨ h , h − 1 : 𝗄 n ¯ ≅ 𝗄 n + k ¯ ⟩ . L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)\cong\mathsf{k}\langle h,h^{-1}:\overline{\mathsf{k}^{n}}\cong\overline{\mathsf{k}^{n+k}}\rangle. Then by [ 7 , Theorem 5.2] 𝒱 ⁡ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) ) ≅ 𝒱 ⁡ ( 𝗄 ) / ⟨ [ 𝗄 n ] = [ 𝗄 n + k ] ⟩ ≅ M n , n + k . \mathcal{V}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k))\cong\mathcal{V}(\mathsf{k})/\langle[\mathsf{k}^{n}]=[\mathsf{k}^{n+k}]\rangle\cong M_{n,n+k}. Here, for n , k ∈ ℕ + n,k\in\mathbb{N}^{+} , M n , n + k M_{n,n+k} is the finite commutative monoid M n , n + k := { 0 , x , 2 ​ x , … , n ​ x , … , ( n + k − 1 ) ​ x } , with relation ​ ( n + k ) ​ x = n ​ x . M_{n,n+k}:=\big\{0,x,2x,\dots,nx,\dots,(n+k-1)x\big\},\ \ \ \ \mbox{with relation}\ (n+k)x=nx. Returning to the graded setting, we have L 𝗄 ( n , n + k ) ≅ gr 𝗄 ⟨ h , h − 1 : 𝗄 n ¯ ≅ gr 𝗄 n + k ​ ( − 1 ) ¯ ⟩ , L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}\mathsf{k}\langle h,h^{-1}:\overline{\mathsf{k}^{n}}\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}\overline{\mathsf{k}^{n+k}(-1)}\rangle, and by Corollary 7.6 𝒱 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) ) ≅ 𝒱 gr ​ ( 𝗄 ) / ⟨ [ 𝗄 n ] = [ 𝗄 n + k ​ ( − 1 ) ] ⟩ ≅ ⟨ 1 , ( n / n + k ) i , i ∈ ℤ ⟩ ⊆ ℚ . \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k))\cong\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(\mathsf{k})/\langle[\mathsf{k}^{n}]=[\mathsf{k}^{n+k}(-1)]\rangle\cong\big\langle 1,(n/n+k)^{i},i\in\mathbb{Z}\big\rangle\subseteq\mathbb{Q}. Since [ L 𝗄 ( n , n + k ) ) ] [L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k))] represents 1 1 in this monoid, which is then a strongly order unit, by Proposition 2.6 , we conclude that L 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k) is a strongly graded ring. (This can also be obtained from looking at the generators and relations of the algebra.) Therefore, an application of Dade’s theorem, imply that 𝒱 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) ) ≅ 𝒱 ⁡ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) 0 ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k))\cong\mathcal{V}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)_{0}) , which in turn implies that the zero component ring L 𝗄 ​ ( n , n + k ) 0 L_{\mathsf{k}}(n,n+k)_{0} has IBN. 7.3. The graded 𝒱 \mathcal{V} -monoid of a graded Bergman algebra II Let R R be a Γ \Gamma -graded k k -algebra where Γ \Gamma is an abelian group and k k a field concentrated in degree zero. Moreover, let P P be a non-zero graded finitely generated projective R R -modules. Set S := R ⟨ e : P ¯ → P ¯ ; e 2 = e ⟩ . S:=R\big\langle e:\overline{P}\to\overline{P};~e^{2}=e\big\rangle. Theorem 7.8 . There is a monoid isomorphism 𝒱 gr ( S ) ≅ 𝒱 gr ( R ) ⟨ [ P 1 ( γ ) ] , [ P 2 ( γ ) ] ( γ ∈ A ) ∣ [ P ( γ ) ] = [ P 1 ( γ ) ] + [ P 2 ( γ ) ] ( γ ∈ Γ ) ⟩ , \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(S)\cong\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)\Big\langle[P_{1}^{(\gamma)}],[P_{2}^{(\gamma)}]~(\gamma\in A)\mid[P(\gamma)]=[P_{1}^{(\gamma)}]+[P_{2}^{(\gamma)}]~(\gamma\in\Gamma)\Big\rangle, given by tensoring. Proof. It follows from Proposition 7.1 , Theorem 6.9 , [ 7 , Theorem 5.1] and 6.10 that 𝒱 gr ​ ( S ) ≅ \displaystyle\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(S)\cong~ 𝒱 ⁡ ( S ​ # ​ Γ ) \displaystyle\mathcal{V}(S\#\Gamma) ≅ \displaystyle\cong~ 𝒱 ( lim → R # Γ A ⟨ f ( γ ) : P A ( γ ) ¯ → P A ( γ ) ¯ ; ( f ( γ ) ) 2 = f ( γ ) ( γ ∈ A ) ⟩ ) \displaystyle\mathcal{V}(\varinjlim R\#\Gamma_{A}\Big\langle f^{(\gamma)}:\overline{P_{A}^{(\gamma)}}\to\overline{P_{A}^{(\gamma)}};~(f^{(\gamma)})^{2}=f^{(\gamma)}~(\gamma\in A)\Big\rangle) ≅ \displaystyle\cong~ lim → 𝒱 ( R # Γ A ⟨ f ( γ ) : P A ( γ ) ¯ → P A ( γ ) ¯ ; ( f ( γ ) ) 2 = f ( γ ) ( γ ∈ A ) ⟩ ) \displaystyle\varinjlim\mathcal{V}(R\#\Gamma_{A}\Big\langle f^{(\gamma)}:\overline{P_{A}^{(\gamma)}}\to\overline{P_{A}^{(\gamma)}};~(f^{(\gamma)})^{2}=f^{(\gamma)}~(\gamma\in A)\Big\rangle) ≅ \displaystyle\cong~ lim → 𝒱 ( R # Γ A ) ⟨ [ P 1 ( γ ) ] , [ P 2 ( γ ) ] ( γ ∈ A ) ∣ [ P A ( γ ) ] = [ P 1 ( γ ) ] + [ P 2 ( γ ) ] ( γ ∈ A ) ⟩ \displaystyle\varinjlim\mathcal{V}(R\#\Gamma_{A})\big\langle[P_{1}^{(\gamma)}],[P_{2}^{(\gamma)}]~(\gamma\in A)\mid[P_{A}^{(\gamma)}]=[P_{1}^{(\gamma)}]+[P_{2}^{(\gamma)}]~(\gamma\in A)\big\rangle ≅ \displaystyle\cong~ 𝒱 ( R # Γ ) ⟨ [ P 1 ( γ ) ] , [ P 2 ( γ ) ] ( γ ∈ Γ ) ∣ [ P ( γ ) ] = [ P 1 ( γ ) ] + [ P 2 ( γ ) ] ( γ ∈ Γ ) ⟩ \displaystyle\mathcal{V}(R\#\Gamma)\big\langle[P_{1}^{(\gamma)}],[P_{2}^{(\gamma)}]~(\gamma\in\Gamma)\mid[P^{(\gamma)}]=[P_{1}^{(\gamma)}]+[P_{2}^{(\gamma)}]~(\gamma\in\Gamma)\big\rangle ≅ \displaystyle\cong~ 𝒱 ( R # Γ ) ⟨ [ P 1 ( γ ) ] , [ P 2 ( γ ) ] ( γ ∈ Γ ) ∣ [ P ( γ ) # Γ ] = [ P 1 ( γ ) ] + [ P 2 ( γ ) ] ( γ ∈ Γ ) ⟩ \displaystyle\mathcal{V}(R\#\Gamma)\big\langle[P_{1}^{(\gamma)}],[P_{2}^{(\gamma)}]~(\gamma\in\Gamma)\mid[P(\gamma)\#\Gamma]=[P_{1}^{(\gamma)}]+[P_{2}^{(\gamma)}]~(\gamma\in\Gamma)\big\rangle ≅ \displaystyle\cong~ 𝒱 gr ( R ) ⟨ [ P 1 ( γ ) ] , [ P 2 ( γ ) ] ( γ ∈ Γ ) ∣ [ P ( γ ) ] = [ P 1 ( γ ) ] + [ P 2 ( γ ) ] ( γ ∈ Γ ) ⟩ . \displaystyle\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)\big\langle[P_{1}^{(\gamma)}],[P_{2}^{(\gamma)}]~(\gamma\in\Gamma)\mid[P(\gamma)]=[P_{1}^{(\gamma)}]+[P_{2}^{(\gamma)}]~(\gamma\in\Gamma)\big\rangle. It remains to show that the isomorphism 𝒱 gr ( R ) ⟨ [ P 1 ( γ ) ] , [ P 2 ( γ ) ] ( γ ∈ A ) ∣ [ P ( γ ) ] = [ P 1 ( γ ) ] + [ P 2 ( γ ) ] ( γ ∈ Γ ) ⟩ ≅ 𝒱 gr ( S ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)\big\langle[P_{1}^{(\gamma)}],[P_{2}^{(\gamma)}]~(\gamma\in A)\mid[P(\gamma)]=[P_{1}^{(\gamma)}]+[P_{2}^{(\gamma)}]~(\gamma\in\Gamma)\big\rangle\cong\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(S) is given by tensoring. But that follows from Lemmas 7.2 , 7.3 and 7.4 , see the proof of Theorem 7.5 . ∎ Corollary 7.9 . There is a Γ \Gamma -monoid isomorphism 𝒱 gr ( S ) ≅ 𝒱 gr ( R ) ⟨ [ P 1 ] , [ P 2 ] ∣ [ P ] = [ P 1 ] + [ P 2 ] ⟩ \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(S)\cong\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)\big\langle[P_{1}],[P_{2}]\mid[P]=[P_{1}]+[P_{2}]\big\rangle given by tensoring. Proof. See the proof of Corollary 7.6 . ∎ 8. Realisation of Γ \Gamma -monoids as non-stable K K -theory of graded rings Throughout this section, Γ \Gamma is an abelian group, 𝗄 \mathsf{k} is a Γ \Gamma -graded field concentrated in degree zero, and R R is a graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra. Recall from § 2.1 that if ( M , i ) (M,i) is a pointed Γ \Gamma -monoid with i ∈ M i\in M an Γ \Gamma -order unit, then i i is a strong order unit if for any m ∈ M m\in M , we have m ≤ k ​ i m\leq ki for some k ∈ ℕ k\in\mathbb{N} . Furthermore, i i is an invariant order unit if i γ = i {}^{\gamma}i=i for any γ ∈ Γ \gamma\in\Gamma . Theorem 8.1 . Let ( M , i ) (M,i) be a pointed conical Γ \Gamma -monoid with a distinguished order unit i i . Then there is a hereditary graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra R R such that ϕ : ( M , i ) ≅ ( 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) , [ R ] ) \phi:(M,i)\cong(\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R),[R]) as pointed Γ \Gamma -monoids. Furthermore, R R has a weak universal property: if S S is a Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra with Γ \Gamma -monoid homomorphism ψ : ( M , i ) → ( 𝒱 gr ​ ( S ) , [ S ] ) \psi:(M,i)\rightarrow(\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(S),[S]) , then there is a (nonunique) graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra homomorphism R → S R\rightarrow S such that the following diagram commutes. ( M , i ) \textstyle{(M,i)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} ϕ \scriptstyle{\phi} ψ \scriptstyle{\psi} ( 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) , [ R ] ) \textstyle{(\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R),[R])\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} − ⊗ R S \scriptstyle{-\otimes_{R}S} ( 𝒱 gr ​ ( S ) , [ S ] ) \textstyle{(\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(S),[S])} Furthermore, (1) R R is a strongly graded ring if and only if i i is a strong order unit. (2) R R is a crossed-product ring if and only if i i is an invariant order unit. Proof. Let X = { i , p ϕ , q ϕ ∣ ϕ ∈ Φ } X=\{i,p_{\phi},q_{\phi}\mid\phi\in\Phi\} be a set of non-zero generators for the Γ \Gamm