u_{\psi},v_{\psi}\neq 0 for any ψ ∈ Φ \psi\in\Phi . Let R ′ R^{\prime} be the Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra 𝗄 Φ \mathsf{k}^{\Phi} which is concentrated in degree 0 0 . Then 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ′ ) = ⟨ P ϕ ​ ( ϕ ∈ Φ ) ⟩ \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R^{\prime})=\big\langle P_{\phi}~(\phi\in\Phi)\rangle is freely generated by generators P ϕ ​ ( ϕ ∈ Φ ) P_{\phi}~(\phi\in\Phi) whose sum is [ R ′ ] [R^{\prime}] . Let Z Z be the set of all finite subsets of Ψ \Psi . Clearly Z Z is a directed set together with the inclusion relation. For any A ∈ Z A\in Z let M A = ⟨ X ∣ Y A ⟩ M_{A}=\langle X\mid Y_{A}\rangle where Y A = { u ψ = v ψ ∣ ψ ∈ B } Y_{A}=\{u_{\psi}=v_{\psi}\mid\psi\in B\} . If A ⊆ A ′ ∈ Z A\subseteq A^{\prime}\in Z , then clearly there is a canonical Γ \Gamma -monoid homomorphism α A A ′ : M A → M A ′ \alpha_{A}^{A^{\prime}}:M_{A}\to M_{A^{\prime}} . One checks easily that M M is the direct limit of the direct system { M A , α A A ′ ∣ A ⊆ A ′ ∈ Z } \{M_{A},\alpha_{A}^{A^{\prime}}\mid A\subseteq A^{\prime}\in Z\} in the category of Γ \Gamma -monoids. Now fix an A ∈ Z A\in Z . Let R A R_{A} be the Γ \Gamma -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra obtained from R ′ R^{\prime} by adjoining isomorphisms U ψ ¯ ≅ V ψ ¯ ​ ( ψ ∈ A ) \overline{U_{\psi}}\cong\overline{V_{\psi}}~(\psi\in A) where U ψ U_{\psi} and V ψ V_{\psi} are the graded R ′ R^{\prime} -modules corresponding to u ψ u_{\psi} and v ψ v_{\psi} , respectively. Then 𝒱 gr ​ ( R A ) ≅ M A \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R_{A})\cong M_{A} by Corollary 7.6 , and this isomorphism maps [ R A ] [R_{A}] to i i . If A ⊆ A ′ ∈ Z A\subseteq A^{\prime}\in Z , then clearly there is a canonical graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra homomorphism β A A ′ : R A → R A ′ \beta_{A}^{A^{\prime}}:R_{A}\to R_{A^{\prime}} . Let R R be the direct limit of the direct system { R A , β A A ′ ∣ A ⊆ A ′ ∈ Z } \{R_{A},\beta_{A}^{A^{\prime}}\mid A\subseteq A^{\prime}\in Z\} in the category of graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebras. Since 𝒱 gr \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}} commutes with direct limits, we obtain 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) = 𝒱 gr ​ ( lim → ⁡ R A ) = lim → ⁡ 𝒱 gr ​ ( R A ) ≅ lim → ⁡ M A = M . \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)=\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(\varinjlim R_{A})=\varinjlim\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R_{A})\cong\varinjlim M_{A}=M. Clearly the isomorphism 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) ≅ M \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)\cong M maps [ R ] [R] to i i . By Lemma 4.10 , R R is graded isomorphic to a unital hyper Leavitt path algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( H ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(H) whose grading is induced by a weight map. That R R is hereditary and has a weak universal property follow as in the proof of Theorem 8.1 . ∎ 9. Application to graph algebras The Graded Classification Conjecture for Leavitt path algebras states that the graded Grothendieck group, K 0 gr K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0} , is a complete invariant for these algebras ( [ 13 , 15 ] , [ 1 , §7.3.4] ): Conjecture 9.1 . Let E E and F F be finite graphs. (1) For any order preserving ℤ ⁡ [ x , x − 1 ] \mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}] -module homomorphism ϕ : K 0 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ) → K 0 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( F ) ) \phi:K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)) with ϕ ⁡ ( [ L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ] ) = L 𝗄 ​ ( F ) \phi([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)])=L_{\mathsf{k}}(F) , there exists a unital ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -homomorphism ψ : L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) → L 𝗄 ​ ( F ) \psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(F) such that K 0 gr ​ ( ψ ) = ϕ K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi . (2) For any order preserving ℤ ⁡ [ x , x − 1 ] \mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}] -module isomorphism ϕ : K 0 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ) → K 0 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( F ) ) \phi:K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)) with ϕ ⁡ ( [ L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ] = [ L 𝗄 ​ ( F ) ] CLOSE \phi([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)]=[L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)] , there exists a unital ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -isomorphism ψ : L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) → L 𝗄 ​ ( F ) \psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(F) such that K 0 gr ​ ( ψ ) = ϕ K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi . Here the order preserving ℤ ⁡ [ x , x − 1 ] \mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}] -module isomorphism K 0 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ) ≅ K 0 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( F ) ) K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\cong K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)) should give that these algebras are graded Morita equivalent (see Conjecture 9.4 ). Since the graded K K -theory of Leavitt path algebras coincides with equivariant K K -theory of graph C ∗ C^{*} -algebras one can extend the conjecture to the setting of C ∗ C^{*} -algebras. Denote by γ E \gamma_{E} the gauge circle actions on C ∗ ​ ( E ) C^{*}(E) and K 0 𝕋 ​ ( C ∗ ​ ( E ) ) K^{\mathbb{T}}_{0}(C^{*}(E)) the equivariant K K -theory of C ∗ ​ ( E ) C^{*}(E) [ 23 ] . There are canonical order preserving isomorphisms of ℤ ⁡ [ x , x − 1 ] \mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}] -modules (see [ 15 , p. 275] , [ 16 ] and [ 14 , Proof of Theorem A] ). K 0 gr ​ ( L ⁡ ( E ) ) ≅ K 0 ​ ( L ⁡ ( E × ℤ ) ) ≅ K 0 ​ ( C ∗ ​ ( E × ℤ ) ) ≅ K 0 𝕋 ​ ( C ∗ ​ ( E ) ) . K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L(E))\cong K_{0}(L(E\times\mathbb{Z}))\cong K_{0}(C^{*}(E\times\mathbb{Z}))\cong K_{0}^{\mathbb{T}}(C^{*}(E)). (9.1) Thus one can pose the analytic version of Conjecture 9.1 as follows. Conjecture 9.2 . Let E E and F F be finite graphs. Then there is an order preserving ℤ ⁡ [ x , x − 1 ] \mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}] -module isomorphism ϕ : K 0 𝕋 ​ ( C ∗ ​ ( E ) ) ⟶ K 0 𝕋 ​ ( C ∗ ​ ( F ) ) , \phi:K^{\mathbb{T}}_{0}(C^{*}(E))\longrightarrow K_{0}^{\mathbb{T}}(C^{*}(F)), with ϕ ⁡ ( [ C ∗ ​ ( E ) ] ) = [ C ∗ ​ ( F ) ] \phi([C^{*}(E)])=[C^{*}(F)] if and only if C ∗ ​ ( E ) ≅ C ∗ ​ ( F ) C^{*}(E)\cong C^{*}(F) which respect the gauge action. In fact in Conjecture 9.1 if the graded Grothendieck groups are isomorphic, one should have that the isomorphism between the Leavitt path algebras is indeed a ∗ * -isomorphism. If this is the case, then Conjecture 9.1 implies Conjecture 9.2 . For, if K 0 𝕋 ​ ( C ∗ ​ ( E ) ) ≅ K 0 𝕋 ​ ( C ∗ ​ ( F ) ) K^{\mathbb{T}}_{0}(C^{*}(E))\cong K_{0}^{\mathbb{T}}(C^{*}(F)) , then by ( 9.1 ), K 0 gr ​ ( L ℂ ​ ( E ) ) ≅ K 0 gr ​ ( L ℂ ​ ( F ) ) K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathbb{C}}(E))\cong K_{0}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathbb{C}}(F)) , so L ℂ ( E ) ≅ gr L ℂ ( F ) L_{\mathbb{C}}(E)\cong_{\operatorname{gr}}L_{\mathbb{C}}(F) via a ∗ * -isomorphism. This implies that C ∗ ​ ( E ) ≅ C ∗ ​ ( F ) C^{*}(E)\cong C^{*}(F) which respects the gauge action (see [ 2 , Theorem 4.4] ). The notion of talented monoids allows us to write the classification conjectures for Leavitt and C ∗ C^{*} -algebras in a unified manner. The positive cone of the graded Grothendieck group of a Leavitt path algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(E) can be described purely based on the underlying graph, via the so-called talented monoid T E T_{E} of E E [ 18 ] . The benefit of talented monoids is that they give us control over elements of the monoids (such as minimal elements, atoms, etc.) and consequently on the “geometry” of the graphs such as the number of cycles, their lengths, etc. (see [ 18 , 12 ] ). Definition 9.3 . Let E E be a row-finite directed graph. The talented monoid of E E , denoted T E T_{E} , is the commutative monoid generated by { v ( i ) ∣ v ∈ E 0 , i ∈ ℤ } \{v(i)\mid v\in E^{0},i\in\mathbb{Z}\} , subject to v ⁡ ( i ) = ∑ e ∈ s − 1 ​ ( v ) r ⁡ ( e ) ​ ( i + 1 ) v(i)=\sum_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}r(e)(i+1) for every i ∈ ℤ i\in\mathbb{Z} and every v ∈ E 0 v\in E^{0} that is not a sink. The additive group ℤ \mathbb{Z} of integers acts on T E T_{E} via monoid automorphisms by shifting indices: For each n , i ∈ ℤ n,i\in\mathbb{Z} and v ∈ E 0 v\in E^{0} , define v n ​ ( i ) = v ⁡ ( i + n ) {}^{n}v(i)=v(i+n) , which extends to an action of ℤ \mathbb{Z} on T E T_{E} . Throughout we will denote elements v ⁡ ( 0 ) v(0) in T E T_{E} by v v . The crucial ingredient for us is the action of ℤ \mathbb{Z} on the monoid T E T_{E} . The general idea is that the monoid structure of T E T_{E} along with the action of ℤ \mathbb{Z} resemble the graded ring structure of a Leavitt path algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(E) . Thus the conjectures above roughly state that isomorphism of talented monoids can lift to the isomorphisms of graded and equivariant K K -theory of respected Leavitt and graph C ∗ C^{*} -algebras. We therefore can formulate both conjectures as follows: Conjecture 9.4 . Let E E and F F be finite graphs and 𝗄 \mathsf{k} a field. Then the following are equivalent. (1) The talented monoids T E T_{E} and T F T_{F} are ℤ \mathbb{Z} -isomorphic; (2) The Leavitt path algebras L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(E) and L 𝗄 ​ ( F ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(F) are graded Morita equivalent; (3) The graph C ∗ C^{*} -algebras C ∗ ​ ( E ) C^{*}(E) and C ∗ ​ ( F ) C^{*}(F) are equivariant Morita equivalent. Furthermore, the following are equivalent. (1) The talented monoids T E T_{E} and T F T_{F} are pointed ℤ \mathbb{Z} -isomorphic; (2) The Leavitt path algebras L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(E) and L 𝗄 ​ ( F ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(F) are graded isomorphic; (3) The graph C ∗ C^{*} -algebras C ∗ ​ ( E ) C^{*}(E) and C ∗ ​ ( F ) C^{*}(F) are equivariant isomorophic. G. Arnone in [ 6 ] and L. Vas in [ 27 ] answered part (2) of Conjecture 9.1 , independently and with different approaches, in positive. In fact Arnone shows that the lifting map can be diagonal preserving graded ∗ * -homomorphism and Vas shows that the graphs can consist of infinite edges. Using theory developed here, we can in fact show that for any ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra A A and any pointed ℤ \mathbb{Z} -homomorphism ϕ : 𝒱 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ) → 𝒱 gr ​ ( A ) \phi:\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A) , we can lift ϕ \phi to a graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra homomorphism ψ : L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) → A \psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow A , which preserves the injectivity. Theorem 9.5 . Let E E be a finite graph and let A A be a ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra. Let ϕ : T E → 𝒱 0 gr ​ ( A ) \phi:T_{E}\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(A) be a ℤ \mathbb{Z} -monoid homomorphism with ϕ ⁡ ( 1 E ) = [ A ] \phi(1_{E})=[A] . Then there exists a unital ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra homomorphism ψ : L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) → A \psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow A such that 𝒱 0 gr ​ ( ψ ) = ϕ \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi . Furthermore, if ϕ ⁡ ( v ) ≠ 0 \phi(v)\not=0 , for all v ∈ E 0 v\in E^{0} , then ψ \psi is monomorphism. Proof. Consider the semisimple 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra R = ∏ E 0 𝗄 R=\prod_{E^{0}}\mathsf{k} , the product of | E 0 | |E^{0}| -copies of the field 𝗄 \mathsf{k} . Then 𝒱 gr ( R ) ≅ ⟨ v ( i ) ∣ v ∈ E 0 , i ∈ ℤ ⟩ , \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)\cong\big\langle v(i)\mid v\in E^{0},\,i\in\mathbb{Z}\big\rangle, (9.2) with the ℤ \mathbb{Z} -action v n ​ ( i ) = v ⁡ ( i + n ) {}^{n}v(i)=v(i+n) , for n , i ∈ ℤ n,i\in\mathbb{Z} . Denote p v ​ ( i ) p_{v}(i) , where v ∈ E 0 v\in E^{0} and i ∈ ℤ i\in\mathbb{Z} , the graded finitely generated projective R R -module with 𝗄 ⁡ ( i ) \mathsf{k}(i) appears in v v -th component of R R and zero elsewhere. Throughout, we write p v p_{v} for p v ​ ( 0 ) p_{v}(0) . Note that ⨁ v ∈ E 0 p v ≅ R \bigoplus_{v\in E^{0}}p_{v}\cong R as graded R R -module and the isomorphism classes [ p v ​ ( i ) ] [p_{v}(i)] correspond to v ⁡ ( i ) v(i) in ( 9.2 ). Next we consider the pairs of graded finitely generated projective R R -modules p v p_{v} and ⨁ e ∈ s − 1 ​ ( v ) p r ⁡ ( e ) ​ ( 1 ) \bigoplus_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}p_{r(e)}(1) , for v ∈ E 0 v\in E^{0} that are not sinks. We construct the graded ring S := R ⟨ h v , h v − 1 : p v ¯ ≅ ⨁ e ∈ s − 1 ​ ( v ) p r ⁡ ( e ) ​ ( 1 ) ¯ ⟩ , S:=R\big\langle h_{v},h_{v}^{-1}:\overline{p_{v}}\cong\overline{\bigoplus_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}p_{r(e)}(1)}\big\rangle, (9.3) for vertices v v which are not sink. This amounts to adjoining matrices to R R with relations which define the 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(E) (see Theorem 5.15 ),