we obtain the following commutative diagram of ℤ \mathbb{Z} -monoids: 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) \textstyle{\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} A ⊗ R − \scriptstyle{A\otimes_{R}-} 𝒱 gr ​ ( A ) \textstyle{\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A)} T E \textstyle{T_{E}\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} ϕ \scriptstyle{\phi} Thus [ q v ] = ϕ ⁡ ( v ) = ϕ ⁡ ( ∑ e ∈ s − 1 ​ ( v ) r ⁡ ( e ) ​ ( i + 1 ) ) = ∑ e ∈ s − 1 ​ ( v ) [ q r ⁡ ( e ) ​ ( i + 1 ) ] , [q_{v}]=\phi(v)=\phi\big(\sum_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}r(e)(i+1)\big)=\sum_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}[q_{r(e)}(i+1)], implying graded A A -module isomorphisms q v \displaystyle q_{v} ≅ ⨁ e ∈ s − 1 ​ ( v ) q r ⁡ ( e ) ​ ( 1 ) \displaystyle\cong\bigoplus_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}q_{r(e)}(1) A ⊗ R p v \displaystyle A\otimes_{R}p_{v} ≅ A ⊗ R ( ⨁ e ∈ s − 1 ​ ( v ) p r ⁡ ( e ) ​ ( 1 ) ) , \displaystyle\cong A\otimes_{R}\big(\bigoplus_{e\in s^{-1}(v)}p_{r(e)}(1)\big), for vertices v v which are not sink. Since L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(E) is the universal ring providing these isomoprhisms (see 9.3 and 9.4 ), it follows that there is a graded 𝗄 \mathsf{k} -algebra homomorphism ψ : L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) → A \psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow A , such that the following diagram is commutative: 𝒱 gr ​ ( R ) \textstyle{\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(R)\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} A ⊗ R − \scriptstyle{A\otimes_{R}-} 𝒱 gr ​ ( A ) \textstyle{\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A)} 𝒱 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ) \textstyle{\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces\ignorespaces} ϕ = A ⊗ L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) − \scriptstyle{\;\phi=A\otimes_{L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)}-} Finally, suppose ϕ ⁡ ( v ) ≠ 0 \phi(v)\not=0 , for all v ∈ E 0 v\in E^{0} . Since ϕ ⁡ ( v ) = [ A ⊗ L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ​ v ] = [ A ​ ψ ​ ( v ) ] \phi(v)=[A\otimes_{L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)}L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)v]=[A\psi(v)] , it follows that ψ ⁡ ( v ) ≠ 0 \psi(v)\not=0 , for all v ∈ E 0 v\in E^{0} . Now the graded uniqueness theorem for Leavitt path algebras ( [ 1 , Theorem 2.2.15] ) implies that ψ \psi is injective. ∎ Corollary 9.6 . Let E E and F F be finite graphs and ϕ : K 0 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ) → K 0 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( F ) ) \phi:K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)) a preordered ℤ ⁡ [ x , x − 1 ] \mathbb{Z}[x,x^{-1}] -module homomorphism with ϕ ⁡ ( [ L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ] ) = L 𝗄 ​ ( F ) \phi([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)])=L_{\mathsf{k}}(F) . Then there exists a unital ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded homomorphism ψ : L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) → L 𝗄 ​ ( F ) \psi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(F) such that K 0 gr ​ ( ψ ) = ϕ K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(\psi)=\phi . Furthermore if ϕ \phi is injective, so is ψ \psi . Proof. Since the positive cone of K 0 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ) K^{\operatorname{gr}}_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)) is precisely 𝒱 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ) \mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)) (see [ 3 ] ) and ϕ \phi is preordered, we obtain a ℤ \mathbb{Z} -monoid homomorphism ϕ : 𝒱 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ) → 𝒱 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( F ) ) \phi:\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E))\rightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(F)) . The Corollary now follows from Theorem 9.5 . ∎ Remark 9.7 . Although throughout the paper, by setting the grade group Γ \Gamma to be trivial, we recover the non-graded universal constructions, Corollary 9.6 , however, is not valid for the (non-graded) Grothendieck group K 0 K_{0} . This is because the positive cone of K 0 ​ ( L ​ ( E ) ) K_{0}(L(E)) is not necessarily 𝒱 ⁡ ( L ⁡ ( E ) ) \mathcal{V}(L(E)) . As an example, since K 0 ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( 1 , 2 ) ) = 0 K_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,2))=0 and K 0 ​ ( L ​ ( 2 , 3 ) ) = 0 K_{0}(L(2,3))=0 , we have a preordered pointed monoid homomorphism K 0 ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( 1 , 2 ) ) → K 0 ​ ( L ⁡ ( 2 , 3 ) ) K_{0}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,2))\rightarrow K_{0}(L(2,3)) , however there does not exist any unital ring homomorphsim L 𝗄 ​ ( 1 , 2 ) → L 𝗄 ​ ( 2 , 3 ) . L_{\mathsf{k}}(1,2)\rightarrow L_{\mathsf{k}}(2,3). We finish this section by giving a new proof for the graded uniqueness theorem for Leavitt path algebras (See [ 1 , Theorem 2.2.15] for an element-wise proof). Although this proof can be extended to arbitrary graphs, for simplicity we work with Leavitt path algebras associated to finite graphs, where all our applications are concerned. Theorem 9.8 . Let E E be a finite graph, A A a ℤ \mathbb{Z} -graded ring and ϕ : L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) → A \phi:L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\rightarrow A a graded ring homomorphism. If ϕ ⁡ ( v ) ≠ 0 \phi(v)\not=0 for all v ∈ E 0 v\in E^{0} , then ϕ \phi is injective. Proof. The ring homomorphism ϕ \phi induces a Γ \Gamma -monoid homomorphism ϕ ¯ : 𝒱 gr ​ ( L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ) \displaystyle\overline{\phi}:\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)) ⟶ 𝒱 gr ​ ( A ) , \displaystyle\longrightarrow\mathcal{V}^{\operatorname{gr}}(A), [ L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ​ v ] \displaystyle[L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)v] ⟼ [ A ​ ϕ ​ ( v ) ] \displaystyle\longmapsto[A\phi(v)] Since any graded finitely generated projective L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(E) -module, is generated by some L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ​ v L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)v , v ∈ E 0 v\in E^{0} (see the first part of the proof of Theorem 9.5 ) and since ϕ ⁡ ( v ) ≠ 0 \phi(v)\not=0 for all v ∈ E 0 v\in E^{0} , then ϕ ¯ − 1 ​ ( 0 ) = { 0 } . \overline{\phi}^{-1}(0)=\{0\}. Suppose ϕ ⁡ ( a ) = 0 \phi(a)=0 , for some 0 ≠ a ∈ L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) 0\not=a\in L_{\mathsf{k}}(E) . Without the loss of generality we can assume a a is homogeneous. Consider the left ideal L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ​ a L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)a . Since L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) L_{\mathsf{k}}(E) is graded von Neumann regular, L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ​ a = L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ​ e L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)a=L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)e , for some homogeneous idempotent e e . Thus L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ​ a L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)a is a graded finitely generated projective module and ϕ ¯ ​ ( [ L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ​ a ] ) = [ L 𝗄 ​ ( E ) ​ ϕ ​ ( a ) ] = 0 \overline{\phi}([L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)a])=[L_{\mathsf{k}}(E)\phi(a)]=0 , which is a contradiction. ∎ Acknowledgements We would like to thank Gene Abrams whose germane questions led us to try to understand Bergman’s paper. Our thanks to George Bergman who provided us with invaluable inputs which improved the presentation of the paper. References [1] G. Abrams, P. Ara, M. Siles Molina, Leavitt path algebras. Lecture Notes in Mathematics, vol. 2191, Springer Verlag, 2017. [2] G. Abrams, M. Tomforde, Isomorphism and Morita equivalence of graph algebras , Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. 363 (2011), no. 7, 3733–3767. [3] P. Ara, R. Hazrat, H. Li, A. Sims, Graded Steinberg algebras and their representations , Algebra Number Theory 12 (2018), no. 1, 131–172. [4] P. Ara, K. Goodearl, Leavitt path algebras of separated graphs , J. reine angew. Math. 669 (2012), 165–224. [5] G. Arnone, G. Cortiñas, Graded K-theory and Leavitt path algebras , J. Algebraic Combin. 58 (2023), no. 2, 399–434. [6] G. Arnone, Lifting morphisms between graded Grothendieck groups of Leavitt path algebras , J. Algebra 631 (2023), 804–829. [7] G.M. Bergman, Coproducts and some universal ring constructions , Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. 200 (1974) 33–88. [8] G.M. Bergman, Modules Over Coproducts of Rings , Trans. Amer. Math. Soc. 200 (1974) 1–32. [9] G.M. Bergman, W. Dicks, Universal derivations and universal ring constructions , Pacific J. Math. 79 (1978), no. 2, 293–337. [10] X-W. Chen, D. Yang, Homotopy categories, Leavitt path algebras and Gorenstein projective modules , IMRN, 10 (2015) 2597–2633. [11] P.M. Cohn, Some remarks on the invariant basis property , Topology 5 (1966), 215–228. [12] L. Cordeiro, D. Gonçalves, R. Hazrat, The talented monoid of a directed graph with applications to graph algebras , Revista Matemática Iberoamericana, 38 (2022), no. 1, 223–256. [13] G. Cortiñas, R. Hazrat, Classification conjectures for Leavitt path algebras , arXiv:2401.04262v2. [14] S. Eilers, E. Ruiz, A. Sims, Amplified graph C ∗ C^{*} -algeb